  \ to -1. I need to keep these details in mind as I move forward!I'm reviewing the dropout configuration in the DSConvBlock, where dropout relies on the rng_key set when applying the network. It looks good. I also see that there's no explicit weight initialization, just the default, which is lecun normal. Now, regarding the batch size, I noticed that we set BATCH_SIZE to 64, but train_single.py uses funcs.get('batch_size', 64). It seems like 'funcs' might handle this by mapping the batch size to our constant, which could come from the main system if BATCH_SIZE exists. I think I can trust these settings!I've set the BASE_SEED to 42, which is good! While we're evaluating, it's an option to use the Private Memory Room for jotting down notes, but we also have external token management to consider. I realize I haven't really explored the system/main.py yet; maybe checking out storage/system/main.py would help. From the research document, it seems important to focus on defaults.py and train_single.py—we’ve\
  \ done that already! I’m planning multiple experiments to prepare for the paper. I'll keep it focused with six main approaches, while also considering logistic improvements and some ablations. For now, waiting for the results seems like a good strategy!I see we can set our submission’s ranking to author, but it's already at the top since it’s the newest. Next, we should evaluate the logs to determine any necessary architecture modifications. Pre-saving our code to lineage storage sounds smart; I’ll write 'noema/rna_models/dsconv_attn.py' for future use, which will allow easy imports in later submissions. I need to check system/defaults.py regarding loss functions related to multiclass classification. Meanwhile, I’ll focus on recording my research plan. I'll execute a few actions to gather necessary information, then write it down!I'll start by checking on the 'private_memory' help content, but maybe it's better to just wait for evaluation results for efficiency. We should focus on planning\
