parts:
- text: "The k=11 mean-pooling DSConv is our current best (Score 0.6311). I’ll run two focused, unified ablations: SE(0.25) and dilated DSConv.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"SE(0.25) + k=11 with Mean Pooling (DSConv)\"\ntags: \"cnn, depthwise separable, squeeze-excite, mean pooling, multi-dataset\"\nabstract: \"Adds channel-wise squeeze-and-excitation (se_ratio=0.25) to DSConv with kernel size 11 and mean pooling. Tests whether lightweight channel attention improves across tasks while retaining unified architecture.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/noema')\n\n  from models.dsconv_attn import build_network\n  import optax\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'd_model': 256,\n          'num_blocks': 4,\n          'kernel_size': 11,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'pooling': 'mean',\n          'include_depthwise': True,\n          'se_ratio': 0.25,\
    \          # test SE\n          'positional_encoding': True\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      d_output = hparams.get('d_output', 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get('task_type', 'regression')\n      return build_network(d_output, task_type, hparams)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      ds = trial_data.get('dataset'); vm = trial_data.get('val_metric')\n      print(f\"[Complete] {ds} val_metric={vm}\")\n```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Dilated DSConv (1,2,4,8) with Mean Pooling\"\ntags: \"cnn, depthwise separable, dilated conv, receptive field, multi-dataset\"\nabstract: \"Uses dilated depthwise-separable convolutions with dilations (1,2,4,8) and mean pooling to expand receptive field without\
    \ increasing parameters excessively. Tests gains on CRISPR and long-range signals while remaining unified.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/noema')\n\n  from models.dsconv_mix import build_network_v2\n  import optax\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'd_model': 256,\n          'num_blocks': 4,\n          'kernel_size': 7,        # paired with dilations to expand RF\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'pooling': 'mean',\n          'block_type': 'dilated',\n          'dilations': (1, 2, 4, 8),\n          'se_ratio': 0.0,\n          'positional_encoding': True\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      d_output = hparams.get('d_output', 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get('task_type', 'regression')\n      return build_network_v2(d_output, task_type, hparams)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n     \
    \     optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      ds = trial_data.get('dataset'); vm = trial_data.get('val_metric')\n      print(f\"[Complete] {ds} val_metric={vm}\")\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Tick 14 update — New best: Score 0.6311 (k=11 mean pooling). Gated pooling underperformed mean. Queued two unified ablations:\n  - SE(0.25) + k=11 mean (tests channel attention).\n  - Dilated DSConv (1,2,4,8) + mean (tests larger receptive field).\n  Next: multi-kernel inception and cosine LR+warmup after these finish.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Considering additional experiments**\n\nI'm thinking about proposing some new experiments. One idea is to test SE(0.25) with mean pooling and k=11 DSConv to see if channel attention helps in classification tasks. That seems promising! \n\nThere’s also the dilated DSConv with mean pooling, which could benefit CRI-Off and CRI-On with longer-range interactions. We’ve built dsconv_mix.py to support this, which is exciting. \n\nLastly, I’m considering a cosine learning rate with warmup, using a custom create_optimizer to enhance our previous approach. It could be a nice improvement!**Exploring optimizer options**\n\nI'm looking into how AdamW in Optax can accept a learning rate schedule, which could be either a float or an optax.Schedule. I’m thinking of proposing a create_optimizer function to set this learning rate schedule. \n\nHowever, I notice the training loop only passes the learning_rate parameter, so I need to ensure my function can create the schedule while still\
  \ using this argument. \n\nAdditionally, I’m considering running two experiments: one with SE(0.25) and mean pooling using kernel 11, and another with Dilated DSConv and mean pooling using base kernels of 7 or 11. I'll also need to update our Private Memory with results and plans.**Planning for memory and experiments**\n\nI'm thinking about writing to shared storage for stable code modules, but it doesn't seem necessary at the moment. I also note that ranking by score on the scoreboard is already done. \n\nWe want to keep the architecture unified without dataset-specific changes, focusing on fairness. There’s a plan to communicate with Public Memory or for paper writing, though the Archive Room isn’t available until we mature after 50 ticks. Currently, we're at 14 ticks, so we can't publish yet. Private Memory is fine, but Public Memory is also off-limits for now. \n\nI'll proceed to the Research Counter to submit our two experiments.**Implementing new models**\n\nI’m planning to implement\
  \ \"SE(0.25) + mean pooling + k=11\" using the dsconv_attn build_network with the se_ratio set to 0.25. For the \"Dilated DSConv with mean pooling + k=11,\" I’ll use the dsconv_mix build_network_v2 with block_type set to 'dilated' and dilations possibly at (1,2,4,8). \n\nIt seems effective to use kernel size 7 for RBC while keeping unity. \n\nI’m considering two experiments: \"Dilated DSConv with mean pooling\" and \"Inception DSConv with mean pooling.\" Mean pooling has shown better results than gating, which I’ll skip for now because it produced lower scores.**Preparing experimental submissions**\n\nI want to explore multi-head attention pooling, but since previous attention didn't yield improvements, I’ll focus on two primary submissions instead. The first is \"SE(0.25) with k=11 mean pooling\" using dsconv_attn. The second submission is \"Dilated DSConv mean pooling\" while considering time and complexity. I aim to incorporate a few parameters, like kernel size and dilations. \n\n\
  I will also need to manage the code and data for memory efficiency while planning the YAML content for actions concerning updates and experiments. Finally, I'll prepare actions for submission without exceeding the concurrency limit.**Finalizing submissions and updates**\n\nI’m preparing to submit the first experiment with the dilated DSConv. After that, I’ll head to private_memory to reply with a content update. I’m also considering whether to rank the score, but it doesn't seem necessary right now. \n\nI want to double-check the code to ensure the correct import path for dsconv_mix. The function should return the DSConvMixNetwork. I’ll include the BASE_SEED variable as a constant for consistency, and I'll finalize the content and disclaimers before submitting everything."
