tick: 15
role: model
parts:
- text: "The k=11 mean-pooling DSConv is SOTA (0.6311). I’ll add a unified concat pooling (mean + attention) to recover ncRNA while keeping strong APA/Modif, then queue it while the dilated run finishes.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/models/dsconv_attn.py}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n\n  def sinusoidal_positional_encoding(L: int, d_model: int):\n      pos = jnp.arange(L)[:, None]\n      i = jnp.arange(d_model)[None, :]\n      denom = jnp.power(10000.0, (2 * (i // 2)) / jnp.maximum(1.0, float(d_model)))\n      angles = pos * denom\n      pe = jnp.where((i % 2) == 0, jnp.sin(angles), jnp.cos(angles))\n      return pe\n\n  class SqueezeExcite1D(nn.Module):\n      se_ratio: float = 0.0\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          if self.se_ratio <= 0.0:\n              return x\n          C = x.shape[-1]\n          h = jnp.mean(x, axis=1)\n     \
    \     h = nn.Dense(max(1, int(C * self.se_ratio)))(h)\n          h = nn.relu(h)\n          gate = nn.Dense(C)(h)\n          gate = nn.sigmoid(gate)\n          return x * gate[:, None, :]\n\n  class DSConvBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      kernel_size: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n      se_ratio: float = 0.0\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          residual = x\n          y = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), feature_group_count=self.d_model, padding='SAME')(y)\n          y = nn.gelu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,))(y)\n          y = SqueezeExcite1D(self.se_ratio)(y)\n          y = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(y, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return residual + y\n\n  class AttnPool1D(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.tanh(nn.Dense(self.d_model)(x))\n\
    \          a = nn.Dense(1)(h)\n          w = nn.softmax(a, axis=1)\n          return jnp.sum(w * x, axis=1)\n\n  class GatedMeanAttnPool1D(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          mean_pool = jnp.mean(x, axis=1)\n          attn_pool = AttnPool1D(self.d_model)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          g = nn.LayerNorm()(mean_pool)\n          g = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(g)\n          g = nn.gelu(g)\n          g = nn.Dense(1)(g)\n          g = nn.sigmoid(g)\n          return g * attn_pool + (1.0 - g) * mean_pool\n\n  class ConcatMeanAttnPool1D(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          mean_pool = jnp.mean(x, axis=1)                          # (B, C)\n          attn_pool = AttnPool1D(self.d_model)(x, deterministic=deterministic)  # (B, C)\n          return jnp.concatenate([mean_pool, attn_pool], axis=-1)   # (B, 2C)\n\n  class RNANet(nn.Module):\n \
    \     d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = 'regression'\n      d_model: int = 256\n      num_blocks: int = 4\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      pooling: str = 'attn'   # 'attn' | 'mean' | 'max' | 'gated' | 'concat'\n      include_depthwise: bool = True\n      se_ratio: float = 0.0\n      positional_encoding: bool = True\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(x)\n          if self.positional_encoding:\n              L = h.shape[1]\n              h = h + sinusoidal_positional_encoding(L, self.d_model)[None, :, :]\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          if self.include_depthwise:\n              for _ in range(self.num_blocks):\n                  h = DSConvBlock(self.d_model, self.kernel_size, self.dropout_rate, self.se_ratio)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          if self.pooling\
    \ == 'attn':\n              pooled = AttnPool1D(self.d_model)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          elif self.pooling == 'gated':\n              pooled = GatedMeanAttnPool1D(self.d_model)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          elif self.pooling == 'concat':\n              pooled = ConcatMeanAttnPool1D(self.d_model)(h, deterministic=deterministic)  # (B, 2C)\n          elif self.pooling == 'max':\n              pooled = jnp.max(h, axis=1)\n          else:\n              pooled = jnp.mean(h, axis=1)\n\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(pooled)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z)   # handles both C and 2C pooled dims\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(z)\n          if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  class DSConvAttnNetwork:\n      def __init__(self, d_input, d_output, task_type,\
    \ **kwargs):\n          self.network = RNANet(d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type, **kwargs)\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          if rng_key is None:\n              rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n          return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def build_network(d_output: int, task_type: str, hparams: dict):\n      d_input = 4\n      kwargs = dict(\n          d_model=hparams.get('d_model', 256),\n          num_blocks=hparams.get('num_blocks', 4),\n          kernel_size=hparams.get('kernel_size',\
    \ 7),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.1),\n          pooling=hparams.get('pooling', 'attn'),\n          include_depthwise=hparams.get('include_depthwise', True),\n          se_ratio=hparams.get('se_ratio', 0.0),\n          positional_encoding=hparams.get('positional_encoding', True),\n      )\n      return DSConvAttnNetwork(d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type, **kwargs)\n```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Concat Mean+Attention Pooling (k=11 DSConv)\"\ntags: \"cnn, depthwise separable, concat pooling, unified, multi-dataset\"\nabstract: \"Concatenate mean and attention pooled features before the head to fuse robust global signals with focused motif attention. Unified DSConv with kernel size 11; only output dims differ by task.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/noema')\n\n  from models.dsconv_attn import build_network\n  import optax\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate':\
    \ 0.001,\n          'd_model': 256,\n          'num_blocks': 4,\n          'kernel_size': 11,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'pooling': 'concat',      # new concat pooling\n          'include_depthwise': True,\n          'se_ratio': 0.0,\n          'positional_encoding': True\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      d_output = hparams.get('d_output', 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get('task_type', 'regression')\n      return build_network(d_output, task_type, hparams)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      ds = trial_data.get('dataset'); vm = trial_data.get('val_metric')\n      print(f\"[Complete] {ds} val_metric={vm}\")\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\n\
    content: |\n  Tick 15 update — SE hurt average; mean pooling with k=11 remains best (0.6311). Queued concat mean+attention pooling to recover ncRNA while preserving APA/Modif gains, alongside the pending dilated DSConv run. Next candidates after these: inception multi-kernel blocks and cosine LR + warmup on k=11 mean baseline.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Evaluating access and experiments**


  The Public Memory Room is currently unavailable due to requiring maturity, but we might gain access later. In the meantime, we can plan our paper. Since the Archive Room is also off-limits, we can’t publish yet. We should gather at least five experiments, and we''ve got more than that already. Next steps involve running experiments with a multi-kernel inception block or testing a larger kernel size. We may also consider a combo of mean pooling and multi-head attention gating, but the gating hasn’t performed well.**Analyzing kernel performance**


  I found that the best improvements came from using larger kernels and mean pooling, leading to a significant boost in MRL and CRI Off scores, which increased from 0.0605 to 0.0651. ncRNA also saw an improvement, going from 0.6892 to 0.7554 with k=11 mean pooling. Interestingly, earlier attention pooling gave a score of 0.8662 for ncRNA but had lower metrics overall. I think we should test “dilated DSConv” next since it might enhance interactions. Experimenting with "mha pooling" could help too, but pure mean might still be the best option.**Exploring learning rate schedule**


  I need to test a learning rate schedule for training, which currently uses the default limit of 100 epochs and early stopping. I believe I can implement only the optimizer without the scheduler in create_optimizer. Using optax, I can incorporate a warmup/cosine schedule, which might enhance performance. I’ll look at options like cosine decay with warmup to 1.0 * lr, and check optax.warmup_cosine_decay_schedule for this. Though training steps tied to dataset sizes can''t be directly controlled, estimating steps based on batches and epochs might still work. Adjusting dropout rates could be another approach to consider.**Considering architecture modifications**


  I’m thinking about adding "residual gating across blocks" or "skip connections across pooling," but I want to keep it unified. It’s interesting that ncRNA improved significantly from 0.8662 with attention pooling to 0.7554 using mean pooling with k=11. The initial attention pooling gave the best classification results but performed poorly on average across tasks. My idea is to utilize "two-head outputs" by concatenating both pooled features while maintaining a unified structure. This would allow the network to choose representation via a linear head, potentially improving performance across datasets without being dataset-specific. Concatenation could help the head weigh features differently, which seems like a novel but straightforward approach!**Implementing new pooling strategy**


  I’m considering proposing "Concat pooling" with mean, max, and attention to keep the parameter count low. It’s important to note that classification requires logits for our head outputs. I want to design "ConcatMeanAttnPool1D" to return 2*C features, with the head adapting to this dynamic dimension. We can also explore "multi-kernel inception" plus mean pooling as another option. While evaluating task 17, I''ll prepare code for the "concat pooling variant" and "inception block," but we can only queue two submissions at a time. I’ll update the dsconv_attn.py to add this new pooling class and ensure we maintain the rest of the architecture—the "concat" pooling could help improve classification results!**Preparing submission and code**


  Alright, I’m ready to submit the evaluation with "Mean+Attention Concat Pooling (k=11 DSConv)." After this submission, we''ll have 17 and the new 18 pending. I’ll incorporate the DSConvBlock from an earlier fixed file into dsconv_attn.py, ensuring I don''t reintroduce prior bugs by not calling .apply inside the forward method. We''ll include essential imports like jax and jnp, and set up the submission with the proper title and tags. Remember to avoid colons in the title to comply with YAML rules. Lastly, I’ll note everything in private memory and summarize the new plan!'
