role: model
parts:
- text: "Strong progress. Next, I’ll test an optimizer schedule on the dilated-mean baseline.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Dilated DSConv + Mean Pooling + Cosine LR Warmup\"\ntags: \"cnn, depthwise separable, dilated conv, optimizer schedule, cosine warmup, multi-dataset\"\nabstract: \"Keep the unified dilated DSConv + mean pooling architecture and add a cosine decay LR with warmup in AdamW to improve stability and generalization across datasets.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/noema')\n\n  from models.dsconv_mix import build_network_v2\n  import optax\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'd_model': 256,\n          'num_blocks': 4,\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'pooling': 'mean',\n          'block_type': 'dilated',\n          'dilations': (1, 2, 4, 8),\n          'se_ratio': 0.0,\n          'positional_encoding':\
    \ True\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      d_output = hparams.get('d_output', 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get('task_type', 'regression')\n      return build_network_v2(d_output, task_type, hparams)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      # Cosine decay with warmup; step-based schedule (epochs*steps) handled by optimizer state\n      schedule = optax.warmup_cosine_decay_schedule(\n          init_value=0.0,\n          peak_value=learning_rate,\n          warmup_steps=500,\n          decay_steps=20000,\n          end_value=learning_rate * 0.1\n      )\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=schedule, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      ds = trial_data.get('dataset'); vm = trial_data.get('val_metric')\n      print(f\"[Complete] {ds} val_metric={vm}\")\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n\
    `/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Tick 18 update — Inception (k=5,9,15) scored 0.6180 (below dilated-mean). Queued “Dilated DSConv + Mean + Cosine LR Warmup” to test if scheduling improves the current SOTA (0.6365) and balances ncRNA/MRL. Pending: dilated + concat pooling.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Summarizing progress**\n\nI’m reflecting on what to do next. Here’s where I stand: I’m Noema I, a Recursive Agent in Station. My research task involves Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling using 7 datasets. I’ve implemented the DSConv+Attention model, and it achieved a score of 0.5917. Now, I’m wondering about the next steps in this research process and how I can further improve the results or leverage the findings.**Analyzing ablations**\n\nI’m reviewing the ablation results, and wow, there are some interesting findings! \n\n- Mean pooling without attention improved the score to 0.6190, which is nice! \n- Removing depthwise convolutions didn’t go well, dropping the score to 0.4935, particularly affecting MRL. \n- Gated mean-attention pooling resulted in a score of 0.6018, which was worse than the mean pooling method. \n- The kernel size 11 mean pooling saw an improvement to 0.6311 overall. \n\nThe dilated DSConv mean pooling achieved a new state of the art (SOTA) at\
  \ 0.6365!**Preparing for next steps**\n\nI’ve patched the dsconv_mix file to correct the kernel_dilation parameter, and now there’s a pending submission for the Dilated DSConv + Concat pooling (ID 24). I’m eagerly awaiting the results!\n\nLooking ahead, I plan to use a Cosine LR schedule with warmup for the dilated-mean baseline. I’m considering tweaking the pooling mixing coefficient too. The training code lets us supply an optimizer through the create_optimizer function, but I’ll need to implement a learning rate schedule within optax. We’re also keeping the BATCH_SIZE constant, starting at 64, and might adjust the dropout rate, though it seems fine for now!**Preparing for next experiments**\n\nI’m thinking we should run the Cosine LR with Warmup using the dilated-mean baseline and possibly check the dropout rate at 0.05 or 0.2. Right now, we have a submission concurrency limit of 2, and there are 24 submissions running—22 have finished. We can submit one additional evaluation now.\n\
  \nI also want to create a scheduled optimizer variant and remember that gradient clipping was included earlier in our submissions. Lastly, using 'mha' pooling with multiple heads might enhance ncRNA without significantly affecting MRL, but I should not overlook that the \"concat pooling\" strongly improved ncRNA while lowering MRL.**Implementing the training schedule**\n\nI’m considering the dilated concat method for maintaining high MRL; results are still pending. The CRI-Off score has improved to 0.0663, and I’m contemplating further enhancements with k=11 or various dilations.\n\nWe can’t access the Archive Room yet since our maturity is below 50 ticks. However, I’ll prepare a draft internally. My next move involves submitting a new experiment with “Dilated DSConv + Mean Pooling + Cosine LR Warmup,” using the schedule function from Optax. \n\nI’ll set parameters and organize the schedule while iterating on dropout and keeping track of overall performance without complicating our approach.\
  \ Time to code!**Submitting the new experiment**\n\nI’m planning to create an update for private memory while aiming to compile at least five experiments for a paper. We have over eight experiments with evaluation IDs: 2, 8, 9, 12, 13, 16, 17, 20, 22, and 24 currently running, which is great! \n\nNow, I’m going to submit the new run titled \"Dilated DSConv + Mean Pooling + Cosine LR Warmup\" with relevant tags and an abstract that highlights the goal of improving stability and generalization. I’ll craft a concise YAML for submission and ensure everything is formatted properly. Time to proceed!"
