- text: "The 4-head dilated + concat MHA is new SOTA (0.6622). I’ll test a capacity bump on that variant and log the ablation table.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Dilated DSConv + Concat MHA (d_model=320)\"\ntags: \"cnn, depthwise separable, dilated conv, multi-head attention, capacity\"\nabstract: \"Increase model width to d_model=320 for the dilated DSConv + concat MHA pooling architecture to test whether modest capacity improves cross-dataset performance without changing the unified design.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/noema')\n\n  from models.dsconv_mix import build_network_v2\n  import optax\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'd_model': 320,          # capacity bump\n          'num_blocks': 4,\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'pooling': 'concat_mha',\n          'attn_heads': 4,\n          'block_type':\
    \ 'dilated',\n          'dilations': (1, 2, 4, 8),\n          'se_ratio': 0.0,\n          'positional_encoding': True\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      d_output = hparams.get('d_output', 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get('task_type', 'regression')\n      return build_network_v2(d_output, task_type, hparams)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      ds = trial_data.get('dataset'); vm = trial_data.get('val_metric')\n      print(f\"[Complete] {ds} val_metric={vm}\")\n```\n\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/results/ablations.csv}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  eval_id,title,score,APA_R2,CRI-Off_Spearman,CRI-On_Spearman,MRL_R2,Modif_AUC-ROC,PRS_R2,ncRNA_Accuracy\n  31,Dilated DSConv (1,2,4,8) + Concat MHA Pooling,0.6622,0.8367,0.0858,0.5176,0.8288,0.9514,0.5611,0.8538\n\
    \  34,Dilated DSConv (1,2,4,8) + Concat MHA Pooling (8 heads),0.6611,0.8330,0.0775,0.5072,0.8425,0.9508,0.5644,0.8523\n  24,Dilated DSConv (1,2,4,8) + Concat Mean+Attention Pooling,0.6477,0.8458,0.0803,0.4381,0.8250,0.9519,0.5650,0.8277\n  17,Dilated DSConv (1,2,4,8) with Mean Pooling,0.6365,0.8465,0.0663,0.4880,0.8134,0.9525,0.5735,0.7154\n  13,Kernel Sweep: k=11 with Mean Pooling (DSConv),0.6311,0.8587,0.0651,0.4753,0.7377,0.9521,0.5734,0.7554\n  20,Concat Mean+Attention Pooling (k=11 DSConv),0.6255,0.8365,0.0769,0.4690,0.6479,0.9509,0.5639,0.8338\n  8,Ablation: Mean Pooling (no attention),0.6190,0.8565,0.0605,0.5288,0.6807,0.9520,0.5654,0.6892\n  22,Inception DSConv (k=5,9,15) with Mean Pooling,0.6180,0.8442,0.0675,0.4827,0.7305,0.9513,0.5701,0.6800\n  27,Dilated DSConv + Mean Pooling + Cosine LR Warmup,0.6063,0.8325,0.0775,0.4417,0.6303,0.9509,0.5699,0.7415\n  12,Gated Mean–Attention Pooling (DSConv),0.6018,0.8584,0.0618,0.4625,0.6698,0.9518,0.5726,0.6354\n  2,Depthwise CNN with\
    \ Attention Pooling for RNA,0.5917,0.7550,0.0284,0.5150,0.6883,0.7255,0.5637,0.8662\n  16,SE(0.25) + k=11 with Mean Pooling (DSConv),0.5866,0.8435,0.0585,0.4711,0.6491,0.9523,0.5699,0.5615\n  9,Ablation: No Depthwise Convs (MLP + attention pooling),0.4935,0.7424,0.0508,0.4230,0.0579,0.9416,0.5139,0.7246\n  1,Vanilla MLP Baseline,0.2401,0.3258,0.0336,0.3232,0.0402,0.6377,0.1324,0.1877\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Tick 22 update — Queued capacity bump (d_model=320) for the SOTA dilated + concat MHA model. Logged ablations to storage/noema/results/ablations.csv. Pending: triple pooling run.\n  Plan:\n  - If width helps, try mild depth bump (num_blocks=5) next; otherwise keep 4 blocks for parsimony.\n  - Draft paper sections: methods (unified DSConv+dilations, pooling study), ablations (table logged), insights (pooling vs receptive field vs tasks), and limitations (CRI-Off still modest).\n```"
thinking_content: "**Exploring architectural improvements**\n\nI'm considering exploring the CRI-Off metric, which is currently low at 0.0858, but there's room for improvement. I'm thinking about pairwise interaction modeling, possibly with low-rank pairwise multiplicative mixing, while keeping the architecture simple and unified. Gated channel mixing like SSM or lightweight transformers could be alternatives, but pretrained models aren't allowed. I can suggest a \"gated linear attention\" pooling method instead and look into increasing the d_model from 256 to 384 to enhance performance, all while focusing on parsimony.**Evaluating model adjustments**\n\nI'm noting that we have a concurrency limit of 2, with 37 currently running evaluations. It seems that evaluations 34, 31, and others are complete, so we can submit another under this limit. I'm considering proposing a \"Dilated DSConv + Concat MHA\" approach using either 5 or 6 blocks, but I want to manage training time effectively. I'm\
  \ also thinking about exploring residual connections or learnable combinations instead of just concatenation, since prior gating attempts didn’t yield improvements. Additionally, I want to consider the implications of reverse-complement symmetry for RNA sequences, especially with regard to directional tasks, aiming for strand-aware positional encoding or a directional mask.**Exploring dataset generalization**\n\nI'm thinking about dataset generalization and considering that it might be better not to pursue that route. Instead, I could compute \"center-crop and side-crop pooling\" to focus on the central region for shorter tasks, while ensuring unification across all tasks. Another idea is to integrate \"parametrized positional window weighting\" in pooling, using a parameter vector that generates Gaussian weighting with learnable mu and sigma, normalized for coverage across variable lengths. However, since we already have attention mechanisms in place, I might explore experimenting with\
  \ \"RBF pooling\" as an alternative.**Focusing on CRISPR task stabilization**\n\nGiven the current results, I think we should concentrate on stabilizing CRISPR tasks. Our CRI-On has improved to 0.5176, which is great, while CRI-Off is at 0.0858. I'm considering incorporating \"pairwise interaction for CRI-Off,\" which would concatenate sgRNA and target sequences, though we can't treat CRI-Off as a separate dataset. Implementing a general cross-position bilinear mixing might assist interactions in sequences. I could also explore a module like \"Cross-covariance pooling\" or \"gated local cross-correlation\" across positions, focusing on concatenated sequences that are 43 long. Additionally, introducing a \"depthwise conv + global context vector\" could be beneficial.**Developing pooling strategies**\n\nI’m considering a new general method called \"Patchwise self-attention\" within pooling. We currently use MHA pooling but I realize it’s not true self-attention. I think implementing a “global\
  \ query vector per head” might enhance our attention by capturing cross-position interactions. Our MultiHeadAttnPool1D uses dense mappings, which is similar to learned queries, but I feel we could refine this process. \n\nNext, I want to experiment with \"max gating over conv features\" and \"channel mixing MLP\" and focus on parameters like depth and dropout rates. We have multiple configurations to test, including adjusting model widths and blocks while keeping track of resource limits for submissions. I’ll queue the additional experiments and ensure that we prepare our analysis soon, possibly using Private Memory for drafts and analysis.**Compiling evaluation results**\n\nI think creating a CSV to summarize the evaluation results would be really helpful. I have all the data available, so I'll compile a table with the necessary columns like ID, score, and various metrics. \n\nHere’s what I’ll include: the highest scores and their corresponding results. I'll write this to 'storage/noema/results/ablations.csv'.\
  \ After that, I’ll move to private memory to craft a summary capsule. \n\nThen I'll set up a new experiment with updated configurations for 'd_model', 'pooling', and 'attn_heads'. Finally, I’ll ensure everything follows the proper YAML formatting."
