- text: "The concat MHA (4 heads) remains best (0.6622). I’ll log new results, try a lighter dropout to see if we can squeeze a bit more, and keep drafting.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/results/ablations.csv}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  eval_id,title,score,APA_R2,CRI-Off_Spearman,CRI-On_Spearman,MRL_R2,Modif_AUC-ROC,PRS_R2,ncRNA_Accuracy\n  31,Dilated DSConv (1,2,4,8) + Concat MHA Pooling,0.6622,0.8367,0.0858,0.5176,0.8288,0.9514,0.5611,0.8538\n  34,Dilated DSConv (1,2,4,8) + Concat MHA Pooling (8 heads),0.6611,0.8330,0.0775,0.5072,0.8425,0.9508,0.5644,0.8523\n  43,Dilated DSConv + Concat MHA (5 blocks: 1,2,4,8,16),0.6590,0.8464,0.0823,0.4456,0.8575,0.9515,0.5667,0.8631\n  37,Dilated DSConv (1,2,4,8) + Concat Mean+Max+MHA Pooling,0.6567,0.8451,0.0661,0.4756,0.8247,0.9505,0.5550,0.8800\n  38,Dilated DSConv + Concat MHA (d_model=320),0.6550,0.8396,0.0786,0.4592,0.8256,0.9517,0.5685,0.8615\n  47,Dilated DSConv + Concat Heads MHA Pooling (4 heads),0.6545,0.8398,0.0614,0.4634,0.8373,0.9509,0.5675,0.8615\n\
    \  45,Dilated DSConv + Concat MHA (kernel_size=9),0.6502,0.8407,0.0757,0.4563,0.8170,0.9511,0.5659,0.8446\n  24,Dilated DSConv (1,2,4,8) + Concat Mean+Attention Pooling,0.6477,0.8458,0.0803,0.4381,0.8250,0.9519,0.5650,0.8277\n  17,Dilated DSConv (1,2,4,8) with Mean Pooling,0.6365,0.8465,0.0663,0.4880,0.8134,0.9525,0.5735,0.7154\n  13,Kernel Sweep: k=11 with Mean Pooling (DSConv),0.6311,0.8587,0.0651,0.4753,0.7377,0.9521,0.5734,0.7554\n  20,Concat Mean+Attention Pooling (k=11 DSConv),0.6255,0.8365,0.0769,0.4690,0.6479,0.9509,0.5639,0.8338\n  8,Ablation: Mean Pooling (no attention),0.6190,0.8565,0.0605,0.5288,0.6807,0.9520,0.5654,0.6892\n  22,Inception DSConv (k=5,9,15) with Mean Pooling,0.6180,0.8442,0.0675,0.4827,0.7305,0.9513,0.5701,0.6800\n  27,Dilated DSConv + Mean Pooling + Cosine LR Warmup,0.6063,0.8325,0.0775,0.4417,0.6303,0.9509,0.5699,0.7415\n  12,Gated Mean–Attention Pooling (DSConv),0.6018,0.8584,0.0618,0.4625,0.6698,0.9518,0.5726,0.6354\n  2,Depthwise CNN with Attention Pooling\
    \ for RNA,0.5917,0.7550,0.0284,0.5150,0.6883,0.7255,0.5637,0.8662\n  16,SE(0.25) + k=11 with Mean Pooling (DSConv),0.5866,0.8435,0.0585,0.4711,0.6491,0.9523,0.5699,0.5615\n  9,Ablation: No Depthwise Convs (MLP + attention pooling),0.4935,0.7424,0.0508,0.4230,0.0579,0.9416,0.5139,0.7246\n  1,Vanilla MLP Baseline,0.2401,0.3258,0.0336,0.3232,0.0402,0.6377,0.1324,0.1877\n```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Dilated DSConv + Concat MHA (dropout=0.05)\"\ntags: \"cnn, depthwise separable, dilated conv, multi-head attention, regularization\"\nabstract: \"SOTA architecture with slightly reduced dropout (0.05) to test if lighter regularization improves APA/MRL while preserving CRISPR and ncRNA performance.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/noema')\n\n  from models.dsconv_mix import build_network_v2\n  import optax\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'd_model': 256,\n          'num_blocks': 4,\n\
    \          'kernel_size': 7,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.05,      # reduced dropout\n          'pooling': 'concat_mha',\n          'attn_heads': 4,\n          'block_type': 'dilated',\n          'dilations': (1, 2, 4, 8),\n          'se_ratio': 0.0,\n          'positional_encoding': True\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      d_output = hparams.get('d_output', 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get('task_type', 'regression')\n      return build_network_v2(d_output, task_type, hparams)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      ds = trial_data.get('dataset'); vm = trial_data.get('val_metric')\n      print(f\"[Complete] {ds} val_metric={vm}\")\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\n\
    content: |\n  Tick 27 update — Concat-heads MHA underperformed (0.6545). SOTA still 0.6622 (dilated + concat MHA, 4 heads). Logged results; queued reduced-dropout (0.05) on SOTA to test regularization. CLS pooling is running. If no further gains, I’ll finalize the paper draft (method, ablations, insights) and snapshot best submission for reproducibility.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Planning experiments and analysis**\n\nThe \"Paper Guidance\" suggests publishing at least five experiments, and we have a lot of results to work with. I should solidify the results, save the best code snapshot, and create an analysis file in our lineage storage. Next, we might consider modest changes like adjacency mixing or mixture of experts (MoE). We need to ensure our architecture remains consistent across datasets, while exploring things like gated residual mixing or lightweight kernels. However, the training loop operates on CPU, which may complicate things.**Exploring token mixing methods**\n\nI’m considering using \"global token mixing with Fourier features\" like S4 or FNet, but they might be a bit heavy. Another idea is \"lightweight cross-correlation pooling\" or perhaps \"co-attention between two halves.\" However, these might be too specific for the CRISPR Off dataset, which concatenates sgRNA+target. Our methods should remain largely consistent across\
  \ datasets. I think adding a symmetric bi-directional local cross-correlation could help, alongside implementing general pairwise interactions via low-rank pooling. Our architecture needs to accommodate this without dataset restrictions.**Considering convolution strategies**\n\nI have this interesting idea: adding a \"pairwise interaction term via Toeplitz convolution\" could help capture complementarity, or I might try \"self-convolution\" on the length dimension using depthwise convolutions with a reversed sequence. A simpler approach could be introducing \"Conv + Dilated + Bi-Conv,\" even incorporating reverse complement mapping for RNA. The loader can map T and U to a single channel for generic complement mapping. While reverse complement might offer invariance, I must be cautious since directionality matters in RNA tasks. I could also explore \"learned positional gating\" for reweighting positions efficiently, but we already have pooling gating.**Exploring training adjustments**\n\
  \nI have an idea regarding training-specific changes: they're limited to creating an optimizer, which we've already done. I wonder if we could adjust the batch size instead. Currently, it's set to 64, but maybe I could test 128? Since the default uses a variable for batch size, I'm considering setting it to 128—but the effects are uncertain. I might want to read CLS test results, but it's still running. The Reflection Chamber is available for further planning, so I could check the room and use /execute_action commands to reflect, though I'm unsure if it’s suitable.**Planning next experiments**\n\nI have a couple of ideas for our next experiments: First, I could implement \"residual connections with skip stacking\" and \"stochastic depth\" for a Depth=4 configuration to improve generalization. Secondly, I might introduce a \"channel mixing MLP\" like gMLP after pooling. However, it might be better to avoid chasing small incremental improvements right now and focus on preparing for our paper.\
  \ \n\nI could store updated results in private memory and also work on our ablations CSV to include all recent results. We can queue our next test for dropout rates and potentially refine parameters based on what we learned earlier.**Organizing next steps**\n\nI need to ensure that the content includes a code import for `build_network_v2` and set `_define_hyperparameters` accordingly. After that, I think it might be good to visit the Reflection Chamber, but it’s not essential. \n\nMy planned actions include going to research, merging and writing updated ablations to CSV, and submitting a new test with \"Dilated DSConv + Concat MHA (dropout 0.05).\" Even though there’s one evaluation running, we can still manage two concurrent evaluations. I'll also double-check the YAML formatting before compiling all our important entries into the CSV. Let’s move forward!"
