parts:
- text: "The concat MHA (4 heads) remains SOTA (0.6622). I’ll log the CLS result and try a dual-dilation interaction block to improve CRISPR while keeping MRL strong.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/results/ablations.csv}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  eval_id,title,score,APA_R2,CRI-Off_Spearman,CRI-On_Spearman,MRL_R2,Modif_AUC-ROC,PRS_R2,ncRNA_Accuracy\n  31,Dilated DSConv (1,2,4,8) + Concat MHA Pooling,0.6622,0.8367,0.0858,0.5176,0.8288,0.9514,0.5611,0.8538\n  34,Dilated DSConv (1,2,4,8) + Concat MHA Pooling (8 heads),0.6611,0.8330,0.0775,0.5072,0.8425,0.9508,0.5644,0.8523\n  43,Dilated DSConv + Concat MHA (5 blocks: 1,2,4,8,16),0.6590,0.8464,0.0823,0.4456,0.8575,0.9515,0.5667,0.8631\n  37,Dilated DSConv (1,2,4,8) + Concat Mean+Max+MHA Pooling,0.6567,0.8451,0.0661,0.4756,0.8247,0.9505,0.5550,0.8800\n  38,Dilated DSConv + Concat MHA (d_model=320),0.6550,0.8396,0.0786,0.4592,0.8256,0.9517,0.5685,0.8615\n  47,Dilated DSConv + Concat Heads MHA Pooling (4\
    \ heads),0.6545,0.8398,0.0614,0.4634,0.8373,0.9509,0.5675,0.8615\n  48,Dilated DSConv + CLS Attention Pooling (concat mean + cls),0.6535,0.8278,0.0637,0.5200,0.8236,0.9497,0.5696,0.8200\n  45,Dilated DSConv + Concat MHA (kernel_size=9),0.6502,0.8407,0.0757,0.4563,0.8170,0.9511,0.5659,0.8446\n  24,Dilated DSConv (1,2,4,8) + Concat Mean+Attention Pooling,0.6477,0.8458,0.0803,0.4381,0.8250,0.9519,0.5650,0.8277\n  17,Dilated DSConv (1,2,4,8) with Mean Pooling,0.6365,0.8465,0.0663,0.4880,0.8134,0.9525,0.5735,0.7154\n  13,Kernel Sweep: k=11 with Mean Pooling (DSConv),0.6311,0.8587,0.0651,0.4753,0.7377,0.9521,0.5734,0.7554\n  20,Concat Mean+Attention Pooling (k=11 DSConv),0.6255,0.8365,0.0769,0.4690,0.6479,0.9509,0.5639,0.8338\n  8,Ablation: Mean Pooling (no attention),0.6190,0.8565,0.0605,0.5288,0.6807,0.9520,0.5654,0.6892\n  22,Inception DSConv (k=5,9,15) with Mean Pooling,0.6180,0.8442,0.0675,0.4827,0.7305,0.9513,0.5701,0.6800\n  27,Dilated DSConv + Mean Pooling + Cosine LR Warmup,0.6063,0.8325,0.0775,0.4417,0.6303,0.9509,0.5699,0.7415\n\
    \  12,Gated Mean–Attention Pooling (DSConv),0.6018,0.8584,0.0618,0.4625,0.6698,0.9518,0.5726,0.6354\n  2,Depthwise CNN with Attention Pooling for RNA,0.5917,0.7550,0.0284,0.5150,0.6883,0.7255,0.5637,0.8662\n  16,SE(0.25) + k=11 with Mean Pooling (DSConv),0.5866,0.8435,0.0585,0.4711,0.6491,0.9523,0.5699,0.5615\n  9,Ablation: No Depthwise Convs (MLP + attention pooling),0.4935,0.7424,0.0508,0.4230,0.0579,0.9416,0.5139,0.7246\n  1,Vanilla MLP Baseline,0.2401,0.3258,0.0336,0.3232,0.0402,0.6377,0.1324,0.1877\n```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Dilated DSConv + Dual-Dilation Interaction + Concat MHA\"\ntags: \"cnn, depthwise separable, dilated conv, interaction gating, attention pooling\"\nabstract: \"Augment each dilated DSConv block with a second depthwise branch at 2x dilation and an interaction term (elementwise product) before pointwise GLU, to capture cross-scale motif interactions. Keep concat mean+MHA pooling. Unified across datasets.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n\
    \  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n\n  class SqueezeExcite1D(nn.Module):\n      se_ratio: float = 0.0\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          if self.se_ratio <= 0.0:\n              return x\n          C = x.shape[-1]\n          h = jnp.mean(x, axis=1)\n          h = nn.Dense(max(1, int(C * self.se_ratio)))(h)\n          h = nn.relu(h)\n          gate = nn.Dense(C)(h)\n          gate = nn.sigmoid(gate)\n          return x * gate[:, None, :]\n\n  class DSConvInteractBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      kernel_size: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n      dilation: int = 1\n      se_ratio: float = 0.0\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          residual = x\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          # Depthwise conv at dilation d\n          y1 = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n                       feature_group_count=self.d_model,\
    \ padding='SAME',\n                       kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,))(h)\n          # Depthwise conv at dilation 2d (cap at >=1)\n          d2 = max(1, self.dilation * 2)\n          y2 = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n                       feature_group_count=self.d_model, padding='SAME',\n                       kernel_dilation=(d2,))(h)\n          y1 = nn.gelu(y1)\n          y2 = nn.gelu(y2)\n          # Cross-scale interaction\n          y_int = jnp.tanh(y1) * jnp.tanh(y2)\n          y = (y1 + y2 + y_int) / 3.0\n          # Pointwise GLU\n          z = nn.Dense(2 * self.d_model)(y)\n          a, g = jnp.split(z, 2, axis=-1)\n          y = a * nn.sigmoid(g)\n          y = SqueezeExcite1D(self.se_ratio)(y)\n          y = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(y, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return residual + y\n\n  class MultiHeadAttnPool1D(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      num_heads: int = 4\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self,\
    \ x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          a = nn.tanh(nn.Dense(self.d_model)(h))\n          a = nn.Dense(self.num_heads)(a)\n          w = nn.softmax(a, axis=1)\n          pooled_heads = jnp.einsum('blh,blc->bhc', w, h)\n          return pooled_heads.mean(axis=1)\n\n  class RNANetDilatedInteract(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = 'regression'\n      d_model: int = 256\n      num_blocks: int = 4\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      attn_heads: int = 4\n      dilations: tuple = (1, 2, 4, 8)\n      se_ratio: float = 0.0\n      positional_encoding: bool = True\n\n      def sinusoidal_positional_encoding(self, L, d_model):\n          pos = jnp.arange(L)[:, None]\n          i = jnp.arange(d_model)[None, :]\n          denom = jnp.power(10000.0, (2 * (i // 2)) / jnp.maximum(1.0, float(d_model)))\n          angles = pos * denom\n          return jnp.where((i % 2) == 0, jnp.sin(angles),\
    \ jnp.cos(angles))\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(x)\n          if self.positional_encoding:\n              L = h.shape[1]\n              h = h + self.sinusoidal_positional_encoding(L, self.d_model)[None, :, :]\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              h = DSConvInteractBlock(\n                  d_model=self.d_model,\n                  kernel_size=self.kernel_size,\n                  dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate,\n                  dilation=self.dilations[i % len(self.dilations)],\n                  se_ratio=self.se_ratio\n              )(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          mean_pool = jnp.mean(h, axis=1)\n          attn_pool = MultiHeadAttnPool1D(self.d_model, num_heads=self.attn_heads)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          pooled = jnp.concatenate([mean_pool, attn_pool],\
    \ axis=-1)\n\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(pooled)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(z)\n\n          if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  class NetWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, **kwargs):\n          self.net = RNANetDilatedInteract(**kwargs)\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, drop_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          vars = self.net.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': drop_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return vars['params']\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.net.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          if rng_key is None:\n              rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n  \
    \        return self.net.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'd_model': 256,\n          'num_blocks': 4,\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'attn_heads': 4,\n          'dilations': (1, 2, 4, 8),\n          'se_ratio': 0.0,\n          'positional_encoding': True\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      d_output = hparams.get('d_output', 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get('task_type', 'regression')\n      return NetWrapper(\n          d_input=4,\n          d_output=d_output,\n          task_type=task_type,\n          d_model=hparams.get('d_model', 256),\n          num_blocks=hparams.get('num_blocks', 4),\n          kernel_size=hparams.get('kernel_size', 7),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.1),\n          attn_heads=hparams.get('attn_heads', 4),\n          dilations=tuple(hparams.get('dilations',\
    \ (1, 2, 4, 8))),\n          se_ratio=hparams.get('se_ratio', 0.0),\n          positional_encoding=hparams.get('positional_encoding', True)\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      ds = trial_data.get('dataset'); vm = trial_data.get('val_metric')\n      print(f\"[Complete] {ds} val_metric={vm}\")\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Tick 28 update — CLS pooling improved CRI-On (0.5200) but hurt APA/MRL, avg 0.6535. SOTA still 0.6622 (dilated + concat MHA). Logged results. Queued dual-dilation interaction blocks to capture cross-scale motif interactions for CRISPR while preserving strong MRL.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Finalizing and drafting tasks**\n\nI need to finalize the draft paper and prepare for an archive submission, but it looks like the Archive Room is unavailable until the project matures. Previously, I thought that both the Public Memory Room and Archive Room would be inaccessible until then, which seems to be correct since we need to wait for 50 ticks for maturity unlock. \n\nIn the meantime, I can keep drafting, and perhaps update the ablations file with the CLS 48 results. When 52 returns, I should log that as well.**Exploring MHA pooling ideas**\n\nI have a new idea for using MHA pooling with scaled dot-product attention on learned queries, similar to multi-query pooling where K heads produce separate aggregated features that are concatenated. We tried per-head concatenation before, but it didn't improve results. \n\nAnother thought is to use gating to weigh the contributions of mean and MHA adaptively per dataset without relying on dataset metadata, which could help\
  \ while keeping the system unified. I could also explore a global k-mer dictionary with 1D convolutions and potentially add two branches with different dilations. At the same time, I’m considering residual pre-norm with a lower dropout of 0.05, as those results are still pending.**Considering dropout and pooling strategies**\n\nI’m thinking about using layer dropout or stochastic depth with a small drop path for better generalization, although it might not be strictly necessary. Another option is downsampling pooling with strided depthwise convolution to reduce the length dimension early on, which could help with long sequences like ncRNA. \n\nWe already have MHA pooling, but maybe a hierarchical approach would be beneficial despite compute constraints, as training is limited to 30 minutes. Efficient Channel Attention (ECA) could introduce minimal overhead compared to Squeeze-and-Excitation (SE), which didn’t show improvement. \n\nAdditionally, I’m exploring positional convolution with\
  \ gating and considering how pairwise interactions are crucial for our sequences. I wonder if we should compute convolution over concatenated pairs instead of just depthwise convolutions, keeping the architecture unified while potentially incorporating outer product pooling across positions.**Integrating attention mechanisms**\n\nI’m considering integrating simple positionwise bilinear gating between channels using depthwise convolution, which allows for a channel-per-position approach. Alternatively, symmetric pairwise attention could be applied across positions, but that would need to be careful since dataset-specific operations aren't allowed. \n\nSince our inputs are concatenated, the network should generally manage any sequence type. I think adding a local self-attention block with limited windowed attention might help with CRI tasks and MRL while keeping compute manageable. I could implement this local self-attention block before pooling to unify across tasks effectively.**Finalizing\
  \ architecture and actions**\n\nI’ve realized that adding a local self-attention block before pooling could capture more interactions. I’m considering an architecture with depthwise convolution, local self-attention, and MHA pooling, which might enhance CRI/APA tasks. \n\nFor now, I need to log the CLS result, submit the \"Dilated DSConv + Local Self-Attention\" variant, and potentially store my windowed attention code. I’ll also create the required modules and ensure we’re using the allowed functions. Additionally, I’ll record a new line in ablations.csv and keep everything concise while following through with my action items!**Implementing DSConvInteractBlock**\n\nI’m ready to implement the DSConvInteractBlock. First, I’ll create a depthwise convolution with dilation d1 and another convolution with dilation 2*d1. I’ll calculate y1 and y2 and then combine them using either jnp.tanh or after a gelu activation. Afterward, I’ll sum y1 and y2 and include a pointwise convolution to 2*d_model\
  \ for GLU, following the existing pattern with Dense. \n\nAdditionally, I’ll implement optional SqueezeExcite and utilize dropout. Now, I’ll craft a concise Private Memory reply while ensuring I outline these actions clearly!"
