parts:
- text: "SOTA still 0.6622. I’ll log the temp=0.5 result, then test removing positional encoding (conv models may not benefit from absolute sinusoidal bias), and keep notes tidy while RMSNorm runs.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/results/ablations.csv}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  eval_id,title,score,APA_R2,CRI-Off_Spearman,CRI-On_Spearman,MRL_R2,Modif_AUC-ROC,PRS_R2,ncRNA_Accuracy\n  31,Dilated DSConv (1,2,4,8) + Concat MHA Pooling,0.6622,0.8367,0.0858,0.5176,0.8288,0.9514,0.5611,0.8538\n  34,Dilated DSConv (1,2,4,8) + Concat MHA Pooling (8 heads),0.6611,0.8330,0.0775,0.5072,0.8425,0.9508,0.5644,0.8523\n  43,Dilated DSConv + Concat MHA (5 blocks: 1,2,4,8,16),0.6590,0.8464,0.0823,0.4456,0.8575,0.9515,0.5667,0.8631\n  37,Dilated DSConv (1,2,4,8) + Concat Mean+Max+MHA Pooling,0.6567,0.8451,0.0661,0.4756,0.8247,0.9505,0.5550,0.8800\n  38,Dilated DSConv + Concat MHA (d_model=320),0.6550,0.8396,0.0786,0.4592,0.8256,0.9517,0.5685,0.8615\n  47,Dilated DSConv\
    \ + Concat Heads MHA Pooling (4 heads),0.6545,0.8398,0.0614,0.4634,0.8373,0.9509,0.5675,0.8615\n  58,Dilated DSConv + Concat MHA (attn_temp=0.7),0.6542,0.8414,0.0771,0.4615,0.8188,0.9510,0.5710,0.8585\n  60,Dilated DSConv + Concat MHA (attn_temp=0.5),0.6566,0.8393,0.0741,0.4714,0.8572,0.9515,0.5704,0.8323\n  48,Dilated DSConv + CLS Attention Pooling (concat mean + cls),0.6535,0.8278,0.0637,0.5200,0.8236,0.9497,0.5696,0.8200\n  54,Dilated DSConv + Dual-Dilation Interaction + Concat MHA,0.6487,0.8081,0.0751,0.4710,0.8291,0.9512,0.5681,0.8385\n  45,Dilated DSConv + Concat MHA (kernel_size=9),0.6502,0.8407,0.0757,0.4563,0.8170,0.9511,0.5659,0.8446\n  52,Dilated DSConv + Concat MHA (dropout=0.05),0.6370,0.8317,0.0685,0.4429,0.7699,0.9512,0.5611,0.8338\n  24,Dilated DSConv (1,2,4,8) + Concat Mean+Attention Pooling,0.6477,0.8458,0.0803,0.4381,0.8250,0.9519,0.5650,0.8277\n  17,Dilated DSConv (1,2,4,8) with Mean Pooling,0.6365,0.8465,0.0663,0.4880,0.8134,0.9525,0.5735,0.7154\n  13,Kernel Sweep:\
    \ k=11 with Mean Pooling (DSConv),0.6311,0.8587,0.0651,0.4753,0.7377,0.9521,0.5734,0.7554\n  20,Concat Mean+Attention Pooling (k=11 DSConv),0.6255,0.8365,0.0769,0.4690,0.6479,0.9509,0.5639,0.8338\n  8,Ablation: Mean Pooling (no attention),0.6190,0.8565,0.0605,0.5288,0.6807,0.9520,0.5654,0.6892\n  22,Inception DSConv (k=5,9,15) with Mean Pooling,0.6180,0.8442,0.0675,0.4827,0.7305,0.9513,0.5701,0.6800\n  27,Dilated DSConv + Mean Pooling + Cosine LR Warmup,0.6063,0.8325,0.0775,0.4417,0.6303,0.9509,0.5699,0.7415\n  12,Gated Mean–Attention Pooling (DSConv),0.6018,0.8584,0.0618,0.4625,0.6698,0.9518,0.5726,0.6354\n  2,Depthwise CNN with Attention Pooling for RNA,0.5917,0.7550,0.0284,0.5150,0.6883,0.7255,0.5637,0.8662\n  16,SE(0.25) + k=11 with Mean Pooling (DSConv),0.5866,0.8435,0.0585,0.4711,0.6491,0.9523,0.5699,0.5615\n  9,Ablation: No Depthwise Convs (MLP + attention pooling),0.4935,0.7424,0.0508,0.4230,0.0579,0.9416,0.5139,0.7246\n  1,Vanilla MLP Baseline,0.2401,0.3258,0.0336,0.3232,0.0402,0.6377,0.1324,0.1877\n\
    ```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Dilated DSConv + Concat MHA (no positional encoding)\"\ntags: \"cnn, depthwise separable, dilated conv, pooling, positional encoding\"\nabstract: \"SOTA architecture without sinusoidal positional encoding to test whether absolute position bias is unnecessary for convolutional backbones across RNA tasks.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/noema')\n\n  from models.dsconv_mix import build_network_v2\n  import optax\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'd_model': 256,\n          'num_blocks': 4,\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'pooling': 'concat_mha',\n          'attn_heads': 4,\n          'block_type': 'dilated',\n          'dilations': (1, 2, 4, 8),\n          'se_ratio': 0.0,\n          'positional_encoding': False   # ablation: remove PE\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      d_output = hparams.get('d_output',\
    \ 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get('task_type', 'regression')\n      return build_network_v2(d_output, task_type, hparams)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      ds = trial_data.get('dataset'); vm = trial_data.get('val_metric')\n      print(f\"[Complete] {ds} val_metric={vm}\")\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Tick 32 update — temp=0.5 improved to 0.6566 but still below 0.6622 SOTA. Logged results. Submitted “no positional encoding” ablation (conv backbones may not need absolute PE). RMSNorm run is still pending. If no further gains, I’ll finalize the paper with Eval 31 as the recommended unified baseline and submit when Archive opens.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating next steps in submissions**\n\nArchive isn't available for my current approach, but that's no reason to stop writing. I might snapshot and package the state-of-the-art code instead. I can propose creating a reproducibility script to reference the hyperparameters in the submissions folder and produce visual highlights for each dataset.\n\nFor my next steps, I need to save the updated ablation results with attn_temp=0.5 to append to the ablations.csv. Also, I should check if RMSNorm (Eval 65) has finished since the last output shows it is still running.**Exploring review and potential changes**\n\nI can attempt to review 65, but since it's still running, the review would just return as pending.\n\nOne idea is to consider a small change: switching from \"pre-norm vs post-norm\" in DSConv. They're currently using LayerNorm before the convolution. I could propose not using pre-norm and instead applying normalization after the residual, which adds some complexity\
  \ to the design.\n\nAnother thought involves gating inference with learned gating between mean and MHA, but previous attempts showed lower performance, so I might skip that.\n\nI could also explore using T5-style relative positional bias. However, since the architecture is convolution-based with pooling, and sinusoidal positional encoding is already included, dropping positional encoding could enhance performance. It’d be interesting to test pos_encoding=False as an ablation to see if it improves pattern recognition, even though past SOTA used it as True.\n\nLastly, I might try combining max pooling with MHA and concatenating that with the mean.**Considering variations and improvements**\n\nOne variation I’m thinking about is using a \"weighted sum of the mean and MHA\" with gating based on pooled stats across heads. They've tried gating before, and it decreased performance.\n\nI also wonder about adjusting the batch size or learning rate schedule. It seems like testing a learning rate\
  \ of 0.0005 could help with generalization, although we can't change the training loop. The current setup has a robust constant learning rate of 1e-3.\n\nAnother possible improvement could be switching from GELU to SiLU activation, but it's a minor change.\n\nI could explore using \"residual dropout or stochastic depth,\" but that's not straightforward.\n\nAlso, I’m reflecting on adjustments to the feature dimensions. Reducing d_model from 320 to 288, 224, or even 192 might slightly improve generalization, although that's speculative.\n\nWith limited capacity, it’s probably best to finalize the paper and be cautious about resource tokens to avoid high token consumption. We can still afford to do one or two actions.**Planning next steps**\n\nIt seems the user is looking for next actions since they didn’t ask a specific question. Historically, my agent has relied on multiple actions, so I think the steps should include:\n\nFirst, I should update the ablations.csv with the new result for\
  \ row 60. I might also want to refine the paper draft with conclusions, indicating it’s \"final.\"\n\nThe recommended baseline reproduction code is already saved, which is good. \n\nI might consider stopping exploration for now and finalizing things. \n\nSubmitting the minor \"positional_encoding=False\" ablation could help test the translational invariance idea since the architecture uses convolutions. The current setting is pos_enc True, but testing it as False might yield improvements. \n\nHowever, I need to keep in mind the concurrency limits; I see that only 65 is running now, and 60 has finished.**Planning experiment submission and updates**\n\nI can submit one new experiment since I'm allowed to have up to two pending runs. With 65 already running, I propose submitting \"Dilated DSConv + Concat MHA (no positional encoding)\" and potentially adding a \"rank score.\" \n\nNext, I need to update my private notes and include row 60 in the ablations.csv. I should overwrite it with the\
  \ updated dataset, which might be a bit heavy, but that’s fine. \n\nI'll proceed with the following actions: \n\n1. Write to ablations.csv, appending row 60 with score 0.6566 and relevant metrics.\n2. Submit with 'positional_encoding=False' as a test. \n\nLater, I can check if RMSNorm for 65 finished, but that might consume tokens, which is okay. \n\nTo keep things concise, I’ll craft minimal commentary and use the YAML format for the boolean setting. Finally, I want to maintain the proper formatting for each action in the submission."
