  \ major step is publishing our paper. I need to create a capsule in the Archive Room, including at least five experiments and implementation details like the DSConv architecture, dilations schedule, kernel size, and optimizer settings.**Outlining paper structure**\n\nNow, I’m thinking about what actions to take next: I need to review item 74, possibly write the results CSV, head to the archive, and create the capsule with the necessary content. Even though the user hasn't specifically requested anything, I should move forward with these actions. \n\nI’ll plan the content structure for the paper with a title focusing on \"Unified Dilated Depthwise CNNs Without Positional Encodings for Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling,\" including relevant tags and crafting an abstract of around 100-150 words. The capsule protocol requires an abstract, and I don't think there's a strict length limit.**Structuring the paper content**\n\nI need to keep the paper concise while outlining the main sections. Here’s\
  \ my plan:\n\n- Introduction: I’ll unify across seven tasks, highlighting contributions like DSConv, MHA with mean pooling, and the no-PE insight, mentioning the new state-of-the-art (SOTA) score of 0.6801. \n\n- Methods: I want to describe the architecture, including the input, depthwise-separable conv blocks, dilation schedule, kernel size, and dropout settings. I’ll note the improvement in performance from removing positional encoding.\n\n- For Training: I’ll specify parameters like batch size, optimizer settings, early stopping, and epoch limits.\n\n- I’ll create a table for experiments, list evaluations, and show key results related to ablations and analysis on CRI-Off.**Organizing and implementing code**\n\nI need to ensure we focus on reproducibility, starting with providing the necessary code paths and code for the SOTA result submission. I plan to write the results CSV file now. \n\nMy actions will begin with reviewing evaluation 74, parsing the code, and storing relevant content.\
  \ I can't parse programmatically, so I’ll reference the earlier code and create a new file that replicates the architecture, but I want to keep everything aligned with the original submission. \n\nI'll define the network as RNANetSOTA, emphasizing key parameters like depthwise conv blocks and MultiHead Attention pooling. I want to ensure the code abides by our allowed functions and includes essential information for reproducibility. Lastly, I’ll work on the archive capsule and its content, including summaries, architecture details, experiments, and future work.**Implementing training code and network structure**\n\nI need to check that the training code properly expects the `create_network` function to return an object with `init` and `apply` methods, while also confirming the shape of `dummy_input`. Our network will be designed to handle variable sequence lengths, and it's crucial to ensure we're implementing `MultiHeadPool` effectively.\n\nI’ll detail the implementation of the `MultiHeadAttnPool1D`\
  \ class, focusing on using `DenseGeneral` efficiently. I also want to set up `DSConvDilatedBlock` with the appropriate dilation parameters while adhering to our SOTA structure. \n\nI'll make sure to construct the necessary components, define the network, populate the `ablations.csv`, and ensure everything maintains the correct formatting conventions in the archive capsule YAML. This will involve careful consideration of quoting rules in YAML, especially for titles containing colons. Finally, I’ll finalize the actions and generate the necessary content."
