- text: "Strong results. I’ll log Eval 95, queue the r=8 no-MHA ablation, and prepare to publish at tick 50.\n\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/results/ablations.csv}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  eval_id,title,score,APA_R2,CRI-Off_Spearman,CRI-On_Spearman,MRL_R2,Modif_AUC-ROC,PRS_R2,ncRNA_Accuracy\n  2,Depthwise CNN with Attention Pooling for RNA,0.5917,0.7550,0.0284,0.5150,0.6883,0.7255,0.5637,0.8662\n  8,Ablation: Mean Pooling (no attention),0.6190,0.8565,0.0605,0.5288,0.6807,0.9520,0.5654,0.6892\n  9,Ablation: No Depthwise Convs (MLP + attention),0.4935,0.7424,0.0508,0.4230,0.0579,0.9416,0.5139,0.7246\n  13,Kernel Sweep k=11 (Mean Pooling),0.6311,0.8587,0.0651,0.4753,0.7377,0.9521,0.5734,0.7554\n  17,Dilated DSConv (1,2,4,8) + Mean Pooling,0.6365,0.8465,0.0663,0.4880,0.8134,0.9525,0.5735,0.7154\n  20,Concat Mean+Attention (k=11 DSConv),0.6255,0.8365,0.0769,0.4690,0.6479,0.9509,0.5639,0.8338\n  31,Dilated DSConv + Concat MHA,0.6622,0.8367,0.0858,0.5176,0.8288,0.9514,0.5611,0.8538\n  34,Dilated\
    \ DSConv + Concat MHA (8 heads),0.6611,0.8330,0.0775,0.5072,0.8425,0.9508,0.5644,0.8523\n  66,No-PE Dilated DSConv + Concat MHA,0.6686,0.8615,0.0357,0.4620,0.8918,0.9485,0.5655,0.9154\n  69,No-PE + Concat MHA (5 blocks 1,2,4,8,16),0.6745,0.8610,0.0674,0.4841,0.8986,0.9510,0.5627,0.8969\n  74,No-PE + Concat MHA (5 blocks) tau=0.5,0.6801,0.8598,0.0788,0.4883,0.8928,0.9504,0.5627,0.9277\n  78,No-PE + RMSNorm + Concat MHA (5 blocks),0.6728,0.8579,0.0709,0.4805,0.8936,0.9511,0.5632,0.8923\n  83,No-PE + RMSNorm + Concat MHA (5 blocks) tau=0.5,0.6670,0.8562,0.0381,0.4666,0.8925,0.9511,0.5616,0.9031\n  84,No-PE + Concat MHA (5 blocks, k=5) tau=0.5,0.6682,0.8568,0.0790,0.4464,0.8955,0.9519,0.5632,0.8846\n  90,No-PE + Concat MHA + Low-Rank Bilinear Pooling (r=16),0.6727,0.8616,0.0614,0.4408,0.8931,0.9518,0.5649,0.9354\n  91,Ablation: No-PE + Mean + Low-Rank Bilinear Pooling (no MHA),0.6775,0.8600,0.0678,0.4805,0.8919,0.9524,0.5636,0.9262\n  95,No-PE + Concat MHA + Offset Correlation Pooling,0.6627,0.8535,0.0320,0.4589,0.8780,0.9531,0.5646,0.8985\n\
    ```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Ablation: No-PE + Mean + Low-Rank Bilinear Pooling (r=8, no MHA)\"\ntags: \"cnn, depthwise separable, dilated conv, bilinear pooling, ablation, no positional encoding\"\nabstract: \"Lower the bilinear rank from 16 to 8 in the no-PE DSConv backbone with mean + low-rank covariance pooling to test if reduced second-order capacity better balances CRISPR with APA/MRL/ncRNA.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from typing import Sequence\n\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dilation: int = 1\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.d_model,\n              kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n              feature_group_count=self.d_model,\n      \
    \        padding='SAME',\n              kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,),\n              name='dw_conv'\n          )(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,), name='pw_conv')(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  class LowRankBilinearPool(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      r: int = 8\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          u = nn.Dense(self.r)(x)\n          u = u - jnp.mean(u, axis=1, keepdims=True)\n          cov = jnp.einsum('blr,bls->brs', u, u) / (x.shape[1] + 1e-6)\n          cov_flat = cov.reshape((cov.shape[0], self.r * self.r))\n          cov_flat = nn.LayerNorm()(cov_flat)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(cov_flat)\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return z\n\n  class RNANetMeanBi8(nn.Module):\n\
    \      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = 'regression'\n      d_model: int = 256\n      num_blocks: int = 5\n      dilations: Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      r_bilinear: int = 8\n      positional_encoding: bool = False\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(x)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              d = self.dilations[i] if i < len(self.dilations) else self.dilations[-1]\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(self.d_model, self.kernel_size, d, self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          mean_vec = jnp.mean(h, axis=1)\n          bilin_vec = LowRankBilinearPool(self.d_model, r=self.r_bilinear, dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          fused = jnp.concatenate([mean_vec,\
    \ bilin_vec], axis=-1)\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(fused)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(z)\n          if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  class DSConvMean_Bi8Network:\n      def __init__(self, d_input, d_output, task_type, **kwargs):\n          self.network = RNANetMeanBi8(d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type, **kwargs)\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params':\
    \ params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'd_model': 256,\n          'num_blocks': 5,\n          'dilations': [1, 2, 4, 8, 16],\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'r_bilinear': 8,\n          'positional_encoding': False\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      d_output = hparams.get('d_output', 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get('task_type', 'regression')\n      return DSConvMean_Bi8Network(\n          d_input=4,\n          d_output=d_output,\n          task_type=task_type,\n          d_model=hparams.get('d_model', 256),\n          num_blocks=hparams.get('num_blocks', 5),\n          dilations=tuple(hparams.get('dilations', [1, 2, 4, 8, 16])),\n\
    \          kernel_size=hparams.get('kernel_size', 7),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.1),\n          r_bilinear=hparams.get('r_bilinear', 8),\n          positional_encoding=hparams.get('positional_encoding', False),\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      ds = trial_data.get('dataset')\n      vm = trial_data.get('val_metric')\n      print(f\"[Complete] {ds} val_metric={vm}\")\n```"
thinking_content: "**Analyzing CRI-Off Performance**\n\nThe CRI-Off metrics show they remain low, with the highest score being 0.0858 in Eval 31. Overall, the current score is 0.6622, while the best SOTA is at 0.6801 with a CRI-Off of 0.0788. They've addressed a kernel_dilation bug, and their token usage is at 25%. Currently, at tick 49, they're one tick away from maturity next at tick 50, which will allow them to publish after updating their ablations CSV with new results.**Updating Ablations and Strategy**\n\nWe need to include the results from 95 in the ablations.csv, as they've already added 90 and 91. They updated the CSV at tick 48, and now it's essential to add 95's results since they came in at tick 49. Also, when 96 finishes, they should revise it again.  \n\nRegarding next steps, the bilinear r=8 run 96 is ongoing, and results are pending. They could also consider adjusting attention temperature or head count to improve CRI-Off, but previous tests show mixed results. Testing relative\
  \ positional biases might be beneficial, though it’s crucial to maintain generalizability.**Exploring Attention Strategies and Archive Submission**\n\nThey might consider a lightweight relative shift in pooling attention, using content-only with relative positional encoding. To bypass restrictions, they could add relative offsets to attention weights, similar to logit biases derived from relative distances, but I wonder about the complexity of the code.\n\nAnother idea is to implement \"pairwise gating,\" mixing across positions like gated cross-correlation. Although they tried offset correlation pooling before and it was counterproductive, perhaps a combination with better normalization could improve outcomes, but time is tight.\n\nAlso, as they approach maturity at tick 50, they should prepare to submit their results and methods to the Archive Room after that tick.**Preparing for Archive Submission**\n\nThey should start preparing the content files for storage, like \"paper.md\" and\
  \ the submission script. It's also crucial to check that the SOTA script is accurate by fixing the dilation bug and updating the lineage file.\n\nRegarding code improvements, they might adjust dropout and weight decay, but I remember that a constant learning rate worked better than a cosine schedule. Testing PRNG seeding could be an option, but it's not essential.\n\nAt tick 50, they can begin drafting the Archive capsule, including a results table with multiple evaluation IDs. Plus, they might even try an improved variant with no positional encoding and more blocks to enhance depth beyond five.**Planning Next Steps for Experiments and Archive Submission**\n\nTime is limited, so they should look into implementing local gating mixing with depthwise convolution across channels or relative attention in pooling. Keeping complexity in check is important since previous attempts with second-order correlation pooling didn’t yield improvements, so maintaining the current SOTA at 74 seems wise.\n\
  \nImmediate actions include updating ablations.csv with the 95 results and considering token management to prune back. At tick 49, the cooldown on pruning is relevant, but it seems safe to postpone until after maturity. \n\nThey'll want to draft the Archive content as tick 50 approaches, and consider submitting a new evaluation run using \"No-PE + Mean + Low-Rank Bilinear r=8 (no MHA)\" to test its performance, updating ablations.csv accordingly with the new results.**Creating Submission Code**\n\nI’ll create the submission code now. For a potential new run, I consider evaluating \"No-PE + Concat MHA (5 blocks)\" with dropout adjustments at 0.08 or 0.12, but we should avoid crowding the queue, so just one more run. \n\nNext, I’ll update ablations.csv to include the 95 results while ensuring proper formatting. After that, I’ll submit the new run titled \"Ablation: No-PE + Mean + Low-Rank Bilinear Pooling (r=8, no MHA).\" \n\nFor future tasks, we might still consider an Archive plan, but\
  \ we will handle that after tick 50. Lastly, I’ll prepare the YAML quoting for the title and tags properly."
