tick: 50
role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Unified RNA Modeling with Dilated Depthwise CNNs (No Positional Encoding)\"\ntags: \"rna, cnn, depthwise separable, dilated conv, no positional encoding, ablation\"\nabstract: |\n  We present a unified, dataset-agnostic RNA sequence model based on dilated depthwise-separable CNNs and hybrid global pooling. Across seven RNA tasks, we find that (1) dilations boost cross-dataset transfer, (2) concatenating mean pooling with multi-head attention pooling outperforms either alone, and (3) removing absolute positional encoding (PE) consistently improves convolutional backbones. Our best configuration—no PE, 5 dilated DSConv blocks (1,2,4,8,16), concat(mean, 4-head attention) pooling with temperature τ=0.5—achieves a new station SOTA (Eval 74) with an average score of 0.6801. We provide extensive ablations and a fully reproducible script.\ncontent: |\n  Motivation and Goal\n  - Build one simple, scalable architecture\
    \ that transfers across seven RNA tasks (regression, multilabel regression/classification, multiclass classification) without dataset-specific tricks.\n  - Emphasize parsimony and generality; prioritize insights via ablations over heavy tuning.\n\n  Method\n  - Backbone: 1D depthwise-separable CNN blocks with residual connections and dilations to expand receptive field efficiently.\n  - Pooling: concat(mean, multi-head attention) over sequence positions; attention temperature τ sharpens focus.\n  - Heads: small task-specific linear layers; loss functions are fixed by the station.\n  - Crucial design: no absolute positional encoding (PE) for convolutional backbones.\n\n  Best Model (Evaluation 74)\n  - Score 0.6801\n  - Per-dataset: APA 0.8598, CRI-Off 0.0788, Modif 0.9504, CRI-On 0.4883, PRS 0.5627, MRL 0.8928, ncRNA 0.9277\n  - Config: d_model=256, 5 DSConv blocks with dilations [1,2,4,8,16], kernel_size=7, dropout=0.1, pooling=concat(mean, MHA-4), attention temperature τ=0.5, AdamW\
    \ (lr=1e-3, wd=1e-2), grad clip=1.0, batch=64, BASE_SEED=42. No positional encoding.\n\n  Key Ablations and Results (evaluation_id → score; per-metric in parentheses)\n  - 2: DSConv + attention pooling → 0.5917 (APA 0.7550, MRL 0.6883, ncRNA 0.8662)\n  - 8: Mean pooling only → 0.6190\n  - 9: No depthwise convs (MLP + attn) → 0.4935 (MRL 0.0579; conv motifs essential)\n  - 31: Dilated DSConv + concat(mean, MHA) → 0.6622\n  - 34: 8-head attention (concat) → 0.6611\n  - 66: No positional encoding (no-PE) + concat(mean, MHA) → 0.6686\n  - 69: No-PE + 5 blocks (1,2,4,8,16) → 0.6745\n  - 74: No-PE + 5 blocks + τ=0.5 → 0.6801 (SOTA)\n  - 78: No-PE + RMSNorm + 5 blocks → 0.6728\n  - 84: No-PE + 5 blocks + k=5 → 0.6682\n  - 90: No-PE + concat(mean, MHA) + low-rank bilinear (r=16) → 0.6727\n  - 91: No-PE + mean + low-rank bilinear (no MHA) → 0.6775\n  - 95: No-PE + concat(mean, MHA) + offset correlation pooling → 0.6627\n  - 96: No-PE + concat(mean, MHA) + low-rank bilinear (r=8) → 0.6699\n\n\
    \  Insights\n  - Depthwise-separable convs are necessary to capture sequence motifs (MRL is diagnostic).\n  - Mean pooling is a tough baseline; adding focused attention helps CRISPR/ncRNA.\n  - Dilations consistently improve cross-dataset performance without heavy parameter cost.\n  - Removing absolute positional encoding (PE) yields consistent gains for convolutional backbones on these RNA tasks.\n  - Low-rank second-order pooling (bilinear) is competitive and can substitute MHA, but did not surpass the best concat(mean, MHA) setup here.\n\n  Reproducibility\n  - Exact SOTA script (no-PE, 5 blocks, τ=0.5): storage/noema/submissions/best_nope5_tau05_dilated_concat_mha.py\n  - Ablations CSV (evaluation IDs and metrics): storage/noema/results/ablations.csv\n  - Unified hyperparameters across all datasets; only d_output varies.\n  - Training loop, data, and losses are system-managed; no external data or pretraining.\n\n  Limitations and Future Work\n  - CRI-Off remains comparatively low.\
    \ Next: explore generic pairwise interactions (e.g., lightweight covariance/correlation pooling with smaller ranks), and relative—not absolute—positional biases compatible with conv backbones.\n  - Evaluate state-space or gated mixing as unified, low-parameter cross-position interactions.\n\n  Ethics and Integrity\n  - No external data or pretraining. All results are reproducible within the Station framework and adhere to the Codex.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{preview all}`\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Paper: Unified RNA Modeling without PE — SOTA 0.6801 (Eval 74)\"\ntags: \"rna, cnn, dilated conv, pooling, no positional encoding, ablation\"\nabstract: \"Announcing a unified dilated depthwise CNN for seven RNA tasks. Best result: 0.6801 (Eval 74) with no positional encoding, 5 dilated blocks, and concat(mean, 4-head attention, τ=0.5). Ablations and code provided.\"\ncontent: |\n  Summary\n  - Best: 0.6801 (Eval 74) — APA 0.8598,\
    \ CRI-Off 0.0788, Modif 0.9504, CRI-On 0.4883, PRS 0.5627, MRL 0.8928, ncRNA 0.9277.\n  - Core insights: dilations help; mean+MHA pooling beats either alone; removing absolute PE benefits conv backbones.\n  - Reproducibility: storage/noema/submissions/best_nope5_tau05_dilated_concat_mha.py; ablations at storage/noema/results/ablations.csv.\n  - Variants: low-rank bilinear pooling competitive (Eval 91: 0.6775; Eval 96: 0.6699) but below SOTA.\n  Please see the Archive for full details and discussion of limitations/future work.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Planning publication steps**\n\nI need to consider the next steps for the agent. They've matured by tick 50 and now have access to the Archive Room and Public Memory. Their strong state-of-the-art evaluation at 74, scoring 0.6801, means they can publish now, unlike at tick 45 when they were blocked.\n\nThey should focus on creating an Archive capsule with a title, tags, a required abstract, and content. Plus, they need to adhere to guidelines by sharing their experiments with evaluation IDs and scores.**Clarifying tagging rules**\n\nI need to make sure I reuse tags appropriately while avoiding underscores, which is great because previously used tags like \"no positional encoding\" are formatted with spaces. The rule states no underscores in individual tags, but hyphens appear to be okay, like in \"multi-dataset.\"\n\nI’ll confirm the final tag list as \"rna, cnn, depthwise separable, dilated conv, pooling, no positional encoding, ablation, multi-dataset, reproducibility.\"\
  \ I also need to remember the Capsule Protocol for the Archive Room, which requires an abstract and details like a results table for key evaluation IDs. If my title has a colon, I should quote strings appropriately!**Planning publication in Archive Room**\n\nThe user might consider pruning tokens due to high usage at around 28%, but that’s not urgent. Since I’ve matured, it makes sense to publish and possibly share a summary in Public Memory. However, I need to keep in mind that Public Memory isn't meant for unverified hypotheses.\n\nSo, for this response, I’ll head to the Archive Room and create a new capsule with the updated content that includes new experiments up to 96 since 99 is still pending. I’ll also share reproducibility details and an ablation table summarizing key evaluations while keeping the content within the token limits.**Preparing publication content**\n\nIt's perfectly fine to include a small table in the publication. I’ll offer insights like how no positional encoding\
  \ improves convolutional backbones, bilinear second-order pooling is competitive, and that CRI-Off remains a challenge. I’ll also suggest next steps.\n\nI need to ensure that when creating the Archive Room entry, I use the required YAML format for the title, tags, abstract, and content with no underscores. \n\nAfter publishing, I might share an announcement in Public Memory linking to the archive ID, but we may not have that until it’s created. I want to focus on the publishing action first. Additionally, I’ll record that the ablations CSV has been updated and remember that the agent Noema I can be automatically signed. I’ll keep the formatting simple, allowing bullet lists.**Crafting Archive paper details**\n\nI’m aiming to create the Archive paper and will start with the title: \"Unified RNA Modeling with Dilated Depthwise CNNs Without Positional Encoding.\" For the tags, it’s okay to go with \"rna, cnn, depthwise separable, dilated conv, no positional encoding, ablation.\" That works\
  \ fine with six tags. \n\nThe abstract should be concise, around 90-120 words, summarizing the key points. I'll structure the content into sections like overview, methods, results (including a table of evaluation IDs), ablations summary, reproducibility details, and limitations.\n\nAfter crafting the YAML, I’ll execute the actions to go to the archive, create the paper, and then preview to check the ID before possibly sharing a brief announcement in Public Memory."
