role: model
parts:
- text: "The cross-half bilinear helped CRI-On but didn’t improve CRI-Off. I’ll try a learned bipartition to let the model discover interaction groups (useful for concatenated sequences) while remaining unified.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"No-PE + Concat MHA + Learned Bipartite Bilinear Pooling (r=16)\"\ntags: \"cnn, depthwise separable, dilated conv, learned bipartition, bilinear pooling, no positional encoding\"\nabstract: \"Replace fixed mid-split with a learned per-position bipartition and compute a low-rank bilinear outer product between group summaries. Fuse with concat(mean, MHA) pooling on a no-PE dilated DSConv backbone. Unified across datasets; targets pairwise interactions (e.g., concatenated inputs) to lift CRI-Off without harming others.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from typing import Sequence\n\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n\
    \      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dilation: int = 1\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.d_model,\n              kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n              feature_group_count=self.d_model,\n              padding='SAME',\n              kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,),\n              name='dw_conv'\n          )(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,), name='pw_conv')(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  class MultiHeadAttnPool1D(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      num_heads: int = 4\n      attn_temp: float = 0.5\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          B, L, C = x.shape\n          H = self.num_heads\n          assert C % H == 0\n          Dh = C // H\n         \
    \ k = nn.DenseGeneral(features=(H, Dh), axis=-1, name='k_proj')(x)   # (B,L,H,Dh)\n          v = nn.DenseGeneral(features=(H, Dh), axis=-1, name='v_proj')(x)   # (B,L,H,Dh)\n          q = self.param('q', nn.initializers.normal(0.02), (H, Dh))\n          scale = jnp.sqrt(jnp.asarray(Dh, dtype=x.dtype))\n          logits = jnp.einsum('blhd,hd->blh', k, q) / (scale * self.attn_temp)\n          w = nn.softmax(logits, axis=1)                                     # (B,L,H)\n          pooled = jnp.einsum('blh,blhd->bhd', w, v).reshape(B, C)           # (B,C)\n          mean_vec = jnp.mean(x, axis=1)                                     # (B,C)\n          fused = jnp.concatenate([mean_vec, pooled], axis=-1)               # (B,2C)\n          fused = nn.LayerNorm()(fused)\n          fused = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(fused)\n          fused = nn.gelu(fused)\n          fused = nn.Dropout(rate=0.1)(fused, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return fused\n\n  class LearnedBipartiteBilinearPool(nn.Module):\n\
    \      d_model: int\n      r: int = 16\n      tau: float = 1.0\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x: (B, L, C)\n          B, L, C = x.shape\n          # Per-position logits for 2 groups -> soft bipartition\n          logits = nn.Dense(2)(x)                         # (B, L, 2)\n          w = nn.softmax(logits / self.tau, axis=-1)      # (B, L, 2)\n          wA = w[..., 0]                                   # (B, L)\n          wB = w[..., 1]                                   # (B, L)\n          # Low-rank projection\n          U = nn.Dense(self.r)(x)                          # (B, L, r)\n          eps = 1e-6\n          # Weighted means per group\n          sumA = jnp.sum(wA, axis=1, keepdims=True) + eps  # (B,1)\n          sumB = jnp.sum(wB, axis=1, keepdims=True) + eps  # (B,1)\n          muA = jnp.sum(U * wA[..., None], axis=1) / sumA  # (B, r)\n          muB = jnp.sum(U * wB[..., None], axis=1) / sumB  #\
    \ (B, r)\n          # Bilinear outer product\n          M = jnp.einsum('br,bq->brq', muA, muB)           # (B, r, r)\n          flat = M.reshape((B, self.r * self.r))           # (B, r*r)\n          flat = nn.LayerNorm()(flat)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(flat)                 # (B, C)\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return z\n\n  class RNANetLBBP(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = 'regression'\n      d_model: int = 256\n      num_blocks: int = 5\n      dilations: Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      num_heads: int = 4\n      attn_temp: float = 0.5\n      r_bilinear: int = 16\n      tau: float = 1.0\n      positional_encoding: bool = False\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(x)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n\
    \          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              d = self.dilations[i] if i < len(self.dilations) else self.dilations[-1]\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(self.d_model, self.kernel_size, d, self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          mha_vec = MultiHeadAttnPool1D(self.d_model, self.num_heads, self.attn_temp)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          lbbp_vec = LearnedBipartiteBilinearPool(self.d_model, r=self.r_bilinear, tau=self.tau, dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          fused = jnp.concatenate([mha_vec, lbbp_vec], axis=-1)  # (B, 2C)\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(fused)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(z)\n          if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1:\n\
    \              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  class DSConv_MHA_LBBP_Network:\n      def __init__(self, d_input, d_output, task_type, **kwargs):\n          self.network = RNANetLBBP(d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type, **kwargs)\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate':\
    \ 0.001,\n          'd_model': 256,\n          'num_blocks': 5,\n          'dilations': [1, 2, 4, 8, 16],\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'num_heads': 4,\n          'attn_temp': 0.5,\n          'r_bilinear': 16,\n          'tau': 1.0,\n          'positional_encoding': False\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      d_output = hparams.get('d_output', 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get('task_type', 'regression')\n      return DSConv_MHA_LBBP_Network(\n          d_input=4,\n          d_output=d_output,\n          task_type=task_type,\n          d_model=hparams.get('d_model', 256),\n          num_blocks=hparams.get('num_blocks', 5),\n          dilations=tuple(hparams.get('dilations', [1, 2, 4, 8, 16])),\n          kernel_size=hparams.get('kernel_size', 7),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.1),\n          num_heads=hparams.get('num_heads', 4),\n          attn_temp=hparams.get('attn_temp', 0.5),\n          r_bilinear=hparams.get('r_bilinear',\
    \ 16),\n          tau=hparams.get('tau', 1.0),\n          positional_encoding=hparams.get('positional_encoding', False),\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      ds = trial_data.get('dataset')\n      vm = trial_data.get('val_metric')\n      print(f\"[Complete] {ds} val_metric={vm}\")\n```"
thinking_content: "**Planning next steps for user**\n\nI need to plan the next steps for the user's evaluation submission labeled as 106. They’re waiting on the results while I think about updating the ablations CSV, as it sounds like that might be done. I could conduct a quick analysis of previous results; for instance, 101 showed a significant improvement in CRI-On but low in CRI-Off, while 102 was low overall due to an ncRNA drop. I can also manage the token usage effectively.**Analyzing bilinear results**\n\nI'm seeing that the bilinear method isn't significantly helpful for improving CRI-Off beyond about 0.06-0.068, with the best result being 0.0788 from the baseline SOTA 74 using the no-PE with tau0.5 MHA. Interestingly, evaluation 53 from Aletheia using a Siamese-like approach for CRISPR Off-target reached 0.0640 for CRI-Off but achieved 0.6685 overall. I think we should consider proposing general pairwise interactions that are dataset-agnostic, particularly focused on concatenated\
  \ sequences, like using sliding cross-correlation with gating based on the content of halves.**Exploring encoding methods and pooling**\n\nI remember trying offset correlation earlier, but it wasn't effective. I wonder about relative position encoding; while we found no absolute PE beneficial, relative PE might not break unification. However, the training code limits dataset-specific features. But, I could consider adding a generic relative position bias in pooling, perhaps through length-wise positional weighting.\n\nAnother thought involves a cheaper cross-bilinear method with a linear center-of-mass split, which wasn't robust. Using a learned soft mask to separate sequence contributions into two groups for bilinear computation may be more adaptable across datasets, similar to \"learned partition pooling\" or \"attention-based bipartite pooling.\"**Implementing gating and pooling strategies**\n\nI'm planning to implement a gating mechanism that separates positions into two groups using\
  \ a sigmoid function, which will help compute group means through low-rank projection and then form an outer product. This should enable the model to learn to separate groups when datasets have concatenation structures. For datasets without it, trivial gating may apply, leading to bilinear outputs nearing zero, but still stable.\n\nI might also use \"co-attention pooling\" with two learnable queries to generate attention weights for groups A and B, followed by computing bilinear summaries for both groups. Additionally, incorporating \"anti-symmetric pair features\" could involve calculating means and differences across neighbors, keeping in mind the need for architecture consistency across datasets. Currently, run 106 is active, and I can queue another run since there’s a limit of 2 concurrent evaluations.**Planning new submissions and model implementation**\n\nI want to ensure I don't exceed the limit of two concurrent evaluations since only 106 is currently running, allowing me to submit\
  \ one more. I plan on proposing the \"learned bipartition bilinear pooling\" variant, potentially combining it with the mean or MHA for simplicity. \n\nI need to structure the gating mechanism to separate inputs into two groups and compute summary vectors while maintaining stability. I’ll implement necessary modules and ensure they don’t reference lineage imports to avoid previous bugs. After evaluation, I can update the ablations CSV while keeping the code minimal to stay within token usage limits. Finally, I can submit the new method alongside an ablation variant without MHA.**Considering token management and submission preparation**\n\nI'm reviewing token management, noting that we pruned at tick 43 and now it’s tick 54, so we could prune again soon if usage exceeds 75%, though we’re currently at 46%. It doesn't seem necessary to prune right now. \n\nI'm moving forward to craft the YAML code for a new submission titled “No-PE + Concat MHA + Learned Bipartite Bilinear Pooling (r=16).”\
  \ I’ll ensure all sections are properly formatted and consistent, then prepare to submit the action. I’ll also remember to add minimal commentary to the submission for clarity."
