parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Addendum (Tick 62): Hybrid/content-aware pooling results and conclusion\n  - Eval 115 (No-PE + Hybrid Pooling: fixed-query MHA+mean, plus gated content-aware MHA, τ=0.5): 0.6656\n    • APA 0.8483, CRI-Off 0.0488, CRI-On 0.4582, PRS 0.5659, MRL 0.8884, Modif 0.9511, ncRNA 0.8985\n  - Summary of pairwise/content-aware attempts:\n    • Low-rank bilinear pooling (no MHA) competitive (Evals 91: 0.6775; 99: 0.6696) but below SOTA.\n    • Cross-half bilinear (101) improved CRI-On (0.5503) but hurt others; overall 0.6700.\n    • Learned bipartite bilinear (107) slightly raised CRI-Off (0.0659) but degraded CRI-On; 0.6589 overall.\n    • Content-aware query pooling (110/113) regressed APA/Modif; hybrid (115) did not help.\n  - Conclusion: Our SOTA (Eval 74: 0.6801) with a simple, unified backbone (no positional encoding; 5 dilated DSConv blocks with concat mean + fixed-query MHA, τ=0.5) remains the best trade-off.\
    \ Attempts to encode generic pairwise interactions or content-aware pooling did not improve aggregate performance under station constraints.\n  - Reproducibility: SOTA script and ablations log are preserved in lineage storage (storage/noema). We will draft a short follow-up paper detailing negative results and insights on pairwise pooling in unified CNNs for RNA.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Update (Tick 62): Hybrid/content-aware pooling evaluation (ID 115) scored 0.6656 — below SOTA (0.6801, ID 74). Pairwise and content-aware variants did not yield net gains; cross-half bilinear lifted CRI-On but harmed others. Conclusion: keep the simple no-PE dilated DSConv with concat(mean + fixed-query MHA, τ=0.5) as the current best. Preparing a concise follow-up note with negative results + lessons.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{meta}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Noema Meta (Tick 62)\n  - Status: SOTA intact (0.6801, Eval 74). Hybrid/content-aware\
    \ pooling (Eval 115) < SOTA; pairwise variants not net-beneficial.\n  - Next: Write short follow-up paper on pairwise/content-aware pooling negatives and insights; ensure artifacts + commands are reproducible.\n  - Guardrails: unified architecture, no dataset-specific logic; lr=1e-3; batch=64; clip=1.0.\n  - Token plan: prune again after cooldown if budget >75%.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Considering collaboration rules**


  I think the focus here is on how specific datasets influence CRI-Off''s functional capabilities. We might want to look into using a unified general pairwise interaction to improve tasks, even when it''s heavy. Documentation is essential, so I’ll update the archive addendum regarding hybrid pooling negative results and public memory. Sharing high-level code in shared storage seems allowed, but I need to remember that low-level collaboration is forbidden, like importing someone else''s code.**Clarifying code sharing rules**


  I''m noticing some complexity in the rules around code sharing in the Research Counter. It states that agents can review and clone other agents'' code through official channels, and shared storage is meant for collaboration and sharing intermediate results. However, there''s a caveat about low-level collaboration being forbidden, which feels contradictory. The instructions allow for importing modules from other lineage storages, but I''m not sure how that aligns with the collaboration restrictions. It seems a bit unclear!**Examining code sharing and CRI-Off**


  I see that code sharing is permitted, but the "forbidden" rule likely aims to prevent direct collaboration through chat or email. We can share our own utilities without rewriting others'' code. Next, I need to focus on the architecture that models pairwise interactions along their entire length, like using a lightweight polynomial interaction that computes Gram features across channels. For CRI-Off, we need to ensure interactions are unified across datasets but without specific knowledge of concatenation boundaries. The sequence length is a bit confusing; I see some inconsistencies around the maximum length being 43 or 46.**Exploring splitting and pooling methods**


  I''m grappling with the idea of splitting at 23 vs. 20, especially since sgRNA and target concatenation might not be exactly half. A mid-split could cause mismatches, which may explain the poor improvements. I can''t tailor a dataset-specific method, but using a "learned split" with content-driven gating could provide general projections of both sides. We tried "learned bipartite bilinear pooling," and while it improved CRI-Off slightly, CRI-On dropped significantly. Other strategies, like "relative positional bias" or "Offset Correlation Pooling," haven’t been great either, so I’m still searching for the right approach!**Finalizing research tasks and documentation**


  I''m considering several ideas for improving our methods, like using local cross-attention windows and depthwise convolution mixing, but I acknowledge the limitations of CRI-Off''s small dataset. I''m preparing for our second paper, which needs five experiments, so we’re in good shape. I plan to update the archive with the results from eval 115, noting that hybrid pooling didn''t help. I''m also thinking about adjusting our meta prompt to reflect closing this CRISPR loop and planning for future work. Let''s wrap this up efficiently!**Implementing final adjustments**


  I''ve tried using 8 heads before, but it turns out that 4 heads works best. I’m worried that 2 heads might degrade performance, so I think it’s best to stick with 4. I also want to make sure to prune tokens again in 4 ticks if necessary. Additionally, I should include storing our ablation logs into the lineage storage, which I’ve already done previously. Now, I’m ready to produce the final adjustments!'
