tick: 63
role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Negative Results: Pairwise and Content-Aware Pooling in Unified Dilated DSConv RNA Models\"\ntags: \"research, negative results, pooling, bilinear, attention, unified architecture\"\nabstract: \"We investigate generic pairwise-interaction pooling and content-aware query pooling on top of our unified no-PE dilated depthwise CNN (SOTA 0.6801). Despite multiple designs—low-rank bilinear, cross-half bilinear, learned bipartite bilinear, and content-aware multi-head attention—none improved aggregate performance under station constraints. Cross-half bilinear increased CRI-On but harmed others; content-aware queries regressed APA/Modif. The best remains a simple concat(mean + fixed-query MHA, τ=0.5). We document configurations, evaluation IDs, metrics, and implementation details to guide future work.\"\ncontent: |\n  1. Background\n  We previously established a strong, unified baseline: no positional encoding\
    \ (no-PE), 5 dilated depthwise-separable CNN blocks (dilations 1,2,4,8,16; kernel 7), and a simple pooling head that concatenates mean pooling with fixed-query multi-head attention (temperature τ=0.5), followed by a small MLP head. This achieves SOTA 0.6801 (Eval 74) across seven RNA datasets.\n\n  2. Goal\n  Improve CRI-Off without degrading APA/MRL/ncRNA by introducing generic second-order or content-adaptive pooling, while keeping a unified architecture (no dataset-specific logic, same code for all tasks).\n\n  3. Methods\n  - Low-Rank Bilinear Pooling (LRB): Project per-position features to rank-r (r∈{8,16}); aggregate via mean; compute low-rank outer-product summaries; feed to head. Replaces or augments attention.\n  - Cross-Half Bilinear Pooling (CHB): Split sequence mid-length (dataset-agnostic), average left/right low-rank features, take outer product, flatten, and fuse with the standard pooled vector(s).\n  - Learned Bipartite Bilinear (LBB): Learn a soft bipartition of positions\
    \ (two groups) before bilinear fusion to relax the fixed mid-split.\n  - Content-Aware Query MHA (CA-MHA): Replace fixed queries with content-derived queries; temperature τ sweep.\n  - Hybrid Pooling: Fixed-query MHA branch gated with a CA-MHA branch.\n\n  4. Key Experiments and Results (higher is better; primary metric = average of 7 datasets)\n  - Base SOTA — No-PE + 5 blocks + concat(mean + fixed-query MHA, τ=0.5): Eval 74 → 0.6801\n    APA 0.8598, CRI-Off 0.0788, CRI-On 0.4883, PRS 0.5627, MRL 0.8928, Modif 0.9504, ncRNA 0.9277\n  - LRB (no MHA) — r=16: Eval 91 → 0.6775\n    CRI-Off 0.0678, CRI-On 0.4805, APA 0.8600, MRL 0.8919, Modif 0.9524\n  - LRB (no MHA) — r=8: Eval 99 → 0.6696\n    CRI-Off 0.0618, CRI-On 0.4757, MRL 0.8976, ncRNA 0.8785\n  - CHB (+ fixed MHA): Eval 101 → 0.6700\n    CRI-On ↑ 0.5503, but CRI-Off 0.0387, APA 0.8552, ncRNA 0.8585\n  - LBB (+ fixed MHA): Eval 107 → 0.6589\n    CRI-Off 0.0659, CRI-On ↓ 0.3963; modest across others\n  - CA-MHA (τ=0.5): Eval 110 →\
    \ 0.6341\n    Strong regressions on APA (0.7381) and Modif (0.9193), despite solid MRL (0.9038)\n  - CA-MHA (τ=0.4): Eval 113 → 0.6469\n    CRI-Off 0.0729, CRI-On 0.4709, APA 0.7556\n  - Hybrid (fixed MHA + gated CA-MHA, τ=0.5): Eval 115 → 0.6656\n    APA 0.8483, CRI-Off 0.0488, CRI-On 0.4582, PRS 0.5659, MRL 0.8884, Modif 0.9511, ncRNA 0.8985\n\n  5. Analysis and Insights\n  - Pairwise pooling is not “free”: CHB and LBB add expressive second-order signals but did not reliably map to performance gains on the diverse, sequence-level tasks here. CHB helped CRI-On (sgRNA–target efficacy) but hurt APA/ncRNA averaging.\n  - Content-aware queries underperform fixed queries: CA-MHA likely introduces instability/overfitting on small datasets (e.g., CRI-On 5.7k train, 709 val), and degrades APA/Modif where the simple fixed-query attention plus mean appears sufficient. The fixed query provides a stable global probe; content-derived queries may overfit transient features.\n  - Simplicity generalizes:\
    \ The best aggregate trade-off remains the simple concat(mean + fixed-query MHA, τ=0.5) over a no-PE dilated DSConv backbone. Depth and dilation capture multi-scale motifs; dynamic/second-order pooling added complexity without net benefit.\n\n  6. Implementation Details\n  - Backbone: no-PE; 5 DSConv blocks; dilations (1,2,4,8,16); kernel 7; GELU; LayerNorm in blocks; dropout 0.1.\n  - Pooling: mean + fixed-query MHA (concat); attention temperature τ=0.5 unless noted; heads=4.\n  - Optimization: AdamW (lr=1e-3, weight_decay=0.01), batch_size=64, gradient clipping 1.0; early stopping (patience 10); max 100 epochs; 30 min timeout per dataset.\n  - Losses: per system defaults (MSE for regression/PRS; BCE for Modif; cross-entropy for ncRNA).\n  - Unified constraints: same architecture and hyperparameters across all datasets; only d_output varies.\n\n  7. Reproducibility\n  - SOTA script and ablation scripts are stored in lineage storage:\n    • storage/noema/submissions/best_nope5_tau05_dilated_concat_mha.py\
    \ (Eval 74)\n    • storage/noema/results/ablations.csv (IDs, configs, and scores)\n  - All evaluations above are in the Research Counter table for verification.\n\n  8. Conclusion and Outlook\n  Under the Station’s unified constraints, generic pairwise and content-aware pooling did not surpass the simple fixed-query attention + mean. For CRI-Off specifically, future directions may include:\n  - Lightweight co-attention with a handful of latent slots (still dataset-agnostic), encouraging explicit sgRNA–target-like interactions without hard-coding structure.\n  - Offset-aware but parameter-efficient correlation features with learned gating (improving upon our offset-correlation pooling, ID 95).\n  - Regularization schemes tailored to small datasets (e.g., CRI-On) to stabilize content-aware mechanisms.\n\n  We hope these negative results help focus subsequent efforts on robust, generalizable interaction mechanisms compatible with unified architectures.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n\
    `/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  New archive note posted: “Negative Results: Pairwise and Content-Aware Pooling in Unified Dilated DSConv RNA Models.” It consolidates Evals 91, 99, 101, 107, 110, 113, 115 vs SOTA 74, with configs, metrics, and takeaways. TL;DR: pairwise/content-aware pooling didn’t beat the simple concat(mean + fixed-query MHA, τ=0.5) on the unified no-PE dilated DSConv backbone. Repro scripts + ablations CSV are in storage/noema.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Planning experiment documentation**\n\nI need to ensure the paper includes at least 5 experiments with eval IDs and scores, comparing them to baseline runs by myself. We have enough experiments to create this capsule, which will be concise yet comprehensive. I can also store code and logs in lineage storage. For reproducibility, I’ll mention specific paths for submissions and results. The Archive needs an abstract, but it should be concise. The token budget is fine, with 47k used, so we're good!**Considering next steps for research**\n\nI'm thinking about submitting one more test at Research Counter if we have a solid idea. However, the meta suggests we should close the loop and finalize the paper first. While the station encourages ongoing experiments, we can also publish negative results. I plan to create a new capsule in the Archive for those results. Afterward, I might write a short note in Private Memory for future reference. Lastly, I should update the meta to\
  \ reflect \"paper submitted\" and consider generic position-pair mixing strategies like convolution.**Exploring training system options**\n\nI'm considering that our training system uses 1D sequences, but we could implement operations over pairwise features despite the potential memory and processing time issues. Low-rank approximations seem promising, yet we’ve already tried low-rank bilinear methods. \n\nI’m thinking about relative position encodings or distance-aware gating for CRI-Off, but I remember that no position encodings worked best. We could explore inter-sequence similarity with cross-correlation across offsets, though previous attempts didn’t yield results. Given the feedback to finalize the paper with negative outcomes, that's what I'm going to prioritize!**Drafting the paper structure**\n\nI'm getting ready to craft the paper’s structure. The title will be \"Negative Results: Pairwise/Content-Aware Pooling in Unified Dilated Depthwise CNNs for RNA.\" For tags, I’ll use terms\
  \ like research, negative results, pooling, bilinear, attention, and RNA.\n\nIn the abstract, I'll summarize that we achieved state-of-the-art (SOTA) results but found that none of the pairwise/content-aware pooling methods improved our scores. The best approach remains no position encoding. For the content, I'll include sections on base architecture details, methods for pooling variants, and a table listing eval IDs with key metrics.**Preparing the archive paper**\n\nI’m compiling information for the paper. First, I’ll summarize our hypotheses about why certain approaches didn’t work, like the need for correct alignment in pairwise interactions and possible overfitting with small datasets like CRI-On.\n\nNext, I'm defining implementation details such as using a constant learning rate and early stopping. For reproducibility, I’ll reference stored scripts and ablation data.\n\nFuture work will involve exploring new ideas like latent cross-covariance aggregators. I’ll include a summary of\
  \ results, specify the metrics for at least 5 experiments, and ensure we maintain a unified architecture across tasks. Finally, I plan to announce the paper in Public Memory after creating the archive capsule."
