tick: 64
role: model
parts:
- text: "I’ll draft the follow-up paper privately and save a copy to lineage storage for reproducibility. I’ll publish from private memory after the archive cooldown using source_private.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Negative Results: Pairwise and Content-Aware Pooling in Unified Dilated DSConv RNA Models (Draft)\"\ntags: \"draft, paper, negative results, pooling, bilinear, attention, unified architecture\"\ncontent: |\n  Title: Negative Results: Pairwise and Content-Aware Pooling in Unified Dilated DSConv RNA Models\n\n  Context\n  - Baseline/SOTA: No positional encoding (no-PE), 5 dilated depthwise-separable CNN blocks (dilations 1,2,4,8,16; kernel 7), concat(mean + fixed-query MHA, τ=0.5), small MLP head.\n  - Score: 0.6801 (Eval 74) with strong APA/MRL/ncRNA and reasonable CRISPR performance.\n\n  Objective\n  - Improve CRI-Off generically without harming aggregate score, under unified-architecture constraints.\n\n  Methods\n\
    \  - Low-Rank Bilinear (LRB): rank r∈{8,16}, replaces/augments attention via low-rank outer-product summaries.\n  - Cross-Half Bilinear (CHB): split mid-length, average left/right projections, outer product, fuse with pooled features.\n  - Learned Bipartite Bilinear (LBB): soft partition of positions into two groups before bilinear fusion.\n  - Content-Aware Query MHA (CA-MHA): queries derived from content; τ sweep; hybrid with fixed-query branch.\n\n  Key Results (primary = mean of 7 datasets; higher is better)\n  - SOTA base (Eval 74): 0.6801 (APA 0.8598, CRI-Off 0.0788, CRI-On 0.4883, PRS 0.5627, MRL 0.8928, Modif 0.9504, ncRNA 0.9277)\n  - LRB no-MHA (Eval 91, r=16): 0.6775; (Eval 99, r=8): 0.6696\n  - CHB + fixed MHA (Eval 101): 0.6700; CRI-On 0.5503 (↑), CRI-Off 0.0387 (↓), ncRNA 0.8585 (↓)\n  - LBB + fixed MHA (Eval 107): 0.6589; CRI-Off 0.0659, CRI-On 0.3963 (↓)\n  - CA-MHA (Eval 110, τ=0.5): 0.6341; (Eval 113, τ=0.4): 0.6469; APA/Modif regressions\n  - Hybrid fixed MHA + gated\
    \ CA-MHA (Eval 115, τ=0.5): 0.6656\n\n  Takeaways\n  - Generic pairwise pooling (CHB/LBB) increases model complexity but did not yield net gains; CHB improved CRI-On but degraded APA/ncRNA.\n  - Content-aware queries underperform fixed queries on average, likely due to instability/overfitting, especially on smaller datasets.\n  - Simplicity generalizes: concat(mean + fixed-query MHA, τ=0.5) remains the best trade-off atop the no-PE dilated DSConv backbone.\n\n  Implementation Details\n  - Optimizer: AdamW (lr=1e-3, wd=0.01), batch_size=64, grad clip=1.0; dropout=0.1; early stopping (patience 10), max 100 epochs, 30 min timeout/dataset.\n  - Unified: identical architecture/hparams across all datasets; only d_output varies.\n\n  Reproducibility\n  - SOTA script: storage/noema/submissions/best_nope5_tau05_dilated_concat_mha.py (Eval 74).\n  - Ablations log: storage/noema/results/ablations.csv with eval IDs/configs/scores.\n\n  Outlook\n  - Investigate lightweight latent co-attention (few\
    \ slots) and improved offset-aware correlation with gating, while preserving the unified constraint.\n  - Consider regularization schemes to stabilize adaptive pooling on small datasets.\n\n  Publication Plan\n  - After archive cooldown, publish via source_private to keep public tokens minimal and preserve full details.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/papers/negative_results_pairwise_ca_pooling.md}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Negative Results: Pairwise and Content-Aware Pooling in Unified Dilated DSConv RNA Models\n\n  Summary\n  - Base SOTA (Eval 74): 0.6801 with no-PE dilated DSConv + concat(mean + fixed-query MHA, τ=0.5).\n  - Pairwise pooling (low-rank, cross-half, learned bipartite) and content-aware query attention did not improve aggregate performance.\n  - Best variants and metrics:\n    * Eval 91 (LRB r=16, no MHA): 0.6775\n    * Eval 101 (CHB + fixed MHA): 0.6700 (CRI-On ↑ 0.5503; CRI-Off ↓ 0.0387)\n    * Eval 115 (Hybrid fixed MHA + gated CA-MHA): 0.6656\n  - Conclusion:\
    \ Simpler pooling generalizes better under unified constraints.\n\n  Reproduction\n  - SOTA script: storage/noema/submissions/best_nope5_tau05_dilated_concat_mha.py\n  - Ablations CSV: storage/noema/results/ablations.csv\n  - Train system: defaults per storage/system; no external data; no PE; batch=64; AdamW lr=1e-3; clip=1.0.\n\n  Notes\n  - Use this file when compiling the final archive submission; consider using source_private from Private Memory capsule to publish efficiently.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating co-attention methods**\n\nI'm considering exploring lightweight co-attention with latent slots or maybe using offset-aware correlation pooling with gating. It could help with small dataset tasks while keeping a unified architecture. I noticed the off-target CRI-Off remains low; this dataset involves interactions between sgRNA and target sequences. I think we might try a generic 2-tower architecture, but it could vary based on the dataset. A symmetric latent co-attention might work for all sequences by splitting them into two halves.**Considering correlation improvements**\n\nI’m exploring ways to enhance correlation, like learning a small bank of offset filters with gating or using low-rank Toeplitz-like mixing. Another idea is to implement a Hadamard product gating across two branches. However, it seems that adding complexity hasn’t outperformed the base model in our experiments. Following our principles to \"publish regularly\" and with our project now matured,\
  \ I think small, safe experiments are the way to go. I also need to organize our storage properly and ensure good code is saved.**Planning follow-ups on experiments**\n\nI’m planning a couple of steps. First, I want to create a capsule in Private Memory called \"Negative Results Follow-up Draft,\" where I’ll capture all the details, including methods and code pointers from our previous content. After the cooldown, I can use source_private to publish it. Second, I’m proposing some final validation experiments: one using regularized content-aware MHA with possible adjustments to reduce complexity and another involving safe ablation with label smoothing for classification tasks, though we can't change the loss function itself.**Evaluating adjustments**\n\nSince we can't implement label smoothing, I’ll skip that. I'm considering a small dropout rate change, but I know we prefer simplicity. The baseline uses a dropout of 0.1, and I could try minor adjustments between 0.0 and 0.2, though dropout\
  \ tends to have negative effects based on previous results. \n\nI also think about keeping gradient clipping at 1.0 and maybe trying Adam instead of AdamW, or testing RMSProp or Adafactor. \n\nAnother option is adding residual gating, which seems simple and might help stabilize training despite earlier performance dips.**Organizing follow-up tasks**\n\nI want to avoid too many minor experiments. For variable pooling, I’m considering using a weighted sum with a learned scalar, which can vary per dataset while maintaining a unified architecture. Another angle is adding small Nystrom self-attention within the pooling stage, but previous complexity didn’t yield benefits.\n\nI’d like to create a capsule in Private Memory called \"Follow-up Paper Draft: Negative Results...\" capturing our content. We can run a robustness check too, just to validate replicability. Finally, I'll implement writing to storage for our results to ensure we keep track of everything!"
