tick: 67
role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{storage write noema/results/append_tick67.csv}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  evaluation_id,title,score,APA_R2,CRI-Off_Spearman,CRI-On_Spearman,PRS_R2,MRL_R2,Modif_AUC_ROC,ncRNA_Accuracy,notes\n  124,\"No-PE + SOTA Backbone with Symmetric Halfwise Interaction Features (MHA product & abs-diff)\",0.6698,0.8569,0.0468,0.4884,0.5629,0.8822,0.9515,0.9000,\"Halfwise MHA interaction (prod, abs-diff) added; no net gain; CRI-Off worse vs SOTA\"\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 3}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Tick 67 update — Eval 124\n  - Method: SOTA no-PE DSConv backbone + halfwise interaction features from fixed-query MHA on left/right halves (element-wise product and abs-diff), concatenated with global mean+MHA pooled vector.\n  - Result (ID 124): 0.6698 (APA 0.8569, CRI-Off 0.0468, CRI-On 0.4884, PRS 0.5629, MRL 0.8822, Modif 0.9515, ncRNA 0.9000).\n  - Takeaway: Does not surpass SOTA (0.6801, ID 74). CRI-Off remains below SOTA; aggregate\
    \ slightly worse due to MRL drop. Conclusion unchanged: keep simple concat(mean + fixed-query MHA, τ=0.5).\n  - Action: Logged CSV at storage/noema/results/append_tick67.csv. Proceeding to publish negative-results note from Private Capsule #3 after archive cooldown.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Examining optimizer parameters**\n\nI'm diving into the training loop, focusing on the learning rate parameter. I wonder if we can use an optax schedule with optax.adamw; it might be risky, though. Earlier findings suggested using a constant learning rate instead of cosine LR warmup. I'm also considering dropout rate tuning and the number of blocks since we've already used five. MRL had a positive response to depth, and I think we could explore a CRI-Off-specific method for improving alignment. However, I'm unsure about the boundary length for concatenation.**Analyzing dataset operations**\n\nI'm looking at the dataset details that mention an input length of 43, possibly reflecting concatenated lengths for sgRNA and target around 20 each. I'm considering whether symmetrical operations are enough. Another idea could be to use lightweight 2D convolution on the pairwise similarity matrix, computing a \"match\" matrix via dot products of embeddings, followed by small CNN\
  \ and pooling for efficiency. However, I realize that trying to compute a 2D matrix for ncRNA lengths could lead to massive memory and time issues, which isn’t feasible.**Exploring hashed feature computations**\n\nI'm considering the option of computing \"sketch\" hashed features to avoid O(L^2) complexities. Using random feature maps for polynomial kernels could be a path by approximating pairwise interactions across positions with Tensor Sketch. I could aggregate counts from a convolution kernel across positions, but starting from scratch might be cumbersome. Approximate bilinear pooling using Tensor Sketch sounds interesting, with a complexity of O(L*C*log D) implemented via FFT. I think it could work in a jax and optax environment, although it may still be resource-heavy.**Considering global token mixing**\n\nI'm exploring the idea of adopting \"global token mixing\" using S4D or a gated MLP mix after the convolution layer. We already applied depthwise convolutions, but I wonder if\
  \ introducing a gated MLP layer for token mixing would work. Adding a self-attention layer could risk breaking the \"no dataset-specific\" rule, but we could do a lightweight global attention, perhaps a \"performer\" style. Still, I’m concerned about the computation heaviness, especially with sequences of length 1136, as it could blow up to around 82 million operations per forward pass!**Exploring pooling and storage updates**\n\nI'm considering local cross-attention with strided queries, which could reduce complexity from O(L^2) to O(L*C*slots) by using a few learned queries. I could unify datasets with around four latent queries but I wonder if that will capture enough detail. I also think about multi-scale pooling to capture long-range interactions, especially for CRI-Off.\n\nOn the storage side, I need to read existing CSV files, append the new experiment ID 124 results, and create a new file. I'll need to ensure everything is updated properly without running into content overwrite\
  \ issues. It's lots to balance, but I think I can manage it!**Preparing evaluation and memory updates**\n\nI need to specify the new row for evaluation 124, including metrics like id, title, various scores, and notes in the CSV. I could read the existing header, but to simplify things, I might just produce a new file 'results/append_tick67.csv' with the necessary header and the row for 124.\n\nI also plan to update private memory capsule #3 with the results from 124 and a summary note. While I could mention the findings publicly, it may be better to keep it private. \n\nNow, I’ll plan out the commands clearly!"
