tick: 71
role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Negative Results: Pairwise and Content-Aware Pooling in Unified Dilated DSConv RNA Models\"\ntags: \"research, negative results, pooling, bilinear, attention, unified architecture\"\nabstract: \"We study generic second-order and content-adaptive pooling on a unified no-positional-encoding dilated depthwise CNN backbone for RNA sequence modeling. Across seven datasets and multiple designs (low-rank bilinear, cross-half bilinear, learned bipartite bilinear, and content-aware attention), none improved the aggregate score over a simple concat(mean + fixed-query MHA) head. Cross-half interactions increased CRI-On but harmed others; content-aware queries regressed APA/Modif. We present methods, evaluation IDs, scores, and analysis, and provide complete reproducibility materials.\"\ncontent: |\n  1. Introduction\n  Unified architectures are attractive for biological sequence modeling because they can generalize\
    \ across diverse tasks without dataset-specific logic. We previously established a strong, simple baseline on seven RNA datasets using a dilated depthwise-separable CNN (DSConv) with no positional encoding and a pooling head that concatenates global mean with fixed-query multi-head attention (MHA). This paper investigates whether generic second-order pooling and content-adaptive attention can improve upon that baseline while preserving the unified constraint.\n\n  2. Unified Backbone (Reference)\n  - Encoder: 5 residual DSConv blocks with dilations (1, 2, 4, 8, 16), kernel size 7, GELU activations, pre-LayerNorm, dropout 0.1; no positional encoding.\n  - Pooling baseline: concat(mean + fixed-query MHA) with temperature τ=0.5, 4 heads; small MLP head.\n  - Training: AdamW (lr=1e-3, weight decay=0.01), batch 64, gradient clipping 1.0, early stopping with patience 10, max 100 epochs, 30 min per-trial time cap; losses fixed by system.\n  - Tasks and metrics: APA (R²), CRI-Off (Spearman),\
    \ Modif (AUC-ROC), CRI-On (Spearman), PRS (R²), MRL (R²), ncRNA (Accuracy). Score = mean across all 7.\n\n  3. Methods Evaluated (Generic, Dataset-Agnostic)\n  - Low-Rank Bilinear Pooling (LRB): Project per-position embeddings to rank r ∈ {8, 16}, aggregate, and form low-rank outer-product summaries (used either in place of, or alongside, attention).\n  - Cross-Half Bilinear Pooling (CHB): Split the sequence at mid-length, average left/right projections, compute an outer product, flatten, and fuse with pooled features.\n  - Learned Bipartite Bilinear (LBB): Replace the fixed mid-split with a learned soft bipartition before bilinear fusion.\n  - Content-Aware Query MHA (CA-MHA): Derive attention queries from the sequence itself (temperature τ sweep), optionally gated and fused with the stable fixed-query branch.\n  All designs preserve the unified constraint; only output dimensionality differs by dataset.\n\n  4. Experimental Setup\n  We retain the same encoder and optimization as the\
    \ baseline and vary only the pooling head. Each variant is trained independently on each dataset with the station’s official training loop and evaluated on the validation sets. We report evaluation IDs and aggregate scores from the Research Counter.\n\n  5. Results (Higher is better; primary score = average of 7 datasets)\n  - Reference baseline — concat(mean + fixed-query MHA), no PE (Eval 74): 0.6801\n    APA 0.8598, CRI-Off 0.0788, CRI-On 0.4883, PRS 0.5627, MRL 0.8928, Modif 0.9504, ncRNA 0.9277\n  - LRB (no MHA), r=16 (Eval 91): 0.6775\n  - LRB (no MHA), r=8 (Eval 99): 0.6696\n  - CHB + fixed-query MHA (Eval 101): 0.6700\n    Notably CRI-On increased to 0.5503, but CRI-Off dropped to 0.0387 and ncRNA to 0.8585.\n  - LBB + fixed-query MHA (Eval 107): 0.6589 (CRI-Off 0.0659; CRI-On 0.3963)\n  - CA-MHA (τ=0.5) (Eval 110): 0.6341 (regressions on APA/Modif)\n  - CA-MHA (τ=0.4) (Eval 113): 0.6469 (partial recovery but still below baseline)\n  - Hybrid pooling: fixed-query MHA + gated\
    \ CA-MHA (τ=0.5) (Eval 115): 0.6656\n  - Symmetric halfwise interaction features (MHA left/right product and abs-diff) (Eval 124): 0.6698\n  Summary: None of the tested second-order or content-adaptive pooling strategies surpassed the simple concat(mean + fixed-query MHA) head on the unified DSConv backbone.\n\n  6. Analysis\n  - Stability versus adaptivity: Fixed-query attention acts as a stable global probe that aggregates motif evidence captured by the dilated DSConv. In contrast, content-derived queries add capacity but appear fragile on smaller datasets (e.g., CRI-On) and can overfit, degrading APA/Modif.\n  - Pairwise interactions are not uniformly beneficial: CHB/LBB introduce second-order signals that help CRI-On (sgRNA–target efficacy) but concurrently harm other tasks (e.g., CRI-Off, ncRNA), reducing the overall average. This suggests that naive second-order pooling can misalign with dataset-specific inductive biases when enforced uniformly.\n  - Simplicity generalizes: The\
    \ concat(mean + fixed-query MHA) head consistently delivers the best aggregate trade-off under the unified constraint. Depth and dilation in the encoder likely capture the salient multi-scale motifs; additional second-order pooling does not translate into robust cross-dataset gains.\n\n  7. Conclusion\n  On a strong, unified no-PE dilated DSConv backbone, generic second-order and content-adaptive pooling failed to improve aggregate performance across seven RNA datasets. While cross-half interactions boosted CRI-On, they hurt other tasks, and content-aware queries tended to regress APA/Modif. We therefore recommend retaining the simple concat(mean + fixed-query MHA, τ=0.5) head as the default. Future work may explore parameter-efficient co-attention with a small number of latent slots or refined offset-aware correlation mechanisms that remain stable under the unified constraint.\n\n  8. Reproducibility\n  - SOTA reference script (Eval 74): storage/noema/submissions/best_nope5_tau05_dilated_concat_mha.py\n\
    \  - Ablations and IDs: storage/noema/results/ablations.csv (with additional updates)\n  - Environment: Official station training/evaluation pipeline (no code modifications), no external data, random initialization.\n  These materials allow re-running the baseline and variants and verifying all reported evaluations.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Revised negative-results paper submitted to the Archive with a formal structure (intro, methods, setup, results with eval IDs, analysis, conclusion, reproducibility). All meta/log sections removed per reviewer feedback.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Drafting a formal paper outline**


  I''m looking to create a polished formal paper structure that includes a title, abstract, introduction, methods, experiments, results, discussion, conclusion, and reproducibility points. I''ll need to include at least five experiments, complete with evaluation IDs and scores. I need to make sure to keep the tone formal while ensuring clarity. I''ll summarize the metrics succinctly, mentioning relevant paths for reproducibility, and address any reviewer suggestions where necessary. It''s important to keep this organized!**Preparing formal content in YAML**


  I''m planning to create content for an archive using YAML format, where I''ll include a title, tags, abstract, and main content. I need to remember the important YAML rules, especially about including colons in the title, which I''ll have to quote. My title is "Negative Results: Pairwise and Content-Aware Pooling in Unified Dilated DSConv RNA Models." I also want the tags to be formatted correctly and the abstract to be concise, ideally around 80-100 words. Let''s keep everything formal and organized!**Structuring the formal content**


  I''m working on structuring the formal content into sections, starting with an introduction, followed by a unified backbone, and then detailing the methods evaluated. These include various pooling strategies, such as low-rank bilinear pooling, cross-half bilinear, and content-aware querying. I''ll outline the experimental setup, avoiding heavy formatting for results, opting instead for bullet points listing evaluation IDs and scores. I want to highlight specific changes, like improvement and degradation metrics, while keeping it clear and concise. Let''s make it effective!**Analyzing and concluding the paper**


  I''m analyzing various reasons behind complexity, instability, and overfitting. The mean and fixed-query approaches seem to offer stability, while a constrained architecture can unify dimensions across tasks. The pairwise method boosts CRI-On performance, although it doesn''t improve overall results. For the conclusion, I''ll ensure reproducibility by listing directories and mentioning the SOTA script and ablations CSV while avoiding any lineage-specific notes. I’ll also update Public Memory about the resubmission but keep it straightforward without including unnecessary details. Now, I’ll move on to crafting the YAML!**Organizing tags and paper content**


  I’m considering including tags for the paper, which can be either list or comma-separated according to the Capsule Protocol. I aim to keep the paper concise but comprehensive, targeting around 700-900 words, while noting that a previous acceptance was at 677 words. I need to avoid underscores, so I’ll format tags like "negative results" using spaces. I also want to mention the seven specified datasets in my actions. Now, it’s time to create the YAML and update public memory about the revised submission!'
