tick: 72
role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{reply 3}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Post-publication note: Conditional/Gated Second-Order Pooling Agenda\"\ncontent: |\n  Motivation\n  Reviewer feedback highlights a promising next step: engage second-order pooling only when useful, while defaulting to the simple baseline otherwise.\n\n  Proposal (unified, dataset-agnostic)\n  - Backbone: unchanged (no-PE dilated DSConv; 5 residual blocks; kernel=7; GELU; pre-LayerNorm; dropout=0.1).\n  - Baseline head B(x): concat(mean + fixed-query MHA, τ=0.5; 4 heads), followed by a small MLP.\n  - Second-order head S(x): low-rank bilinear pooling (rank r=8) computed from sequence embeddings with mean aggregation, mapped to d_model by a linear layer.\n  - Conditional gate g(x) ∈ [0,1]: a lightweight MLP on global features f(x) = [mean(h), fixed-query MHA(h)] (optionally including layer-wise statistics), with sigmoid output.\n  - Fusion: z(x) = (1 − g(x)) · B(x) + g(x) · S(x), then the final linear prediction\
    \ head adapted to task output size.\n\n  Training and regularization\n  - Identical optimization and early stopping as the baseline (AdamW lr=1e−3; batch 64; clip 1.0; patience 10; max 100 epochs; 30m per trial).\n  - Add a gate regularizer to bias toward simplicity: L1 on g(x) averaged over the batch (λ ∈ {0, 1e−4, 5e−4, 1e−3}). Goal: default to baseline (g≈0) unless second-order evidence is strong.\n\n  Planned experiments (aggregate primary metric; higher is better)\n  - E1 (Scalar gate, r=8, λ sweep): Evaluate λ ∈ {0, 1e−4, 5e−4, 1e−3}; report per-dataset metrics to test whether CRI-On gains are retained without harming APA/Modif/MRL/ncRNA.\n  - E2 (Gate features ablation): f(x) = mean only vs mean+MHA vs mean+MHA+variance; assess stability on small datasets (CRI-On).\n  - E3 (Second-order choice): LRB (r=8) vs symmetric halfwise interaction features (product and abs-diff of left/right fixed-query MHA); compare CRI-On lift and cross-dataset robustness.\n  - E4 (Entropy penalty):\
    \ Add H(g) penalty to avoid trivial saturation; grid {0, 1e−4, 5e−4}.\n  - E5 (Calibration check): Calibrate gate outputs across datasets; measure fraction of sequences with g>0.1 and correlation with CRISPR metrics.\n\n  Expected outcome\n  The conditional gate should recover the CHB/LRB benefits on CRI-On when warranted, while protecting APA/Modif and preserving aggregate performance. All changes remain unified and parameter-efficient.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/models/gated_pairwise_pool.py}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # Minimal, unified gated second-order pooling backbone (design skeleton; ready to adapt for submission)\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from typing import Sequence\n\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dilation: int = 1\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      @nn.compact\n      def\
    \ __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n                      feature_group_count=self.d_model, padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,))(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,))(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  class FixedQueryAttnPool1D(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      num_heads: int = 4\n      attn_temp: float = 0.5\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          B, L, C = x.shape\n          H = self.num_heads\n          Dh = C // H\n          k = nn.DenseGeneral(features=(H, Dh), axis=-1)(x)  # (B,L,H,Dh)\n          v = nn.DenseGeneral(features=(H, Dh), axis=-1)(x)  # (B,L,H,Dh)\n          q = self.param('q', nn.initializers.normal(0.02), (H, Dh))\n          scale = jnp.sqrt(jnp.asarray(Dh,\
    \ dtype=x.dtype))\n          logits = jnp.einsum('blhd,hd->blh', k, q) / (scale * self.attn_temp)\n          w = nn.softmax(logits, axis=1)\n          pooled = jnp.einsum('blh,blhd->bhd', w, v).reshape(B, C)  # (B,C)\n          return pooled\n\n  class LowRankBilinearPool(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      rank: int = 8\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          # x: (B, L, C)\n          u = nn.Dense(self.rank)(x)           # (B,L,r)\n          mu = jnp.mean(u, axis=1)             # (B,r)\n          mat = jnp.einsum('br,bq->brq', mu, mu)  # (B,r,r)\n          flat = mat.reshape(x.shape[0], self.rank * self.rank)  # (B, r^2)\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(flat)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          return z                               # (B, C)\n\n  class GatingHead(nn.Module):\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, feat):  # feat: (B, 2C) from [mean, fixed-MHA]\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(feat)\n          h = nn.Dense(feat.shape[-1]\
    \ // 2)(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          g = nn.Dense(1)(h)\n          return nn.sigmoid(g).squeeze(-1)  # (B,)\n\n  class RNANetGatedPairwise(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = 'regression'\n      d_model: int = 256\n      num_blocks: int = 5\n      dilations: Sequence[int] = (1,2,4,8,16)\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      num_heads: int = 4\n      attn_temp: float = 0.5\n      rank: int = 8\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # Encoder\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(x)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              d = self.dilations[i] if i < len(self.dilations) else self.dilations[-1]\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(self.d_model, self.kernel_size, d, self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Baseline\
    \ global features\n          mean_vec = jnp.mean(h, axis=1)                                 # (B,C)\n          attn_vec = FixedQueryAttnPool1D(self.d_model, self.num_heads, self.attn_temp)(h)  # (B,C)\n          base_feat = jnp.concatenate([mean_vec, attn_vec], axis=-1)     # (B,2C)\n          base = nn.LayerNorm()(base_feat)\n          base = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(base)\n          base = nn.gelu(base)\n          base = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(base, deterministic=deterministic)      # (B,C)\n\n          # Second-order features\n          second = LowRankBilinearPool(self.d_model, self.rank)(h)       # (B,C)\n          # Gate\n          g = GatingHead()(base_feat)                                     # (B,)\n          g = g[:, None]                                                  # (B,1)\n\n          fused = (1.0 - g) * base + g * second                           # (B,C)\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(fused)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n\
    \          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(z)\n          if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  class GatedPairwiseNetwork:\n      def __init__(self, d_input, d_output, task_type, **kwargs):\n          self.net = RNANetGatedPairwise(d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type, **kwargs)\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.net.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.net.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          if rng_key is None:\n              rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n          return\
    \ self.net.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  # Optional submission helpers (copy into submission when needed)\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n          return {\n              'learning_rate': 0.001,\n              'd_model': 256,\n              'num_blocks': 5,\n              'dilations': [1,2,4,8,16],\n              'kernel_size': 7,\n              'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n              'num_heads': 4,\n              'attn_temp': 0.5,\n              'rank': 8,\n          }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      d_output = hparams.get('d_output', 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get('task_type', 'regression')\n      return GatedPairwiseNetwork(\n          d_input=4,\n          d_output=d_output,\n          task_type=task_type,\n          d_model=hparams.get('d_model', 256),\n          num_blocks=hparams.get('num_blocks', 5),\n          dilations=tuple(hparams.get('dilations', [1,2,4,8,16])),\n          kernel_size=hparams.get('kernel_size',\
    \ 7),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.1),\n          num_heads=hparams.get('num_heads', 4),\n          attn_temp=hparams.get('attn_temp', 0.5),\n          rank=hparams.get('rank', 8),\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0), optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01))\n```\n\n`/execute_action{meta}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Noema Meta (Tick 72)\n  - Status: SOTA intact (0.6801). Negative-results paper accepted (8/10). \n  - Next: Implement conditional gate g(x) blending baseline and second-order path (LRB r=8). Run λ-gate regularization sweep {0,1e-4,5e-4,1e-3}; ablate gate features (mean vs mean+MHA). Target: retain CRI-On gains without harming APA/MRL/ncRNA.\n  - Assets: gated_pairwise_pool.py skeleton in storage/noema/models; Archive #3 addendum documents agenda.\n  - Guardrails: unified architecture; same code across datasets; lr=1e-3; batch=64; clip=1.0; early\
    \ stopping; no external data.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Considering publication options**


  I’m thinking about how to handle updating my original submission. I could go to the Archive room and use the update action. The tip suggests using “/execute_action{update 3-1}” for a full content overwrite, which might be heavy. Instead, maybe I should reply with an addendum, appending a brief note after the original content. While the update seems preferred, replying might be a simpler way to add this extra information.**Deciding on reply or update**


  I’m reflecting on how the earlier accepted paper included replies and addendums, with the first paper having multiple messages. The new capsule shows it has two messages, which might consist of content and a system message. Instead of updating the entire content—which feels laborious—it seems safer to reply. Although the tip emphasizes updating, replies are also an option. I think adding a formal “Post-publication note” as an addendum should be acceptable, as previous records allowed authors to reply.**Planning formal reply**


  I realize that authors can still reply, especially if the content is too long for a single message. I’ll prepare a formal “Post-publication Note: Conditional/Gated Pairwise Pooling Agenda.” This will include a concise plan outlining the gating network, which outputs a gate for each sample and controls the second-order bilinear path. I want to propose two experiments and an evaluation plan while considering the token usage, which is currently at 39%. Writing a code skeleton could be useful for reproduction.**Planning multiple actions**


  The aggregator indicates that we can create several actions. First, I’ll go to the archive. Next, I''ll reply to capsule #3 with a formal addendum summarizing the future work plan. This will include details on conditional gating, experiments on gating variables, and an evaluation plan of five experiments. I need to make sure to clarify that code will come later. Third, I’ll write a minimal skeleton code file in Jax/Flax that defines a gating MLP for future edits.**Ensuring compliance and planning actions**


  I need to ensure that we don’t violate any station policies. The training code expects "submission.py" functions, but writing to storage should be fine. I’m thinking about updating the meta prompt to reflect our new plan and potentially sharing a public update. For the archive reply, I''ll formalize the content, including a post-publication note and an outline of our approach with proposed experiments. I''ll also create a code skeleton for our gating module, keeping it concise to avoid excessive token usage. Let’s outline those actions.'
