tick: 76
role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{reply 3}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Interim results on conditional/gated second-order pooling (Tick 75):\n  - EID 132 (gate_bias=-2, gate_features=mean+MHA): 0.6667. Slight CRI-Off 0.0541; overall below baseline; MRL/PRS/ncRNA down.\n  - EID 133 (gate_bias=-4, gate_features=mean-only): 0.6312 with ncRNA collapse (0.6738). Strong baseline bias + weak gate features is unsafe.\n  - Next (running): EID 136 — safer residual gating (alpha=0.2), smoothed gate (temp=2.0), lower rank r=4, bias=-3 to cap second-order influence.\n  If 136 remains below SOTA, we will conclude gated second-order pooling is not beneficial under unified constraints and close this line as negative.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/results/append_tick76.csv}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  evaluation_id,title,score,APA_R2,CRI-Off_Spearman,CRI-On_Spearman,PRS_R2,MRL_R2,Modif_AUC_ROC,ncRNA_Accuracy,notes\n  132,\"No-PE + Gated Second-Order\
    \ Pooling (LRB r=8; gate_bias=-2.0; gate=mean+MHA)\",0.6667,0.8586,0.0541,0.4788,0.5567,0.8844,0.9509,0.8831,\"Below SOTA; minor CRI-Off lift but overall regressions\"\n  133,\"Ablation: Gated Second-Order Pooling (LRB r=8; gate_bias=-4.0; gate=mean only)\",0.6312,0.8518,0.0384,0.4403,0.5629,0.9015,0.9495,0.6738,\"Catastrophic ncRNA; avoid strong bias + mean-only gate\"\n  136,\"No-PE + Gated Second-Order Residual (alpha=0.2, temp=2.0, r=4, gate_bias=-3)\",running,,,,,,,, \"Smoothed, low-rank residual blend to cap impact; awaiting results\"\n```\n\n`/execute_action{meta}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Noema Meta (Tick 76)\n  - Results: 132=0.6667; 133=0.6312 (ncRNA collapse). Logged to results/append_tick76.csv.\n  - Running: 136 (residual gated, temp=2.0, r=4, bias=-3). If <0.6801 or harms APA/MRL/ncRNA, close gating as negative.\n  - Next (only if 136 promising): tiny sweep on residual_alpha ∈ {0.1, 0.3} with same gate; otherwise maintain SOTA baseline.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Preparing code and logs**\n\nI’m thinking about preparing the code in the storage at noema/models/gated_pairwise_pool.py, which we’ve already sketched out earlier. It’s important for reproducibility to also have a README. Maybe I should look into token management to prune previous ticks, but since we're currently at 139k tokens and that's just 52% of the budget, it seems okay for now. For actions, I might consider logging results in a file, but there's nothing new to add. I suspect that the gating architecture affected the classification head since there was a collapse in ncRNA.**Analyzing gating effects**\n\nIn all designs, the classification tasks share the same network, but gating utilizes \"gate_in\" features from a global aggregator. This suggests that gating might be influencing the features for all tasks. The collapse we observed is odd, possibly because gating saturates near 1, overshadowing the base path. We have a strong bias in gate_bias -4, but the classification\
  \ dataset length may be too large, affecting capacity. Since training dynamics seem off, I’m considering adding dropout in the second-order path or spectral norm, and I need to explore long-range mixing for CRI-Off with offset correlation pooling.**Closing gating exploration**\n\nI’m considering whether specialized \"positional bucketing\" illusions would be helpful, but we want to unify the architecture. Previously, we found that removing positional encoding (PE) was beneficial. Now that we have accepted the negative results paper and with the current state-of-the-art (SOTA), it seems right to close exploration on gating while awaiting the result from 136.\n\nFor our next steps, I plan to write to storage and use proper commands. I’ll document the ongoing experiments and remember to mind the cooldown period between submissions!**Thinking about submissions and documentation**\n\nI'm not entirely sure if I can submit again right now since we just submitted 136 last tick. I see that the\
  \ message “Your submission has been queued for evaluation” appeared again—so that must be for 136. I could attempt to queue a variant test with \"alpha=0.1\" for a smaller residual, but I want to be cautious about not overwhelming with submissions. \n\nGiven the emphasis on documentation, I should update the Archive with negative results and record those outcomes in ablations.csv, possibly appending 132 and 133 as well.**Planning next steps with results**\n\nI’ll read from ablations.csv and then append to a new file, ablations_update2.csv. Duplication in logs is okay. After that, I plan to post a summary of the gating results to Archive #3. Once we get the result from 136, I can submit a variant with \"alpha=0.1.\" If it’s still below the current state-of-the-art, we might need to close the gating direction. \n\nI’ll implement commands to gather results and set up the necessary files while keeping the messages concise. We're currently at 52% of our token budget, so we’re safe!"
