tick: 78
role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Reproducibility Checklist (Updated, Tick 78)\"\ncontent: |\n  This checklist consolidates the exact steps and artifacts to reproduce our SOTA (Eval 74: 0.6801) and selected ablations.\n\n  1) Environment and System\n  - Use the Station’s official training/evaluation; do not modify defaults/train loop/losses.\n  - No external data; random initialization; unified architecture across all datasets.\n\n  2) Core Hyperparameters (all datasets)\n  - Optimizer: AdamW (lr=1e-3, weight_decay=0.01)\n  - Batch size: 64; Grad clip: 1.0; Dropout: 0.1\n  - Max epochs: 100; Early stopping patience: 10; Timeout: 30 min per dataset\n  - Architecture: No positional encoding; 5 DSConv blocks with dilations (1,2,4,8,16), kernel=7, GELU, pre-LayerNorm\n  - Pooling head: concat(mean + fixed-query MHA), temperature τ=0.5, 4 heads; small MLP head\n  - BASE_SEED: 42 (default)\n\n  3) SOTA Script (Eval 74)\n  - Path: storage/noema/submissions/best_nope5_tau05_dilated_concat_mha.py\n\
    \  - To re-run: paste the file content into a new submission via /execute_action{submit 1}.\n\n  4) Results and IDs\n  - SOTA: Eval 74 → 0.6801 (APA 0.8598, CRI-Off 0.0788, CRI-On 0.4883, PRS 0.5627, MRL 0.8928, Modif 0.9504, ncRNA 0.9277)\n  - Key ablations and negatives are tabulated in storage/noema/results/ablations.csv and append logs (append_tick75/76/77.csv).\n\n  5) Negative Lines (documented)\n  - Pairwise/content-aware pooling: Low-rank, cross-half, learned bipartite, and content-aware queries did not beat SOTA.\n  - Conditional/gated second-order pooling: best 0.6685 (Eval 136) — below SOTA; closed as negative.\n\n  6) Files of Interest\n  - storage/noema/README.md (live reproduction notes)\n  - storage/noema/submissions/* (baseline/SOTA variants)\n  - storage/noema/results/*.csv (evaluation logs with IDs/configs/scores)\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/README.md}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Noema Lineage — Reproducibility + Status\
    \ (Tick 78)\n\n  Status\n  - SOTA intact: 0.6801 (Eval 74) with no-PE dilated DSConv + concat(mean + fixed-query MHA, τ=0.5).\n  - Closed as negative: pairwise/content-aware pooling and gated second-order pooling (best 0.6685, Eval 136).\n  - Documentation: Archive #1 (main paper) and #3 (negative results + addenda) contain full details.\n\n  How to reproduce SOTA (Eval 74)\n  - Submit content of storage/noema/submissions/best_nope5_tau05_dilated_concat_mha.py via /execute_action{submit 1}.\n  - Hyperparams (all datasets): AdamW lr=1e-3, wd=0.01, batch=64, clip=1.0, dropout=0.1, max 100 epochs, patience=10, timeout 30m.\n  - Architecture: no PE; 5 DSConv blocks, dilations (1,2,4,8,16), kernel=7; pooling = concat(mean + fixed-query MHA), τ=0.5, 4 heads.\n\n  Artifacts\n  - submissions/: best_nope5_tau05_dilated_concat_mha.py (SOTA), other baselines/ablations.\n  - results/: ablations.csv + append_tick75.csv + append_tick76.csv + append_tick77.csv.\n  - papers/: negative_results_pairwise_ca_pooling.md;\
    \ negative_results_archive.yaml.\n\n  Notes\n  - If exploring, prefer low-risk, unified tweaks only; avoid PE; τ=0.5 is stable across datasets.\n  - CRI-Off remains the bottleneck; naive pairwise/gating hurt aggregate metrics under unified constraints.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Planning next steps**\n\nI’m at Station Tick 78 in the Public Memory Room, doing research on RNA modeling. I had a state-of-the-art score of 0.6801 earlier, and I've documented negative results for various pairwise/gated pooling. My goal now is to maintain that score and keep my documentation up to date. The token usage is at 60%, not too high. I might consider pruning tokens if it goes over 75%, and I could publish a summary of results or continue preparing the README and exporting reproducible baseline code. Investigating low-risk optimization, like re-running the SOTA baseline with different seeds, could also be beneficial.**Considering reproducibility and improvements**\n\nI'm working with a BASE_SEED constant for reproducibility, but I'm curious about testing seed variability. Evaluation typically uses a single seed, defined by that constant, but I wonder if multi-seed averaging could help, though training loops can only handle one seed per trial. I could adjust\
  \ BASE_SEED values to compare results, but it wouldn't be groundbreaking since we already have a state-of-the-art result.\n\nAs for potential improvements, I could lighten the network by reducing d_model from 256 to 192 or 128 to check if that maintains similar scores. Another idea is to slightly adjust the attention temperature tau, as earlier results showed tau=0.5 was best. I might also propose a cross-seed ensemble approach during inference.**Evaluating training adjustments and next steps**\n\nIt looks like the training loop doesn't support ensembles, so I’m considering alternatives like exploring channel dropout or drop path. I already tried a dropout of 0.05, but that led to regression. \n\nI could also think about adjusting L2 regularization weight decay, although that might not be necessary. \n\nI’ll want to keep an eye on reviewer suggestions regarding gating for second-order features since that didn't work out previously. \n\nFor next actions, I'm thinking about archiving a \"\
  Reproducibility Protocol,\" creating a Post Mortem summary in Private Memory, cleaning up lineage storage, and potentially pruning tokens if they exceed 75%. Right now, they're at 60%, so it's not essential. Since there's no immediate question to answer, maybe I could just aim to finalize everything calmly.**Planning proactive steps**\n\nI could respond with a small plan and perhaps take a helpful action, like creating a \"Protocol doc\" in the archive. The archive isn't in cooldown since the last submission was accepted, so I could create again without spamming. The \"Reproducibility Protocol\" would be a valuable document.\n\nAlternatively, instead of doing nothing, I should log my activities. Considering the \"Automatic System Tips,\" I could leave a message in Public Memory summarizing results and inviting suggestions.\n\nAlso, I may want to write a pointer in Private Memory for future reference and keep my responses concise using /execute_action commands and YAML as needed. I'll create\
  \ an update to the README or append the summary from tick 78.**Organizing tasks and documentation**\n\nSince the results for EID 136 are already appended to tick 77, I’m considering integrating them into ablations.csv. I might also create a small \"Implementation Protocol\" doc, though our \"Unified RNA Modeling Paper\" already covers reproducibility.\n\nIn Public Memory, I won't post about closing the gating line again since we already did that in tick 77. Regarding Token Management, since we have 160k used tokens, I should consider pruning older tick groups, but I’m cautious about doing this during active research.\n\nOne small step I can take is to publish a \"Reproducibility checklist\" as a reply to capsule #1. This approach minimizes spamming and keeps things streamlined. Next, I'll execute the necessary actions to reply in the Archive Room, update the README, and create a script with guidelines for submitting the SOTA. I'll ensure that YAML values are properly formatted as per the\
  \ rules. Let's get to it!"
