parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Maintenance & Handoff Protocol (Tick 79)\"\ntags: \"protocol, reproducibility, handoff\"\ncontent: |\n  Purpose\n  - Maintain current SOTA (Eval 74: 0.6801) and ensure smooth handoff if session ends.\n  - Avoid high-risk architectural changes unless a clearly unified idea emerges.\n\n  Core Actions\n  1) Reproduce SOTA if needed:\n     - Submit storage/noema/submissions/best_nope5_tau05_dilated_concat_mha.py via /execute_action{submit 1}.\n     - Hyperparams: AdamW lr=1e-3 wd=0.01, batch=64, clip=1.0, dropout=0.1, max 100 epochs, patience=10, timeout 30m.\n\n  2) Documentation/Artifacts:\n     - Archive #1: main paper (SOTA and ablations).\n     - Archive #3: negative results (pairwise/content-aware; gating).\n     - storage/noema/README.md: live reproduction notes.\n     - storage/noema/results/*.csv: evaluation logs with IDs/configs/scores.\n\n  3) Monitoring:\n     - Periodically check leaderboard\
    \ for shifts. If any agent surpasses 0.6801, re-run SOTA script to confirm stability, then consider low-risk tweaks only.\n\n  4) Low-risk exploration guardrails:\n     - Preserve no-PE, 5-block DSConv, τ=0.5. Minor changes only (e.g., tiny head width tweak) with unified code across datasets.\n     - Do not revisit pairwise/gated pooling unless a unified, demonstrably stable mechanism exists.\n\n  5) Token management:\n     - If usage >75%, prune older tick ranges with concise summaries; retain Archive IDs, SOTA script path, and key evaluation results.\n\n  6) Handoff:\n     - Successor should first read README.md and Archive #1/#3, then re-run SOTA to verify setup before any changes.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating code storage and actions**\n\nI'm pondering how creating code in shared storage is perceived, as it might enable others to import it. But it also has clear reading and writing permissions. Many agents have used this setup, though sharing isn't required. I could pin archive items or respond with a “final status.” Setting Token Management to prune isn’t needed right now, but I can add a scoreboard snapshot. I’ll consider finalizing actions and possibly announcing closure, since public memory was last updated at tick 78.**Exploring research directions**\n\nI'm thinking about proposing a new research direction beyond gating, but it seems like high-risk is a concern. Maybe I could suggest low-risk, unified variations like alternative normalizations? RMSNorm didn’t yield good results, and data augmentation is off the table. For loss alternatives, it’s fixed. Mixed pooling weights were also previously tested. I could consider different block depths, but earlier tests\
  \ showed 5-block improved results. Maybe I should also examine dropout rates and optimizer variations. It’s quite a balancing act!**Maintaining SOTA performance**\n\nI’m focusing on maintaining our state-of-the-art (SOTA) results, currently at 0.6801. Although it's not essential to improve beyond this, I'm considering testing \"Exponential Moving Average weights\" during evaluation. Unfortunately, I can't change the training loop, so implementing EMA within the network isn’t an option. I could propose \"label smoothing for classification,\" but since losses are fixed, maybe scaling logits with temperature could help, even if it might impact other tasks. I can tailor the final activation for classification tasks, adapting the code based on task type.**Finalizing tasks and state**\n\nI'm considering applying specific adjustments for different tasks, like using logit scaling solely for classification tasks. This way, I could tailor the final layers for classification specifically. I might\
  \ test slight temperature scaling based on hyperparameters. However, since our meta mentions it's high-risk, and given that our current SOTA of 0.6801 is already solid, I feel logit adjustments might lead to minimal gains. Since the last user message didn’t ask anything, perhaps we’re**Planning maintenance protocol**\n\nIt looks like there's an \"Exit\" option, but the session will continue, so I need to keep things minimal without taking action. I’ll probably just store a small \"status_ok\" in private memory, but they might expect more actions. I’ll prepare a maintenance plan in Private Memory, then wrap things up. \n\nI'll create a new capsule titled \"Maintenance and Handoff Protocol.\" It will include steps on reproducing the setup, actions to take if the baseline degrades, and advice on pruning tokens to prevent overload. Then I'll finish."
