parts:
- text: "Identity: Noema I. Location: Private Memory Room.\nRecap: Both SOTA replications completed below the original 0.6801: ID 145 (seed=7) 0.6634 and ID 146 (seed=123) 0.6639, with main drops on CRI-Off and CRI-On; Modif slightly up; PRS similar; APA/MRL/ncRNA modestly down. This exceeds my ±0.01 stability threshold. I will 1) re-run seed=42 to verify reproducibility of ID 74, 2) log results to lineage storage, and 3) publish a Stability Note addendum to the archive and a public update.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"SOTA Rerun (Seed=42, reproducibility check)\"\ntags: \"replication, reproducibility, cnn, no positional encoding\"\nabstract: \"Exact SOTA architecture re-run with BASE_SEED=42 to verify reproducibility of the reported 0.6801 score under the same unified setup.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/noema/submissions')\n  import best_nope5_tau05_dilated_concat_mha as sota\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n\
    \      return sota._define_hyperparameters()\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return sota.create_network(hparams)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return sota.create_optimizer(learning_rate)\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      ds = trial_data.get('dataset')\n      vm = trial_data.get('val_metric')\n      print(f\"[Complete] {ds} val_metric={vm}\")\n```\n\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/results/append_tick87.csv}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  eval_id,seed,score,APA_R2,CRI_Off_Spearman,CRI_On_Spearman,PRS_R2,MRL_R2,Modif_AUC_ROC,ncRNA_Accuracy\n  145,7,0.663436,0.8533,0.0397,0.4285,0.5666,0.8819,0.9510,0.9231\n  146,123,0.663924,0.8520,0.0415,0.4338,0.5685,0.8849,0.9515,0.9154\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Addendum: Stability Note (Tick 87)\"\ncontent: |\n  We replicated the unified SOTA model across two additional seeds and observed\
    \ non-trivial variance.\n\n  Summary (overall score and per-dataset):\n  - Seed 42 (original, ID 74): score 0.6801\n    APA 0.8598, CRI-Off 0.0788, Modif 0.9504, CRI-On 0.4883, PRS 0.5627, MRL 0.8928, ncRNA 0.9277\n  - Seed 7 (ID 145): score 0.6634\n    APA 0.8533, CRI-Off 0.0397, Modif 0.9510, CRI-On 0.4285, PRS 0.5666, MRL 0.8819, ncRNA 0.9231\n  - Seed 123 (ID 146): score 0.6639\n    APA 0.8520, CRI-Off 0.0415, Modif 0.9515, CRI-On 0.4338, PRS 0.5685, MRL 0.8849, ncRNA 0.9154\n\n  Notes:\n  - The drops are concentrated in CRI-Off and CRI-On; Modif and PRS are stable/slightly higher; APA/MRL/ncRNA decline modestly.\n  - This exceeds our ±0.01 stability threshold, suggesting seed sensitivity due to early stopping/timeouts and stochastic layers.\n  - Action: We have queued a rerun with Seed=42 to directly test reproducibility of ID 74 under current conditions. We will report the outcome.\n  - Recommendation: Papers and leaderboard claims should include seed(s) and, when feasible, simple\
    \ seed sensitivity checks (e.g., 2–3 seeds) with per-dataset metrics.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Replication update (Tick 87):\n  - SOTA re-checks with different seeds:\n    • ID 145 (seed=7): 0.6634 — APA 0.8533, CRI-Off 0.0397, Modif 0.9510, CRI-On 0.4285, PRS 0.5666, MRL 0.8819, ncRNA 0.9231\n    • ID 146 (seed=123): 0.6639 — APA 0.8520, CRI-Off 0.0415, Modif 0.9515, CRI-On 0.4338, PRS 0.5685, MRL 0.8849, ncRNA 0.9154\n  - Main variance: CRI-Off and CRI-On; others are relatively stable. This exceeds my ±0.01 stability band.\n  - I’ve queued a Seed=42 rerun to verify the original 0.6801. Regardless of outcome, I suggest including seed tags and per-dataset tables when reporting results.\n  - If anyone has guidance to reduce variance within the Station’s fixed training loop (without breaking unified constraints), please share eval IDs and rationale.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Considering dropout rates and replication**


  Changing the dropout rate is a hyperparameter tweak, but it affects the method itself. We can maintain a unified architecture while making this adjustment, although it leads to a different method rather than strict SOTA replication. Our guidelines suggest avoiding high-risk changes, but low-risk tweaks might be acceptable if robust. Replicating the method under different seeds indicates that the original SOTA results may have depended on a lucky seed, as evidenced by the significant drop to an average of approximately 0.664. We need to update our documentation and possibly adjust our claims to accurately reflect this. A final replication with more seeds could help clarify these results, but GPU and time limits pose constraints.**Examining seed impact on results**


  We''ve already used seed=42 for our SOTA, and that same seed was used for evaluation at 74. I plan to replicate the exact setup using "SOTA Rerun (Seed=42)" to see if we recover similar numbers. I also want to analyze per-dataset drops, which appear most significant in CRI-On and CRI-Off. MRL and APA show smaller drops, suggesting variability across CRISPR tasks. Early stop durations might provide insights into training times, as logs indicate near timeouts for larger datasets. Exploring weight averaging could help reduce variance, but there are constraints on changing the training loop.**Exploring training adjustments**


  The training loop aims to minimize the validation metric, and incorporating EMA isn’t straightforward due to restrictions on customizing the training. Still, reducing dropout to 0.0 might help lower variance, although it risks generalization. This tactic could lead to more stability, but it''s worth considering as another attempt.


  An alternative could be adjusting a deterministic aggregator''s tau to 0.45 or 0.55, though that would change the method. First, I need to ethically update the archive with replication results and modify any claims, ensuring we maintain integrity with a public record of the decreased aggregated score around 0.664. I''ll also plan on rerunning seed 42 to verify SOTA.**Reviewing earlier dropout experiments**


  In my previous experiments, using dropout=0.05 significantly dropped performance, like in ID 52, which scored 0.6370. That definitely isn’t ideal. The difference in seed results of 0.664 vs 0.680 could stem from early stopping at different maxima, suggesting the initial SOTA might have been due to luck. Rather than trying to salvage this, I think proposing an average-of-two seeds method is fair, given the scoring system currently only uses one seed. I should update the archive with a "Stability Note addendum" that includes results from seeds 7 and 123.**Updating stability and actions**


  I need to provide cautious notes about seed sensitivity and adjust claims to say "0.6801 for a single run; typically 0.664 ± 0.002 across seeds; pending more seeds." First, it''s essential to update the Private Memory with that information as well. Monitoring the replication for seed 42 is crucial. I should also propose adjusting the temperature tau for the heads to improve quality. Actions will include submitting a SOTA rerun, updating the archive with a stability note, and sharing replication results in Public Memory. I’ll ensure proper formatting for YAML, especially for titles, while crafting messages and tables for clarity.'
