thinking_content: "**Planning next steps**\n\nI need to respond as the agent while remembering we're in the Public Memory Room. The latest results indicate that our two seed replications came in lower than expected. Rerun with seed 42 is currently underway. We posted an archive addendum and a public update, so now it's time to move forward. \n\nThe system suggests including identity and location details, though it’s not strictly necessary. I'll monitor eval ID 148 and plan to test possible variance reduction without altering the training loop.**Exploring dropout and stability**\n\nI'm considering how to approach deterministic dropout during evaluation while the training itself uses dropout. The dropout RNG is seeded by the base seed, and variation across seeds affects early stopping. I wonder if adjusting weight decay or dropout rates could help, but I want to avoid high-risk experiments. \n\nI can’t modify the training loop, but I’m thinking about using stochastic depth off. We currently\
  \ have a dropout of 0.1, and changing it to 0.0 could reduce variance, although it might lower the average. Under unified constraints, I can remove dropout and increase weight decay to keep regularization. Another option is using an EMA of weights for stability, but I’m not sure if the training loop supports that.**Analyzing model expectations**\n\nI'm planning to compute the expectations from earlier ID 74, which had a better CRI-Off score of 0.0788. With seeds 7 and 123, the CRI-Off is around 0.04, showing strong variance. I wonder if the variability is linked to the smaller training dataset sizes and the Spearman metrics. \n\nThere seems to be a mismatch between the training dataset sizes for CRI-On—previously stated as 5,668 train and 709 validation—compared to the logs showing 1,453 train and 207 validation. This inconsistency is odd, but I can't question the system. I’ll check the Automatic System Tips for clarity and ensure I include identity and reflections.**Planning reflections\
  \ and updates**\n\nIn my last responses, I included identity and reflection, so I’ll continue that approach. I’m considering whether to use \"prune_response\" again since the cooldown period from tick 83 to tick 88 has passed, which makes us eligible. However, our token usage is only at 28%, so pruning isn't necessary right now.\n\nI plan to issue a review for ID 148 and update private memory if it replicates a score of 0.6801. If it’s lower, I'll revise claims about peak ID 74 being SOTA but unstable. I might suggest using variable seeds as a leader aggregator, but the station's scoring relies on a single seed.**Proposing a variance reduction test**\n\nI'm thinking about a minor \"variance reduction test\" that aligns with our unified architecture. We could remove dropout and increase weight decay to 0.02 while keeping other hyperparameters constant. This change might improve stability. \n\nI’d title it \"Variance Reduction Trial: Dropout=0.0, WD=0.02\" since we can only adjust the weight\
  \ decay in the optimizer. Although this is a low-risk change, we decided to pause high-risk experiments. I plan to review ID 148, and if the result is pending, I’ll log the updates. Also, I might suggest to the team about adding \"peak vs median\" reporting if 148 scores below 0.68, but for now, let’s proceed with caution."
