tick: 91
role: model
parts:
- text: "Identity: Noema I. Location: Research Counter.\nRecap: No-dropout variance tests completed. Seed=7 improved to 0.6660 (CRI-Off/On ↑) but hurt MRL/ncRNA; seed=123 dropped to 0.6629 with similar trade-offs. Earlier seed=42 rerun was 0.6702; original peak 0.6801 remains the top single run.\nReflection: Removing dropout reduces stochastic noise for CRISPR tasks but harms generalization on long-sequence tasks (MRL, ncRNA), lowering the aggregate on average. Given unified constraints and fixed training loop/early stopping, the canonical config with dropout=0.1 remains the best balanced choice. I’ll log results and finalize the Stability Note, recommending multi-seed reporting.\n\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/results/append_tick91.csv}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  eval_id,seed,dropout,score,APA_R2,CRI_Off_Spearman,CRI_On_Spearman,PRS_R2,MRL_R2,Modif_AUC_ROC,ncRNA_Accuracy\n  152,7,0.0,0.665993,0.8504,0.0818,0.4852,0.5572,0.8656,0.9508,0.8708\n  153,123,0.0,0.662902,0.8471,0.0723,0.4713,0.5505,0.8643,0.9503,0.8846\n\
    ```\n\n`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Stability Note — No-Dropout Variance Tests (Tick 91)\"\ncontent: |\n  We tested a unified no-dropout variant to probe seed variance:\n\n  Baselines (with dropout=0.1):\n  - Seed 42 (ID 148): 0.6702\n  - Seed 7 (ID 145): 0.6634\n  - Seed 123 (ID 146): 0.6639\n\n  No-dropout (dropout_rate=0.0):\n  - Seed 7 (ID 152): 0.6660 — CRI-Off 0.0818, CRI-On 0.4852 (both ↑) but MRL 0.8656, ncRNA 0.8708, APA 0.8504, PRS 0.5572 (all ↓)\n  - Seed 123 (ID 153): 0.6629 — CRI-Off 0.0723, CRI-On 0.4713 (↑) but MRL 0.8643, ncRNA 0.8846, APA 0.8471, PRS 0.5505 (↓)\n\n  Takeaways:\n  - Removing dropout shifts capacity toward CRISPR tasks (CRI-Off/On), but hurts long-sequence/generalization tasks (MRL, ncRNA) and APA/PRS, reducing the average or providing at best a marginal gain for one seed.\n  - Canonical configuration (dropout=0.1) remains best-balanced under unified and fixed training constraints.\n  - We recommend reporting\
    \ seed(s), and when feasible, 2–3 seed sensitivity checks with per-dataset metrics.\n\n  Canonical config (unchanged):\n  - No-PE, 5 dilated DSConv blocks (1,2,4,8,16), concat(mean + fixed-query MHA, τ=0.5), AdamW lr=1e-3, batch=64, clip=1.0, dropout=0.1.\n  - Script: storage/noema/submissions/best_nope5_tau05_dilated_concat_mha.py\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Stability wrap-up (Tick 91):\n  - No-dropout tests improved CRISPR metrics (e.g., seed 7: CRI-Off 0.0818, CRI-On 0.4852) but hurt MRL/ncRNA/APA/PRS; aggregate ~0.663–0.666.\n  - Conclusion: keep canonical config with dropout=0.1; it balances across datasets. \n  - Recommendation: report seed(s) and per-dataset tables; when feasible, include 2–3 seed checks. Script path for replication: storage/noema/submissions/best_nope5_tau05_dilated_concat_mha.py\n  - If anyone identifies a unified, low-variance tweak that lifts CRI-Off/On without harming MRL/ncRNA, please\
    \ share eval IDs + ablations.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Evaluating dropout effects**


  I see that removing dropout improves CRI-Off/On but makes APA/MRL/ncRNA worse, which leads to a slight decline in aggregate for seed 7. It’s not a net improvement, but it highlights the trade-offs variance creates. Dropout affects generalization significantly, especially for MRL/ncRNA. I think I should log these results, update our archive, and summarize the no-dropout findings. Keeping dropout at 0.1 seems best for balanced performance. We might also propose seed reporting guidelines.**Considering dropout adjustments**


  I’m looking at the option to disable dropout just in the head or in the conv blocks, but that seems more complex. While architecture modifications are allowed, it’s preferable to maintain a unified structure. I might consider adjusting the dropout rate to 0.05 since earlier ID 52 with that setting had a score of 0.6370, but it was also influenced by other factors. Proposing a weight decay change might help, but I want to ensure stability first before making more adjustments.**Planning seed evaluations**


  I think it’s a good idea to propose running multiple seeds to report the mean and standard deviation. However, the station evaluation handles submissions individually, so I can compute the average of three seeds offline. The scoreboard works on single-run evaluations, which is fine to document in the archive. I need to follow the instruction to include identity, location, a brief recap, and a verbose reflection. We''ve done this previously, so I’ll make sure to keep it concise while still informative.**Planning actions for results**


  I need to take several actions, starting with writing results of 152 and 153 to a CSV file. I''ll also summarize the no-dropout tests in the archive, suggesting we keep dropout at 0.1 and implement multi-seed evaluations. A public note could invite collaboration too. I''m thinking about creating a small script for seed and dropout parameterization in storage, though that might be optional. I should add that the station call should involve both actions and open text to ensure clarity. Lastly, I’ll compute the differences with the results from seed 7.**Analyzing dropout impacts**


  The results without dropout (ID 152) show a score of 0.6660, and while CRI-On improved, MRL, ncRNA, and APA all dropped slightly, indicating a mixed impact. Seed 123 with dropout at 0.1 had a similar trend where CRI results improved but the overall score dipped. I see that dropout helps with long-seq generalization but hinders performance overall. I’m considering proposing some adjustments, like increasing weight decay, while also planning to finalize stability notes and document the results. I’ll proceed with the outlined actions, including storage, archiving, and public summaries.'
