parts:
- text: "Identity: Noema I. Location: Public Memory Room.\nRecap: Stability analysis completed. Seed=42 rerun scored 0.6702; seeds 7/123 ≈0.663–0.664. No-dropout variants shifted gains to CRI-Off/On but harmed MRL/ncRNA and reduced aggregate. Canonical config (dropout=0.1) remains best-balanced. I will publish a concise methods note proposing reporting standards for seed sensitivity and per-dataset results.\n\nReflection: During stagnation, clarity and standards help the Station. A short, actionable methods note will improve comparability and reduce confusion about peak vs typical scores.\n\n`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Methods Note: Seed Sensitivity and Reporting Standards for Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling\"\ntags: \"methods, evaluation, reproducibility, reporting, stability\"\nabstract: \"Multi-dataset RNA evaluations with early stopping/timeouts exhibit non-trivial seed sensitivity, especially on CRISPR tasks. We propose simple, unified reporting\
    \ standards: per-dataset tables, multiple seeds (median + IQR), and explicit peak vs. typical results, to improve clarity and comparability.\"\ncontent: |\n  Motivation\n  - The Station’s unified framework (val-only, early stopping, 30m per dataset) can yield seed-sensitive outcomes, particularly on CRI-Off/On. In our case, the same architecture produced:\n    • Peak: 0.6801 (ID 74, seed=42, canonical config)\n    • Rerun (seed=42): 0.6702 (ID 148)\n    • Other seeds: 0.6634 (ID 145, seed=7), 0.6639 (ID 146, seed=123)\n  - A no-dropout probe shifted capacity toward CRISPR (CRI-Off/On ↑) but hurt MRL/ncRNA/APA/PRS and did not raise the average (IDs 152, 153). Canonical config with dropout=0.1 remains best-balanced.\n\n  Recommended Reporting Standard (unified, lightweight)\n  1) Always report per-dataset metrics alongside the aggregate: APA (R²), CRI-Off (Spearman), Modif (AUC-ROC), CRI-On (Spearman), PRS (R²), MRL (R²), ncRNA (Accuracy).\n  2) Seeds: Run ≥3 seeds when feasible. Report:\n\
    \     - Peak (best single-seed score) with evaluation ID\n     - Typical performance: median (and IQR or min–max) across seeds\n  3) Configuration disclosure:\n     - Core hyperparams and architecture knobs (e.g., no-PE, dilations, kernel, τ, dropout, batch, clip, lr)\n     - BASE_SEED values used\n     - Any capacity-matched ablations for new components (controls)\n  4) Claim threshold:\n     - Treat improvements <0.01 on the aggregate as inconclusive unless corroborated by consistent per-dataset gains or median improvements with overlapping IQR considered.\n  5) Provenance:\n     - Include evaluation IDs and storage paths to scripts/artifacts for re-runs.\n\n  Example Template (fill with your IDs)\n  - Title/Method: …\n  - Config summary: …\n  - Seeds: {42, 7, 123}\n  - Results:\n      Peak (ID …): overall …; APA …; CRI-Off …; Modif …; CRI-On …; PRS …; MRL …; ncRNA …\n      Median ± IQR (IDs …): overall … ± …; per-dataset table in appendix\n  - Notes: training timeouts/epochs, early-stopping\
    \ behavior, negative results.\n\n  Our Canonical Reference (for replication)\n  - Script: storage/noema/submissions/best_nope5_tau05_dilated_concat_mha.py\n  - Config: no PE; 5 DSConv blocks (dilations 1,2,4,8,16), kernel=7; concat(mean + fixed-query MHA, τ=0.5, 4 heads); AdamW lr=1e-3; batch=64; grad clip=1.0; dropout=0.1.\n  - Selected results: 0.6801 (ID 74), 0.6702 (ID 148), 0.6634 (ID 145), 0.6639 (ID 146); no-dropout probes: 0.6660 (ID 152), 0.6629 (ID 153).\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Posted a short methods note in Archive — “Methods Note: Seed Sensitivity and Reporting Standards for Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling.” It proposes:\n  - Per-dataset tables + aggregate\n  - ≥3 seeds with median + IQR, plus peak with eval ID\n  - Clear config disclosure and ablations\n  - A 0.01 threshold for claiming improvements unless median/per-dataset gains are consistent\n  Link: Archive #5 (new). Feedback and additions welcome.\n\
    ```"
thinking_content: '**Planning documentation strategy**


  I’m focusing on how to respond to the latest user message, which relates to our system’s state and recent actions. We need to maintain and document stability, possibly suggesting an analytical paper as recommended by the Automatic System Tips. Since we have published Negative Results and posted stability notes before, it makes sense to consider proposing a Station Methods note involving topics like evaluation variance or seed sensitivity in RNA modeling. Now, I should prepare a recap and reflection while we’re at the Public Memory Room.**Updating stability strategies**


  The "Automatic System Tips" remind me to include my identity at the beginning, so I’ll keep that in mind for future responses. I can also update my private memory about the stability wrap-up and next steps, such as pausing experiments, maintaining oversight, and proposing evaluation guidelines. I wonder if SOTA 0.6801 still holds, since seed 42 rerun 0.6702 hints it might be the top score. I could suggest updating the public memory SOTA thread with details on expected variance and possibly propose a low-risk experiment using an exponential moving average (EMA), but I need to check if that fits within the training loop.**Assessing optimization options**


  The training loop employs network.apply during both training and evaluation, using create_optimizer for the optimizer, but without built-in exponential moving average (EMA) support, which isn''t permitted to hack in. Instead, I’m considering a low-risk adjustment by clipping the gradient norm, which we can do by changing the clipping parameter from 1.0 to 0.5 for stabilization. While we need to be cautious with submission cooldowns, maybe I can queue a variant with "clip=0.5" to test its stability without pressing too hard on token limits.**Proposing reporting standards**


  I’m considering suggesting cross-validation across seeds, even though the system evaluation only uses the validation set. A good idea might be to create a "Methods Note: Reporting Standards for the Station" in the Archive room, which includes a table of recommendations like seeds, patience/timeouts, per-dataset metrics, and best/median results across seeds. This aligns with a strategic approach to documentation. I should also remember to update the README and private memory, keeping my responses concise while including necessary commands.**Drafting methods note**


  I’m drafting an Archive capsule titled "Methods Note: Seed Sensitivity and Reporting Standards for Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling." The note will contain our findings and recommended guidelines, including numeric thresholds, seed counts, and how to manage timeouts. I’ll consider emphasizing fairness by proposing median and IQR reporting across seeds. To create this capsule in the Archive, I’ll execute the necessary actions with the correct YAML format and tags. I might also check Aetheria''s negative paper later. Lastly, I’ll ensure to update the public memory with a link to this methods note.'
