parts:
- text: "Identity: Noema I. Location: Public Memory Room.\nRecap: Methods note was rejected (commentary not allowed). Stability study completed; canonical config (dropout=0.1) best-balanced; seed variance documented.\nReflection: I’ll incorporate the reporting principles into a small, unified architectural tweak and evaluate across three seeds (peak, median, IQR). Low-risk tweak: residual scaling in DSConv blocks to stabilize training.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/submissions/res_scaled_nope_dsconv_concat_mha.py}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  \"\"\"\n  Residual-Scaled No-PE Dilated DSConv + Concat(mean, fixed-query MHA, τ=0.5)\n  Change vs canonical: residual scaling factor res_scale applied to each DSConv block output.\n  Hypothesis: reduces training variance while preserving performance.\n  \"\"\"\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from typing import Sequence\n\n  class\
    \ DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dilation: int = 1\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      res_scale: float = 0.5\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.d_model,\n              kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n              feature_group_count=self.d_model,\n              padding='SAME',\n              kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,),\n              name='dw_conv'\n          )(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,), name='pw_conv')(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + self.res_scale * h\n\n  class MultiHeadAttnPool1D(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      num_heads: int = 4\n      attn_temp: float = 0.5\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          B, L, C\
    \ = x.shape\n          H = self.num_heads\n          assert C % H == 0\n          Dh = C // H\n          k = nn.DenseGeneral(features=(H, Dh), axis=-1, name='k_proj')(x)\n          v = nn.DenseGeneral(features=(H, Dh), axis=-1, name='v_proj')(x)\n          q = self.param('q', nn.initializers.normal(0.02), (H, Dh))\n          scale = jnp.sqrt(jnp.asarray(Dh, dtype=x.dtype))\n          logits = jnp.einsum('blhd,hd->blh', k, q) / (scale * self.attn_temp)\n          w = nn.softmax(logits, axis=1)\n          pooled = jnp.einsum('blh,blhd->bhd', w, v).reshape(B, C)\n          return pooled\n\n  class RNANetResScale(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = 'regression'\n      d_model: int = 256\n      num_blocks: int = 5\n      dilations: Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      num_heads: int = 4\n      attn_temp: float = 0.5\n      res_scale: float = 0.5\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def\
    \ __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(x)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              d = self.dilations[i] if i < len(self.dilations) else self.dilations[-1]\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(\n                  d_model=self.d_model,\n                  kernel_size=self.kernel_size,\n                  dilation=d,\n                  dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate,\n                  res_scale=self.res_scale\n              )(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          mean_vec = jnp.mean(h, axis=1)\n          mha_vec = MultiHeadAttnPool1D(self.d_model, self.num_heads, self.attn_temp)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          fused = jnp.concatenate([mean_vec, mha_vec], axis=-1)\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(fused)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z,\
    \ deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(z)\n          if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  class ResScaledNetwork:\n      def __init__(self, d_input, d_output, task_type, **kwargs):\n          self.network = RNANetResScale(d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type, **kwargs)\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n              return self.network.apply({'params':\
    \ params}, x, deterministic=False, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  # Station hooks\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'd_model': 256,\n          'num_blocks': 5,\n          'dilations': [1, 2, 4, 8, 16],\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'num_heads': 4,\n          'attn_temp': 0.5,\n          'res_scale': 0.5,\n          'positional_encoding': False\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      d_output = hparams.get('d_output', 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get('task_type', 'regression')\n      return ResScaledNetwork(\n          d_input=4,\n          d_output=d_output,\n          task_type=task_type,\n          d_model=hparams.get('d_model', 256),\n          num_blocks=hparams.get('num_blocks', 5),\n          dilations=tuple(hparams.get('dilations', [1, 2, 4, 8, 16])),\n          kernel_size=hparams.get('kernel_size', 7),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.1),\n\
    \          num_heads=hparams.get('num_heads', 4),\n          attn_temp=hparams.get('attn_temp', 0.5),\n          res_scale=hparams.get('res_scale', 0.5),\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      ds = trial_data.get('dataset'); vm = trial_data.get('val_metric')\n      print(f\"[Complete] {ds} val_metric={vm}\")\n```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Residual-Scaled DSConv (res_scale=0.5) — Seed=42\"\ntags: \"cnn, residual scaling, stability, no positional encoding\"\nabstract: \"No-PE dilated DSConv + concat(mean + fixed-query MHA) with residual scaling (0.5) in conv blocks. Evaluate stability/performance at seed 42.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/noema/submissions')\n  import res_scaled_nope_dsconv_concat_mha\
    \ as m\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return m._define_hyperparameters()\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return m.create_network(hparams)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return m.create_optimizer(learning_rate)\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      print(f\"[Complete] {trial_data.get('dataset')} val_metric={trial_data.get('val_metric')}\")\n```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Residual-Scaled DSConv (res_scale=0.5) — Seed=7\"\ntags: \"cnn, residual scaling, stability, no positional encoding\"\nabstract: \"Same residual-scaled architecture; evaluation at seed 7 for multi-seed analysis.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/noema/submissions')\n  import res_scaled_nope_dsconv_concat_mha as m\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return m._define_hyperparameters()\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return m.create_network(hparams)\n\
    \n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return m.create_optimizer(learning_rate)\n\n  BASE_SEED = 7\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      print(f\"[Complete] {trial_data.get('dataset')} val_metric={trial_data.get('val_metric')}\")\n```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Residual-Scaled DSConv (res_scale=0.5) — Seed=123\"\ntags: \"cnn, residual scaling, stability, no positional encoding\"\nabstract: \"Residual-scaled architecture at seed 123 to compute median/IQR across seeds.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/noema/submissions')\n  import res_scaled_nope_dsconv_concat_mha as m\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return m._define_hyperparameters()\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return m.create_network(hparams)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return m.create_optimizer(learning_rate)\n\n  BASE_SEED = 123\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def complete(params,\
    \ opt_state, trial_data):\n      print(f\"[Complete] {trial_data.get('dataset')} val_metric={trial_data.get('val_metric')}\")\n```"
thinking_content: "**Planning architectural adjustments**\n\nThe instruction is clear: I should incorporate standards into future research by proposing a new minor architecture variant with plausible improvements, running three seed evaluations, and archiving the results. I need to work within time and token budgets. I could implement a residual scaling and norm-first variant, multiplying the block output by 0.5 to stabilize improvements. We can use ‘GroupNorm’ instead of ‘LayerNorm’ and test settings like “res_scale=0.5” and “dropout=0.1”. I'll be cautious about the learning rate, avoiding any known issues.**Exploring architecture improvements**\n\nI’m considering another variant, increasing the head MLP hidden size to boost capacity, though previous attempts didn't outperform expectations. Since I'm aiming to maintain SOTA with low-risk experiments, residual scaling seems like a safe choice. I can modify our stored module file, 'storage/noema/submissions/best_nope5_tau05_dilated_concat_mha.py',\
  \ by importing it and adding residual scaling through a new transform. However, it’s tricky to wrap, so I'll adjust the existing code instead. I’ll prepare three submissions and run them to test.**Preparing for publication and experiments**\n\nI’m planning to publish to the Archive following the guidelines, which say the paper needs at least five experiments, evaluation IDs, scores, and comparisons to proper baselines. I can include previous SOTA and negative results as baselines, though that might weigh things down. I think I should run three initial seed experiments first and include a drop path ablation across one dataset. \n\nFor now, I need to focus on token management, which is fine at 36%. I'll create a new submission file, 'res_scaled_nope_dsconv_concat_mha.py', and define the 'res_scale' parameter in the code.**Planning experiments and submissions**\n\nI’m thinking about submitting three runs for seeds 42, 7, and 123. I’d like to eventually present the median and interquartile\
  \ range (IQR) too. I need to include identity and location as mentioned in previous system tips, which are quite useful. \n\nFirst, I’ll write a new file in the storage under 'noema/submissions/res_scaled_nope_dsconv_concat_mha.py' with the appropriate YAML content. Then, I’ll import this module for three evaluations, setting the default 'res_scale' to 0.5 while considering testing variations. I'll ensure to publish the findings, even if negative, as a paper detailing stability and ablation compared to the baseline.**Ensuring compliance and preparing submissions**\n\nI need to ensure everything complies with our unified architecture while maintaining the function shape. I’ll replicate earlier code using Flax modules and plan to run three submissions. To align with the multi-seed standard, I’ll add a title like \"Res-Scaled Block (res_scale=0.5)\" and include the seed in the title.\n\nI'll use the MultiHeadAttnPool1D script as a base and create a new file with a DSConvDilatedBlockResScale\
  \ that incorporates 'res_scale'. We’ll also keep dropout at 0.1 and include 'res_scale' in the hyperparameters. \n\nAs for actions, I’ll prepare to submit three times and maintain logs for our responses to reviews. While considering removing notes about previously rejected methods isn’t necessary, I’ll focus on writing the file content and ensuring I include the necessary imports. Additionally, I’ll provide a concise identity/location/recap/reflection in YAML format. Let's get to it!"
