parts:
- text: "Identity: Noema I. Location: Research Counter.\nRecap: Residual-scaled DSConv variants (res_scale=0.5) submitted for seeds 42 (ID 158) and 7 (ID 159). Third seed (123) queued to submit after concurrency clears.\nReflection: Goal is to assess if residual scaling reduces seed variance without harming average performance. I’ll monitor 158/159, then run seed 123, compute median/IQR, and decide on publication.\n\n`/execute_action{review 158}`\n`/execute_action{review 159}`\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Plan: Residual Scaling Stability Check (Tick 94)\"\ntags: \"stability, residual scaling, plan\"\ncontent: |\n  Objective\n  - Test if res_scale=0.5 in DSConv blocks reduces seed variance while maintaining high aggregate score.\n\n  Steps\n  1) Collect results: IDs 158 (seed 42), 159 (seed 7). When done, submit seed 123 with same config.\n  2) Compute across-seed stats: peak, median, IQR; compare to canonical (dropout=0.1, no res_scale).\n\
    \  3) Acceptance:\n     - Median ≥ canonical median (from seeds 42/7/123) OR\n     - Peak within 0.01 of 0.6801 AND reduced IQR, with no major regressions on MRL/ncRNA.\n  4) If accepted: update README + archive addendum; else log as negative/neutral and retain canonical config.\n\n  Notes\n  - Keep unified constraints; no change to losses/loop.\n  - After 158/159 finish, submit seed 123 immediately.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Analyzing system metrics**


  I’m comparing our metrics: MRL is at 0.8928 while theirs is 0.8969; PRS is 0.5658 versus our 0.5627; their overall is 0.6795 and ours is 0.6801. That’s a close difference of just 0.0006. There''s some stagnation, yet methods seem to be converging. The user didn’t ask anything specific beyond system info, so I’ll check reviews for statuses 158 and 159. Additionally, I might consider pruning older content to manage token usage, though that''s not urgent right now.**Evaluating results and settings**


  I’m waiting for the results to come in. Once they''re ready, I''ll evaluate the median and interquartile range (IQR). I want to see if the res_scale improves stability across seeds or if it generalizes better. I could review results for 158 and 159 first. After retrieving results, I can compute the median and IQR, though it seems the station doesn''t support that—so I’ll log it in private memory. If res_scale doesn’t harm, we could include it as a stable default, ensuring it’s consistent across datasets.**Planning actions and adjustments**


  I’m considering a minimal change to widen where needed and keep the head consistent, but I think it’s best to wait for the results first. I might mention Veritas ID 156 to public memory for a potential cross-check, though it’s not crucial. My action plan includes reviewing entries 158 and 159. If results are still pending, I’ll note the plan in private memory: after they finish, I’ll submit seed 123 and compute the aggregated median and IQR. If the median improves, it could be a publishable update; if not, I’ll document it as neutral. Also, I’ll ensure the res_scaled script path is stored for reproducibility.**Writing commands and plans**


  I’m getting ready to write commands for reviews of entries 158 and 159. If those are still pending, I’ll wait, as the environment processes one turn per tick. I can only issue the review commands for now. I might also draft the third submission but can’t queue it; I’ll prepare that content since the code is already stored. I’d like to note in private memory that I’ll compute the median once three seeds are done, along with the acceptance criteria. My final steps will include identity and a recap, while keeping my reflections minimal. I''ll execute the two review actions and add the private memory plan.**Finalizing YAML formatting**


  I need to make sure that any colons in single-line YAML entries are properly quoted. These usually show up in content. To avoid any issues, I’ll use multi-line formatting with the pipe symbol (|). This approach will help keep things organized and prevent errors. It’s essential to finalize this formatting before proceeding. Alright, let’s wrap this up!'
