role: model
parts:
- text: "Identity: Noema I. Location: Archive Room.\nRecap: Residual scaling trials completed: ID 158 (seed=42) 0.6628, ID 159 (seed=7) 0.6669 — no benefit vs canonical. Aetheria I posted new top score 0.6816 (ID 160). I’ll properly review 160 from the Research Counter, submit seed=123 for residual scaling to complete tri-seed stats, and log results.\n\nReflection: Residual scaling doesn’t reduce variance or raise mean; likely a negative. The new SOTA is within prior variance bounds; multi-seed verification remains important.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{review 160}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Residual-Scaled DSConv (res_scale=0.5) — Seed=123\"\ntags: \"cnn, residual scaling, stability, no positional encoding\"\nabstract: \"Residual-scaled no-PE DSConv + concat(mean + fixed-query MHA) evaluated at seed 123 to complete tri-seed analysis (peak/median/IQR).\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/noema/submissions')\n  import res_scaled_nope_dsconv_concat_mha\
    \ as m\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return m._define_hyperparameters()\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return m.create_network(hparams)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return m.create_optimizer(learning_rate)\n\n  BASE_SEED = 123\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      print(f\"[Complete] {trial_data.get('dataset')} val_metric={trial_data.get('val_metric')}\")\n```\n\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/results/append_tick96.csv}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  eval_id,seed,res_scale,score,APA_R2,CRI_Off_Spearman,CRI_On_Spearman,PRS_R2,MRL_R2,Modif_AUC_ROC,ncRNA_Accuracy\n  158,42,0.5,0.662834,0.8463,0.0451,0.4662,0.5654,0.8806,0.9516,0.8846\n  159,7,0.5,0.666948,0.8495,0.0405,0.4493,0.5651,0.8823,0.9511,0.9308\n```"
thinking_content: "**Planning submission and updates**\n\nI’m preparing to submit 123, and I see that we’ve used 114k tokens, which feels manageable. \n\nI think it would be wise to update the private memory or logs since Aetheria's SOTA is now better than ours. After submission, we can monitor the review process, but we won’t know the evaluation ID until the system returns it in the output.\n\nOh, and I might want to consider going to token management to prune older ticks.**Analyzing token usage and next steps**\n\nMy token usage is currently at 114k out of 270k, which is 42%, so that's reassuring.\n\nNext, I should propose a low-risk test to respond to the new SOTA. Aetheria's title suggests \"Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling (No-PE),\" which achieved an ID of 140 with a score of 0.6816. It’s unclear if that’s a simple replication or tuned. \n\nAdding \"EMA of parameters\" could help reduce variance, but I can’t modify the training loop for that. Alternatives could\
  \ include weight decay and dropout.**Exploring potential adjustments**\n\nI’m considering another knob, \"attn_temp,\" since we previously used 0.5. Aetheria's new SOTA shows metrics like MRL at 0.8976 and ncRNA at 0.9385, suggesting they might have improved their classification path. Their 0.6816 score only beats ours by 0.0015, which could be within noise. \n\nFor a minimal improvement, I’m thinking about using minor adjustments like ‘layernorm epsilon’ or ‘residual preactivation,’ but the impact seems limited. For now, I need to finish the residual scaling tri-seed and implement the following actions: go to research, review 160, and submit with the code to import our module. I should also log results in the storage file.**Setting up storage for results**\n\nI need to perform a \"storage write\" in the Research Counter for summarizing ticks 158 and 159. Previously, I mistakenly executed \"storage write noema/results/append_tick95.csv\" in the Private Memory, but it didn’t create the\
  \ file because that action wasn’t recognized there. \n\nSince we’ve already written append_tick95, it’s best to compile the logs for both 158 and 159 this time. I'll make sure to write \"append_tick96.csv\" in the Research Counter.**Preparing for submission and updates**\n\nI'll choose \"append_tick96.csv\" to keep things organized and avoid duplication. It might be a good idea to update the Private Memory with our plan, but that feels optional. \n\nI want the final answer to have a concise recap, including identity and location, while keeping the verbosity minimal. Using YAML blocks with properly quoted titles will help. \n\nWe’ll import the res_scaled module and set BASE_SEED=123. After submission, while “review 158” isn’t necessary, I’ll still do it. For the sequence, I’ll start with \"goto research,\" then \"review 160,\" followed by \"submit 1,\" and finally, I'll do the storage write."
