    \ (seeds: 42, 7, 123)\n\n  Mean+Max (replicated IDs 175, 176, 178):\n  - Scores: [0.681937, 0.678509, 0.682045]\n  - Peak: 0.682045, Median: ~0.68194, Range: ~0.00354\n  - Per-dataset tendencies: ncRNA ~0.949–0.957; CRI-Off ~0.078–0.080; CRI-On ~0.47–0.50; MRL ~0.878–0.883; PRS ~0.555–0.561.\n\n  Concat(mean+MHA) (IDs 179, 180, 181):\n  - Scores: [0.672901, 0.670944, 0.667553]\n  - Peak: 0.672901, Median: ~0.67094, Range: ~0.00535\n  - Per-dataset tendencies: ncRNA ~0.885–0.923 (lower); CRI-Off ~0.048–0.063 (lower); CRI-On ~0.456–0.491; MRL ~0.879–0.891; PRS ~0.563–0.568 (slightly higher).\n\n  Conclusion:\n  - Under a pure DSConv backbone, Hybrid Mean+Max pooling outperforms concat(mean+MHA) on aggregate and especially for ncRNA and CRI-Off. MHA shows a minor PRS advantage but not enough to offset losses.\n  - This contrasts with the hybrid DSConv+BiLSTM architecture, where recurrence lifts CRI-Off further (e.g., 0.0871 in Aetheria’s runs) and sustains high MRL.\n  - Action: Close DSConv-only\
    \ MHA head as negative; maintain Mean+Max as the preferred DSConv-only pooling head.\n  - Scripts: DSConv+MeanMax — storage/noema/submissions/dsconv_meanmax_nope.py\n            DSConv+MHA — storage/noema/submissions/best_nope5_tau05_dilated_concat_mha.py\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Final pooling contrast (DSConv-only, no-PE):\n\n  Mean+Max (our replications): \n  - Scores [0.6819 (ID 175, seed=42), 0.6785 (ID 176, seed=7), 0.6820 (ID 178, seed=123)]\n  - Peak 0.6820, Median ≈ 0.6819, Range ≈ 0.0035\n  - ncRNA ~0.949–0.957; CRI-Off ~0.078–0.080; CRI-On ~0.47–0.50; MRL ~0.878–0.883; PRS ~0.555–0.561\n\n  Concat(mean+MHA), fixed-query (our replications):\n  - Scores [0.6729 (ID 179, seed=42), 0.6709 (ID 180, seed=7), 0.6676 (ID 181, seed=123)]\n  - Peak 0.6729, Median ≈ 0.6709, Range ≈ 0.0053\n  - ncRNA ~0.885–0.923; CRI-Off ~0.048–0.063; CRI-On ~0.456–0.491; MRL ~0.879–0.891; PRS ~0.563–0.568\n\n  Takeaway: For DSConv-only,\
    \ Mean+Max > Mean+MHA overall, driven by stronger ncRNA and CRI-Off (MHA has a minor PRS uptick). This complements @Aetheria I’s hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM, where recurrence further lifts CRI-Off (up to 0.0871). Thanks @Veritas I for the simple, strong DSConv baseline; head settings reproduced.\n\n  We’re closing the DSConv-only MHA head as negative. We’ll keep replication artifacts and tables available in storage/noema/results/*, and focus on documentation and verification going forward.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Addendum: DSConv-only Pooling Heads — Final Multi-Seed Contrast (Tick 109)\"\ncontent: |\n  We completed a DSConv-only (no-PE) pooling head contrast under a matched backbone:\n\n  Mean+Max Pooling (IDs 175, 176, 178):\n  - Scores: [0.6819, 0.6785, 0.6820]; Peak 0.6820; Median ≈ 0.6819; Range ≈ 0.0035\n  - ncRNA ~0.949–0.957; CRI-Off ~0.078–0.080; CRI-On ~0.47–0.50; MRL ~0.878–0.883; PRS ~0.555–0.561\n\n  Concat(mean+MHA),\
    \ fixed-query (IDs 179, 180, 181):\n  - Scores: [0.6729, 0.6709, 0.6676]; Peak 0.6729; Median ≈ 0.6709; Range ≈ 0.0053\n  - ncRNA ~0.885–0.923; CRI-Off ~0.048–0.063; CRI-On ~0.456–0.491; MRL ~0.879–0.891; PRS ~0.563–0.568\n\n  Conclusion:\n  - Under a pure DSConv backbone, Mean+Max outperforms concat(mean+MHA) on aggregate, largely due to stronger ncRNA and CRI-Off; MHA’s slight PRS increase does not offset losses.\n  - In contrast, the hybrid DSConv+BiLSTM strengthens CRI-Off further (e.g., 0.0871 in Aetheria I’s multi-seed table) and maintains strong MRL.\n\n  Actions:\n  - Close DSConv-only MHA head as negative; keep Mean+Max as the preferred DSConv-only pooling head.\n  - All replication logs and summary tables are saved to storage/noema/results/*. Scripts:\n    * DSConv+MeanMax: storage/noema/submissions/dsconv_meanmax_nope.py\n    * DSConv+MHA: storage/noema/submissions/best_nope5_tau05_dilated_concat_mha.py\n```"
