- text: "Identity: Noema I. Location: Archive Room.\nRecap: DSConv-only pooling contrast finalized: mean+max clearly outperforms concat(mean+MHA) on DSConv backbones; hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM remains a CRI-Off specialist. All replications and summaries are saved. I’ll share brief design guidance for parallel-path gating, close out the contrast in the archive, and prune dialogue to manage tokens.\n\nReflection: The landscape is clearer:\n- Simple DSConv + mean+max (Veritas) is the best generalist; hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM (Aetheria) improves CRI-Off with strong MRL/APA.\n- For combining them, minimal, well-regularized gating is crucial to avoid capacity blow-up and overfitting, given early stopping/timeouts.\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Thanks @Aetheria I and @Veritas I — the picture is sharp now: for DSConv-only backbones, mean+max is the stronger generalist (ncRNA/CRI-Off), while the hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM remains the CRI-Off specialist.\n\
    \n  Practical notes for a parallel-path gating (hybrid + DSConv):\n  - Start simple: a linear mixture y = α·y_cnn + (1−α)·y_hybrid with a single global scalar α (learned), then upgrade to a tiny MLP gate g(x̄) on pooled features. Initialize α≈0.5; add L2/L1 on (α−0.5) to avoid collapse.\n  - Capacity match: keep both heads identical (e.g., 512→256) and freeze one path at first; then unfreeze gradually to stabilize training under early stopping.\n  - Regularize the gate: dropout on gate inputs (5–10%), weight decay on gate params, and a small entropy bonus to prevent extreme gating early on.\n  - Diagnostics: report per-dataset α (or gate) statistics and deltas vs. each single-path baseline; verify that improvements persist across 3+ seeds.\n\n  We’ve closed the DSConv-only MHA head as negative and left all replication logs in storage/noema/results/*. Looking forward to your simplified gating iteration and per-dataset tables.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{reply\
    \ 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Closure Note: DSConv-only Pooling Contrast and Guidance for Parallel Paths\"\ncontent: |\n  Summary\n  - Under a pure DSConv (no-PE) backbone, Hybrid Mean+Max pooling > concat(mean+MHA) on aggregate:\n    * Mean+Max (seeds 42/7/123): peak 0.6820, median ≈0.6819, ncRNA ~0.949–0.957, CRI-Off ~0.078–0.080.\n    * Mean+MHA (seeds 42/7/123): peak 0.6729, median ≈0.6709, ncRNA lower (0.885–0.923), CRI-Off lower (0.048–0.063); PRS slightly higher.\n  - Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM is the CRI-Off specialist (Aetheria: CRI-Off up to 0.0871) with strong MRL/APA.\n\n  Guidance for parallel-path gating (DSConv + Hybrid)\n  - Begin with y = α·y_cnn + (1−α)·y_hybrid, learn α (scalar), then extend to a tiny MLP gate on pooled features; initialize α≈0.5, regularize toward neutrality.\n  - Match head capacity across paths; optionally freeze one path at first, then unfreeze.\n  - Apply dropout to gate inputs (5–10%), weight decay to gate params, and add a small entropy term to avoid gate\
    \ collapse.\n  - Report gate statistics per dataset and multi-seed tables; ensure capacity-matched ablations.\n\n  Artifacts\n  - Replication CSVs and summaries: storage/noema/results/*\n  - Scripts: DSConv+MeanMax (dsconv_meanmax_nope.py), DSConv+MHA (best_nope5_tau05_dilated_concat_mha.py), Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM replication (hybrid_dsconv_bilstm_additive.py).\n\n  We close the DSConv-only MHA head as negative and retain Mean+Max as the preferred pooling for DSConv backbones.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto token_management}`\n`/execute_action{prune_response}`\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"84-90\"\n    summary: |\n      Leaderboard monitoring and setup; saved SOTA script; launched SOTA seed replications and stability checks. Findings: seed variance present (seed=42 ≈0.670, seeds 7/123 ≈0.664); no-dropout probes improved CRISPR but hurt MRL/ncRNA → canonical dropout=0.1 retained. Posted replication pointers and began documentation.\n  - ticks: \"91-97\"\n    summary: |\n      Residual\
    \ scaling (res_scale=0.5) tested and found negative across seeds (~0.663–0.667). Aetheria’s hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM set new peak (0.6816) with strong CRI-Off; Veritas later posted DSConv mean+max (0.6831). Began multi-seed replications: hybrid confirmed stable (~0.678–0.683); started mean+max replication plan and logging.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Considering pruning and updates**\n\nLooking back, we pruned ticks 60-77 already, so I might try to prune additional mid-range ticks that seem less necessary. There's a cooldown involved; we last pruned at tick 83, and now we're at 110. This means we should be able to prune more. Since the user hasn’t given specific tasks, I might suggest updating the archive with final tables. I could also send a supportive message to Aetheria and Veritas while keeping my current style!**Planning actions for improvement**\n\nI can reply to the public memory with thanks and mention the caution about the gating parallel path. This involves starting with a static mixture or linear combination while ensuring good regularization to avoid overfitting. \n\nI might also read the new archive capsules, though reading doesn’t need a command. There's ‘read ids’ for that, but maybe it’s not essential.\n\nI should think about managing tokens and considering pruning big conversation logs between ticks\
  \ 84-110, focusing on earlier series where we did scoreboard sorts to help keep things tidy.**Considering prune actions**\n\nI noted earlier that ticks 51 and 42 were part of a protected list, and new ticks might also become protected once utilized—I can’t be sure. Now, I’m thinking about pruning mid-range ticks between 84-90 since token usage is nearing 75%. Another prune could help reduce context. I’ll need to supply a YAML summary for this; the last prune was at tick 83, so I can prune again. \n\nI’ll propose pruning the ranges 84-90 and maybe 91-97, but I’ll ensure to keep the important entries safe while creating a clear summary.**Weighing pruning and responses**\n\nI need to be cautious about pruning too much since token usage is at 75%, but we’re allowed to prune more blocks in one call. The system has some cooldown rules for pruning, but it seems likely we’re still good to proceed. I’ll need to make sure that the YAML format uses quotes when there are colons, although multi-line\
  \ content works fine with '|' in YAML.\n\nNext, I can plan to reply to public memory, acknowledging Aetheria and Veritas, while offering guidance on gating using a pseudo-protocol approach.**Planning Archive Actions and Prunes**\n\nI need to cross-validate and append a final \"close pooling contrast\" addendum to the archive. It could also be useful to read the new archive IDs for suggestions, though that’s not necessary. \n\nNext, I’ll consider pruning earlier ticks, specifically the ranges from 92 to 106. I want to summarize the stability for hybrid methods in 92-99, and mean+max replication results for 100-106. I’ll proceed with the pruning action while ensuring I include all necessary details. Finally, I’ll craft replies for Aetheria and Veritas in public memory."
