thinking_content: "**Considering summary options**\n\nI’m contemplating compiling a final “Replication and leaderboard summary” in my memory. But I’m unsure what the user expects in terms of action. The environment feels like it encourages continued work. I could examine dataset weighting differences by looking at their contributions to the mean, but we already have those means. I might suggest building an ensemble that averages predictions from two different approaches, but the training system limits evaluation methods. I'm wondering if this unification across datasets will actually improve results.**Evaluating gating options**\n\nI’m considering improvements for CRI-On by comparing DSConv meanmax with the dual-path model. While the dual-path lowers CRI-On, it raises MRL/APA. I wonder if averaging DSConv-only meanmax with DSConv plus BiLSTM attention could enhance both scores. Using a fixed α at 0.5 might help keep CRI-On, but I noticed that learned α tends to favor the BiLSTM path. The\
  \ idea of freezing α at a constant across tasks seems low-risk, which could allow us to avoid potential overfitting issues.**Managing submission timing**\n\nI need to be cautious about not exceeding the submission cooldown for research, though the system hasn't confirmed specifics about it. I'm considering submitting “Fixed α=0.5 dual-path” without training α. Should I include seed 42? That might lead to replication. Our token usage looks fine, but I could also propose analyzing specifics of CRI-On. Notably, my DSConv meanmax shows 171 CRI-On at 0.5278, while the gated version is significantly lower. If we fix α close to 1, CRI-On might improve, but that would lead us back to DSConv-only, which isn’t particularly interesting.**Exploring decoupling approach**\n\nI’m considering an alternative called \"Head decoupling,\" which would train multi-task loss across datasets. However, since training is separate for each dataset, the gating parameter α would be shared. Aetheria previously tried\
  \ using α as a function of sequence length, but that didn’t score well. Constant α seems a better choice. Since we've already closed the gating line, perhaps I can publish a \"Replication capsule\" summarizing these findings. I can quickly update the public memory and manage tokens, especially around ticks that have heavy lengths. The cooldown is about four ticks, so I'll wait before pruning again if necessary.**Planning next steps**\n\nI can’t prune again until tick 122 since the last prune was at tick 118. I’ve considered creating a \"Handoff snapshot\" for private memory. Proposing to re-implement DSConv mean+max as a unified code could bring us closer to 171. While small variances are observed, I might attempt a seed sweep, although my instructions say to replicate, not search. Regularization and dropout adjustments don't seem promising. \n\nThere's the option to explore new pooling methods like \"LSE\" to combine mean and max. I could propose updating storage and the public memory\
  \ with findings, particularly regarding closing gating. After that, I'll write an \"SOTA_MONITOR.md\" summarizing the top results. Rather than starting new experiments now, I’ll focus on refining existing documentation and actions.**Planning actions for SOTA monitoring**\n\nI’m outlining the steps: first, I want to navigate to research. Next, I’ll write to the storage file \"SOTA_MONITOR.md\" with a table of the top 10 entries, highlighting changes for specific datasets. I'll include scores for 171, 167, and 187, along with relevant details like author and MRL.\n\nI might also consider updating archive #3 for negative results, but that's optional. Finally, I should ensure proper formatting for the YAML quotes. Once everything is prepared, I’ll implement these actions as planned!"
