role: model
parts:
- text: "Reflection Tick 4 / 5\n\nTheme: calibrating outputs and stabilizing extremes without breaking unification\n\n- Monotone calibration as a lawful “adapter.” The loss is fixed (MSE), but CRI-On/Off are judged by ranks. I can append a tiny 1D, strictly monotone map g after each regression head: ŷ = g(z), where z is the pre-output scalar. A monotone spline with 3–4 knots, enforced by nonnegative slopes (softplus-constrained), would be architecture-agnostic and unified across datasets (classification heads unchanged). This doesn’t hack the training loop; it just shapes the output space so MSE training aligns better with Spearman. I expect reduced heteroscedastic artifacts and fewer rank inversions for CRI tasks, with negligible capacity cost. If it fails to move Spearman beyond noise, I’ll document it as a clean negative.\n\n- Variance control at pooling time. Max’s brittleness shows up as seed variance and rank noise. Before swapping max entirely, two gentle stabilizers:\n  1) Soft\
    \ extremes: concat(mean, LSEτ), τ small (0.25–0.5), computed as τ·log(mean exp(x/τ)) so length normalization is baked in. This retains “evidence of existence” while damping single-position spikes.\n  2) Dispersion proxy: concat(mean, std). If what max captures is “how unevenly strong signals are distributed,” std might be a smoother surrogate.\n\n  Both are principled, unified, and parameter-matched to mean+max. Top-2 mean sits between max and LSEτ and could be a final tiebreaker if needed.\n\n- Small-data discipline. CRI-On (5.6k/709) punishes additional degrees of freedom. This explains why attention, gating, or learned queries often regress CRI-On while helping richer datasets. Any proposed fix should either reduce variance (soft extremes, length-normalized pooling) or re-shape outputs (monotone g) rather than inject more dynamics.\n\n- Invariance as a north star. The best wins preserved translation equivariance (no PE) and pooled with permutation-invariant statistics. I should interrogate\
    \ every new component: does it encode a clear invariance or just add capacity? If it’s the latter, it probably won’t survive multi-seed tests.\n\n- Publication posture. If soft extremes and monotone g don’t lift CRI ranks beyond noise, that’s a valuable negative: “Geometry of pooling and metric alignment in unified RNA models.” The broader claim remains: simplicity plus aligned inductive bias beats ornate mechanisms under unified constraints."
thinking_content: "**Exploring model complexities**\n\nI'm concerned about the danger of overfitting in complex models, emphasizing the need for simplicity. There's a rule about pivoting if stagnation happens, and we have to ensure we're paving the way for future work. \n\nIn an inter-agent environment, it's vital to resist copying code and focus on high-level concept sharing while proposing challenges. I plan to write a brief piece on \"Pooling geometry vs metric alignment\" for clarity. \n\nI also need to avoid dataset-specific configurations and propose a plan with experiments."
