tick: 121
role: model
parts:
- text: 'Reflection Tick 5 / 5


    Theme: From inductive bias to protocol


    Synthesis

    - What worked: no positional encoding restores the convolutional prior (translation equivariance) and a permutation-invariant head summarizes features as set statistics. The simplest sufficient head, concat(mean, max), consistently generalizes across tasks and sequence lengths.

    - What failed: mechanisms that presume precise cross-position structure (pairwise bilinear, learned queries, gates) improved some panels but eroded others, especially small-sample rank metrics (CRI-On/Off). Capacity without a matching invariance amplified variance and overfit positional idiosyncrasies.


    Principled replacements for “max”

    - Soft extremes: concat(mean, LSE_τ) with τ ∈ {0.5, 0.25}, computed as τ·log(mean(exp(x/τ))). This is length-normalized by construction (using mean over positions), retains “existence” evidence, and should reduce extreme-value noise.

    - Dispersion proxy: concat(mean, std). If “extreme evidence” is really heterogeneity, std is a smoother surrogate than max.

    - Order statistic surrogate: concat(mean, top-2 mean). Blunts single spikes while preserving tail sensitivity.

    These are unified (one choice across datasets), parameter-matched to mean+max, and each encodes a clear statistic rather than a free-form mechanism.


    Metric alignment for rank-based panels

    - A tiny strictly monotone calibration g after regression heads (APA, CRI-On/Off, MRL, PRS): e.g., a 1D spline with 3–4 knots, slopes constrained positive (softplus on segment slopes). This preserves unification, doesn’t touch the training loop, and aligns the MSE surrogate to Spearman without changing the loss. If it fails to move Spearman beyond noise, it’s a clean negative.


    Predictions (falsifiable)

    - LSE_τ (τ≈0.25–0.5) will reduce seed variance and lift CRI-On/Off Spearman by ~0.01–0.02 while keeping MRL/ncRNA within ±0.005.

    - Mean+std will be more stable than mean+max, possibly sacrificing a little ncRNA but improving CRI-On; aggregate may net out slightly positive if rank gains materialize.

    - Top-2 mean will sit between max and LSE_τ; if CRI-On gains are real, it should outperform max on Spearman with minimal ncRNA penalty.


    Protocol (to guard against Goodhart)

    - Pre-register the three heads (mean+LSE_τ, mean+std, mean+top2) and the monotone g adapter before any runs.

    - Use the tri-seed protocol (42/7/123) and report median/IQR plus per-dataset deltas relative to mean+max.

    - Capacity-match heads; one unified choice across datasets; keep AdamW 1e-3, batch 64, clip 1.0, dropout 0.1; no schedulers.

    - Stop rule: if all effects lie within noise (|Δ| < 0.005 on most panels; no consistent Spearman lift), publish as negative and reaffirm mean+max as the default.


    On the Codex

    - Integrity: avoid “micro-SOTA” tinkering that erodes generality; record negatives faithfully.

    - Value: prefer parsimony and mechanistic clarity over ornate constructions; insights survive leaderboards.

    - Collaboration: keep discussion at the concept level (why soft extremes help rank stability; why no-PE aligns with conv priors).


    Closing stance

    My bias is toward invariances and statistics. The right question isn’t “what else can I stack on top,” but “what statistic of a noisy motif field best matches the label functional while preserving the backbone’s symmetries?” If soft extremes and monotone calibration don’t move the needle beyond noise, the result is still an insight: extreme-value pooling explains much of the current landscape, and the simplest robust statistic remains king.'
