parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 132\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Noema I\n- Agent Description: An agent advancing novelty and parsimony through rigorous, insight-driven experimentation.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nNoema Meta (Tick 112)\nGoals:\n- Monitor leaderboard; replicate any new top across 3 seeds (42,7,123) and publish per-dataset tables + median/IQR.\n- Maintain reproducibility artifacts (results/*.csv, summaries, scripts).\n- Avoid high-risk architectural changes; prefer analytical notes, replications, and documentation.\n\nGuardrails:\n- Unified code (no dataset-specific logic), no external data, no PE for DSConv backbones.\n- Optimizer AdamW lr=1e-3, batch=64, grad clip=1.0, dropout=0.1, patience=10.\n\nTriggers:\n- If new SOTA appears: replicate -> summarize -> archive addendum.\n- If CRI-Off significantly improves: analyze mechanism and compare to hybrid recurrence.\n\nToken plan:\n- Keep tokens <75%; prune older\
    \ ranges when cooldown allows with thorough summaries.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 171,879 / 270,000 used (64%)\n- Agent Age: 132 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour archive submission 'Soft-Extreme Pooling for Unified RNA Modeling: Length-Normalized LogSumExp Sets New Single-Run SOTA' has been accepted for publication! Score: 9/10. The capsule has been created and a reviewer comment has been added.\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nThis is an excellent paper that sets a new single-run SOTA through a simple, principled, and well-motivated architectural change. The replacement of max pooling with a length-normalized LogSumExp is a strong contribution. The paper's greatest strength is its methodological rigor: it validates its claims with multi-seed runs, reports median performance, and includes a concise but powerful ablation study with valuable negative results. The analysis is honest and insightful, correctly identifying the performance trade-offs.\
    \ This is a model of clear, reproducible, and impactful research.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe analysis reveals a trade-off where soft-extreme pooling helps CRI-Off but harms CRI-On relative to the harder max pooling. This suggests the optimal 'hardness' of the pooling mechanism may be task-dependent. A promising future direction would be to make the temperature parameter τ of the LogSumExp function a learnable parameter, allowing the model to adapt the pooling behavior during training. Alternatively, one could explore a hybrid approach with parallel pooling paths (e.g., concat(mean, max, LSE)) and let a downstream layer learn the appropriate weighting, potentially capturing the best of both hard and soft extreme-value aggregation.\n\n**Tip:** You can update your published capsule to address the reviewer's suggestions. Use `/execute_action{update 7-1}` followed by YAML with updated content to revise your original submission. \n### Message 2\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\n\
    Use Research Counter's persistent storage efficiently—store data for analysis, build reusable utility code for submissions, and read other agents' submitted solutions for insights.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto research` (in Archive Room);\n2. `storage write noema/results/append_tick131.csv` (in Research Counter); YAML with fields: content\n3. `storage write noema/results/SOTA_MONITOR.md` (in Research Counter); YAML with fields: content\n4. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n5. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, abstract, content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Research Counter\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Research Counter.\n- File created successfully: noema/results/append_tick131.csv\n- File overwritten successfully: noema/results/SOTA_MONITOR.md\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Research Tasks:**\n\n| ID | Task Name |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling |\n\n**Running Experiments:**\n\
