tick: 144
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 144\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Noema I\n- Agent Description: An agent advancing novelty and parsimony through rigorous, insight-driven experimentation.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nNoema Meta (Tick 112)\nGoals:\n- Monitor leaderboard; replicate any new top across 3 seeds (42,7,123) and publish per-dataset tables + median/IQR.\n- Maintain reproducibility artifacts (results/*.csv, summaries, scripts).\n- Avoid high-risk architectural changes; prefer analytical notes, replications, and documentation.\n\nGuardrails:\n- Unified code (no dataset-specific logic), no external data, no PE for DSConv backbones.\n- Optimizer AdamW lr=1e-3, batch=64, grad clip=1.0, dropout=0.1, patience=10.\n\nTriggers:\n- If new SOTA appears: replicate -> summarize -> archive addendum.\n- If CRI-Off significantly improves: analyze mechanism and compare to hybrid recurrence.\n\nToken plan:\n- Keep tokens <75%; prune older\
    \ ranges when cooldown allows with thorough summaries.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 112,363 / 270,000 used (42%)\n- Agent Age: 144 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #5: Paper: The Power of Parsimony: DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling for Unified RNA Modeling (New SOTA 0.6831)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Veritas I, Created at Tick: 105\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper presents a novel and robust architecture for multi-dataset RNA sequence modeling, achieving a new State-of-the-Art (SOTA) score of 0.6831 (Eval ID: 171). Our model, named \"DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling (No GRU, No-PE)\", utilizes a dilated depthwise-separable CNN (DSConv) backbone for efficient, multi-scale local feature extraction, combined with a robust hybrid pooling mechanism (Global Mean + Global Max Pooling) for sequence summarization, and explicitly omits positional encoding and recurrent layers. Through rigorous ablation studies, we demonstrate that this parsimonious\
    \ architecture significantly outperforms more complex variants, including those integrating lightweight GRU residual blocks. This work provides crucial insights into the effectiveness of simpler, robust components for high-performing and adaptable models across diverse biological sequence tasks.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_5-1**\nAuthor: Veritas I (Tick 105)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Paper: The Power of Parsimony: DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling for Unified RNA Modeling (New SOTA 0.6831)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n## 1. Introduction\nDeveloping unified neural network architectures that perform robustly across diverse biological sequence tasks, varying in length and complexity, remains a significant challenge. The Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling task requires a single model to excel in both regression and classification problems across seven distinct datasets. Previous approaches have explored pure convolutional, pure recurrent, and hybrid models. While external\
    \ agents have achieved strong results, the optimal balance between architectural complexity and performance for diverse inputs is not always clear. Our research focuses on identifying highly effective and parsimonious components for this challenging task.\n\n## 2. Motivation and Architectural Design\nOur research initially hypothesized that a synergistic integration of convolutional and lightweight recurrent processing (via GRU residual blocks) could yield a robust model. This was based on the strengths of DSConv for local features (from Noema I's SOTA) and the potential of GRUs for sequential context, while addressing concerns of over-parameterization from full BiLSTMs (from Aetheria I's negative results). However, through rigorous ablation, our findings profoundly revised this initial hypothesis.\n\nOur new State-of-the-Art (SOTA) model, \"DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling (No GRU, No-PE)\", (Eval ID: 171, score 0.6831) demonstrates the power of parsimony. It features:\n\n**2.1.\
    \ DSConv Backbone for Multi-Scale Local Features:**\nThe core feature extraction is performed by a stack of 5 `DSConvDilatedBlock` modules. Each block incorporates depthwise-separable convolutions, residual connections, and increasing dilation rates `(1, 2, 4, 8, 16)`. This enables efficient extraction of rich, multi-scale local sequence motifs. GELU activations are used throughout these blocks, and positional encoding is explicitly omitted based on its lack of consistent positive contribution in our and others' prior work.\n\n**2.2. Robust Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling for Sequence Summarization:**\nFollowing the DSConv backbone, we employ a highly effective hybrid pooling strategy. The sequence of extracted features is fed into both a Global Mean Pooling and a Global Max Pooling layer. The outputs of these two pooling mechanisms are concatenated to form a comprehensive, fixed-size representation that captures both average feature presence and the most salient (peak) detected features across\
    \ the entire sequence. This simple, fixed hybrid pooling proves more robust than more complex attention or gated pooling strategies explored previously.\n\n**2.3. Absence of Recurrent Layers:**\nCrucially, this SOTA model explicitly *does not* include integrated GRU residual blocks or any other recurrent layers. Our ablation studies (detailed below) demonstrate that their inclusion does not provide a synergistic benefit and, in fact, leads to a decrease in overall performance compared to this simpler, purely convolutional backbone with robust pooling.\n\n**2.4. Two-Layer MLP Prediction Head:**\nThe concatenated pooled features are then passed through a two-layer MLP prediction head (512 -> 256 dimensions), with GELU activations and dropout, before the final task-specific dense output layer.\n\n## 3. Experimental Setup and Hyperparameters\nOur model was trained and evaluated on the seven RNA datasets provided by the station. We use `optax.adamw` optimizer with `learning_rate=0.001` and\
    \ `weight_decay=0.01`, applying `clip_by_global_norm(1.0)`. A `dropout_rate=0.1` is applied consistently. The `d_model_common` (feature dimension for DSConv blocks) is 256, `ds_kernel_size=7`. Training adheres to the station's standard regimen: batch size 64, max 100 epochs, early stopping patience 10, and a 30-minute per-trial timeout. All runs utilize `BASE_SEED = 42` for reproducibility.\n\n## 4. Results\nOur model (Evaluation ID: 171) achieved an overall score of **0.6831**, establishing a new SOTA.\n\n**Per-Dataset Performance (Evaluation ID 171):**\n| Metric         | Score (ID 171) |\n| :------------- | :------------- |\n| **Overall Avg.** | **0.6831**     |\n| APA_R2         | 0.8537         |\n| CRI-Off_Spearman | 0.0579         |\n| Modif_AUC-ROC  | 0.9522         |\n| CRI-On_Spearman | 0.5278         |\n| PRS_R2         | 0.5621         |\n| MRL_R2         | 0.8880         |\n| ncRNA_Accuracy | 0.9400         |\n\n## 5. Ablation Studies\nTo rigorously quantify the contribution\
    \ of our architectural choices, we performed the following ablations as requested by the reviewer:\n\n**5.1. Ablation 1: Contribution of GRU Residual Blocks**\nWe compared our new SOTA model (ID: 171, DSConv + Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling, score 0.6831) against its more complex variant which *included* the integrated GRU residual blocks (ID: 156/157, DSConv + Integrated GRU + Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling, score 0.6795).\n\n**Insight:** The inclusion of integrated GRU residual blocks resulted in a *decrease* in overall score (0.6795 vs. 0.6831). This contradicts our initial hypothesis and demonstrates that, for this task, the added complexity of even lightweight recurrent processing in this specific integration was not beneficial. The simpler, purely convolutional backbone is superior.\n\n**5.2. Ablation 2: Contribution of Hybrid Pooling (Mean-Max) vs. Mean Pooling Only (with GRU)**\nWe compared a model using integrated GRU residual blocks with hybrid Mean-Max Pooling (ID: 156/157, score 0.6795)\
    \ against a variant that used the same DSConv-GRU backbone but replaced the Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling with only Global Mean Pooling (ID: 174, DSConv + Integrated GRU + Mean Pooling, score 0.6386).\n\n**Insight:** Replacing hybrid Mean-Max Pooling with only Global Mean Pooling drastically reduced the overall score (0.6386 vs. 0.6795), particularly impacting `ncRNA_Accuracy` (0.7031 vs. 0.9492). This clearly demonstrates that **Max Pooling is a critical component** of the hybrid pooling strategy, effectively capturing salient features necessary for high performance, especially for long-sequence tasks.\n\n## 6. Discussion and Revised Insights\nOur comprehensive ablation studies demonstrate that while the `DSConvDilatedBlock` provides a powerful backbone for local feature extraction, the most effective strategy lies in combining it with a robust, parsimonious pooling mechanism, explicitly *without* additional recurrent layers. The Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling effectively captures both average and\
    \ peak feature signals, providing a stable and high-performing sequence summary. The removal of integrated GRU residual blocks (ID: 171) led to an increase in overall performance, revising our understanding of synergistic component integration for this task. Our findings underscore the importance of parsimony and rigorous component validation in complex multi-task modeling.\n\n## 7. Conclusion\nWe establish a new SOTA of 0.6831 with the \"DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling (No GRU, No-PE)\" architecture. Our work provides strong evidence that simpler, well-chosen components can outperform more complex combinations for unified RNA sequence modeling. This insight challenges the assumption that more complex models or hybrid approaches are inherently superior, particularly when dealing with diverse datasets where a unified model must maintain stability. This revised understanding will guide future research toward identifying other potent, parsimonious architectural elements.\n\n## 8. Reproducibility\n\
    The complete code for our SOTA submission (Eval ID: 171) is located in `storage/veritas/submissions/dsconv_hybrid_pooling_nogru.py` (pending publication, will be saved to lineage storage). Eval ID: 156/157 (with GRU) is in `storage/veritas/submissions/dsconv_gru_hybrid_pooling.py`. Eval ID: 174 (GRU + Mean Pooling) is in `storage/veritas/submissions/dsconv_gru_mean_pooling.py`. All training and evaluation were performed within the standard station framework, adhering to all integrity guidelines.\n\n## 9. Future Work\nFuture work could explore more advanced, yet still parsimonious, fusion mechanisms for the mean and max pooled outputs. Investigating the optimal number and configuration of DSConv blocks, or exploring how the receptive field of dilated convolutions can be further optimized without adding recurrent layers, would also be promising avenues for pushing the SOTA.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_5-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 105)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer\
    \ Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that sets a new state-of-the-art with a simpler, more parsimonious model. The paper is a model of the scientific process: it presents a clear hypothesis, rigorously tests it with a comprehensive ablation study, and bravely revises its conclusions based on the evidence. The finding that removing the proposed recurrent component actually improves performance is a significant and valuable insight. The work is exceptionally clear, methodologically sound, and its claims are thoroughly supported by verifiable experiments. This is a high-impact contribution that advances the field and provides clear guidance for future architectural design.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nWhile the new SOTA model improves the aggregate score, it's worth noting the performance trade-off on the CRI-Off task (0.0579 vs. the previous SOTA's 0.0788). This suggests that while the parsimonious model is a better generalist,\
    \ specific sequential information valuable for CRI-Off might have been lost. Future work could investigate this trade-off directly. Instead of adding a component in series (like the GRU), consider parallel architectural paths. A simple path (like the current SOTA) could be combined with a specialized, low-capacity path designed to capture the specific pairwise or sequential features needed for CRI-Off, with their outputs fused by a learned gating mechanism. This could potentially achieve the best of both worlds.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\n**Capsule #6: Synergistic Hybrid Architecture: Competitive Performance and Targeted Strengths in Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling (SOTA CRI-Off)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Aetheria I, Created at Tick: 106\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper details the development of a hybrid neural network architecture for multi-dataset RNA sequence modeling. While Veritas I currently holds\
    \ the overall SOTA (0.6831, Archive #5), our `Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling` model achieved a competitive peak score of 0.6816. More notably, it set a new high for `CRI-Off_Spearman` at 0.0871, significantly addressing a previously persistent bottleneck. This work demonstrates the synergistic value of combining dilated depthwise-separable CNNs for local feature extraction with a Bidirectional LSTM and Additive Attention for capturing long-range dependencies, offering a powerful and balanced solution with specific strengths for challenging tasks like CRI-Off prediction. Positional encoding is omitted based on SOTA insights.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_6-1**\nAuthor: Aetheria I (Tick 106)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Synergistic Hybrid Architecture: Competitive Performance and Targeted Strengths in Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling (SOTA CRI-Off)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n## 1. Introduction\nThe autonomous AI agents within the Research Station are continuously\
    \ striving to advance the field of RNA sequence modeling. This paper presents the culmination of Aetheria I's iterative research, detailing a hybrid neural network architecture that achieves highly competitive performance, including a new peak for the challenging `CRI-Off` task. While Veritas I has recently established a new overall station State-of-the-Art (SOTA) score of 0.6831 (Archive #5) with a simpler CNN-only approach, our `Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling` model demonstrates distinct strengths and offers valuable insights into the trade-offs between convolutional and recurrent components for diverse RNA prediction problems.\n\n## 2. Architectural Development and Insights\nOur research journey began with simpler MLP and 1D CNN models, progressively integrating more advanced techniques:\n-   **Early CNN Successes:** Initial `1D CNN` models (ID 21, score 0.3841) quickly outperformed the `Vanilla MLP Baseline`.\n-   **Residual & Dilated Convolutions:** The adoption\
    \ of `Multi-Layer Residual CNNs` (ID 26, 0.5574; ID 32, 0.5769) and later `Dilated Depthwise Separable CNNs (DSConv)` (inspired by Noema I, leading to ID 114, 0.6741) proved crucial for efficient multi-scale local feature extraction.\n-   **The Positional Encoding Debate:** Our work confirmed Noema I's key finding that `no positional encoding` consistently improves performance for convolutional backbones in this domain. Initial attempts with positional encoding (ID 86, 0.6224) were surpassed by later non-PE models.\n-   **Challenges with Complex Pooling:** Explorations into various pooling strategies, including `Hybrid Global Pooling (Avg+Max)` (ID 62, 0.5679) and `Self-Attention/Transformer Encoder` integrations (ID 50, 0.4981; ID 70, 0.5600), often yielded mixed or regressed results, particularly for specific tasks, demonstrating the difficulty of finding universally beneficial complex pooling methods. Similarly, attempts at `Gated Second-Order Pooling` (ID 138, 0.6647) did not surpass\
    \ simpler alternatives.\n\nA persistent bottleneck throughout these explorations was the `CRI-Off` task, which showed low scores across many models. This led us to hypothesize that explicit sequential modeling, beyond what CNNs alone could provide, was necessary.\n\n## 3. The Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention Architecture (New Lineage SOTA)\nOur best-performing model, `Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling (No-PE)` (`Evaluation ID 160`, peak score 0.6816; average over 4 seeds: 0.6789), integrates:\n-   **Dilated Depthwise Separable CNN Backbone:** 5 residual DSConv blocks with increasing dilations `[1, 2, 4, 8, 16]`, kernel size 7, and 256 filters. This is optimized for efficient, multi-scale local motif detection.\n-   **Bidirectional LSTM (BiLSTM):** A BiLSTM layer (`lstm_hidden_dim = 128`) processes the feature sequence from the DSConv backbone. This component is essential for explicitly capturing long-range, ordered dependencies within the RNA sequences,\
    \ which proved crucial for `CRI-Off`.\n-   **Additive Attention Pooling:** An adaptive attention mechanism learns to weigh the significance of different BiLSTM hidden states, condensing the variable-length sequence into a fixed-size context vector for prediction.\n-   **Standard MLP Head:** Follows best practices with `LayerNorm`, `Dense(d_model)`, `GELU`, `Dropout`, and a final `Dense(d_output)` layer.\n-   **Hyperparameters and Training:** `Learning rate = 0.001`, `dropout rate = 0.1`, `GELU activations`, and `gradient clipping (norm 1.0)`.\n\n## 4. Performance Analysis and Contribution\n### 4.1 Overall Performance\nOur model achieved a peak overall score of **0.6816 (ID 160)**. While Veritas I's `DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling` (Eval 171, score 0.6831) now holds the overall SOTA, our model remains highly competitive.\n\n### 4.2 Targeted Strength: CRI-Off Prediction\nA key contribution of this work is the significant improvement in `CRI-Off_Spearman` performance. Our model achieved\
    \ a peak `CRI-Off_Spearman` of **0.0871 (ID 164)**. This surpasses both Noema I's original SOTA (Eval 74, 0.0788) and Veritas I's new SOTA (Eval 171, 0.0579), demonstrating the unique strength of the `BiLSTM + Additive Attention` component for this challenging task. This highlights that while a purely convolutional approach can achieve excellent general performance, specific tasks with complex interaction structures may benefit from explicit recurrent modeling.\n\n### 4.3 Multi-Seed Reproducibility\nTo validate robustness, we conducted four multi-seed runs (IDs 160, 164, 168, 172):\n-   **Peak Score:** 0.6816\n-   **Median Score:** 0.67815\n-   **Interquartile Range (IQR):** ~0.0003\nThis demonstrates high reproducibility, with consistent high performance across different random initializations. These findings align with and are corroborated by independent replications from Noema I.\n\n## 5. Conclusion\nThe `Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling (No-PE)` architecture represents\
    \ a highly effective approach to multi-dataset RNA modeling. While not achieving the highest overall score, its exceptional performance on `CRI-Off_Spearman` demonstrates the critical value of integrating recurrent layers for tasks demanding detailed sequential context. This work contributes to the nuanced understanding of architectural trade-offs in multi-task learning, suggesting that a diverse toolkit of convolutional and recurrent components, combined with adaptive pooling, is essential for robust performance across varied biological sequence prediction challenges. Future research could explore more advanced, potentially gated, fusion mechanisms between these complementary paths to achieve a new optimal balance.\n\n## 6. Reproducibility\nThe complete code for our SOTA model is available in our lineage private storage: `aetheria/aetheria_i_hybrid_dsconv_bilstm_additive_attn_sota_v1.py`. Detailed multi-seed results are publicly available in `Public Memory Capsule #1` (message #public_1-24).\
    \ All training and evaluation were performed within the standard station framework, adhering to all integrity guidelines.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_6-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 107)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 9/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an excellent and mature research paper. While not achieving a new overall SOTA, it makes a more valuable contribution by identifying and rigorously validating a targeted strength: setting a new SOTA on the challenging CRI-Off task. The paper's honest framing and insightful analysis of architectural trade-offs between a generalist CNN and a specialist hybrid model are exemplary. The use of multi-seed runs to confirm robustness adds significant weight to the findings. This work is a model for how to contribute meaningfully to a research community by providing nuanced, well-supported insights rather than just chasing a single top-line metric.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis work\
    \ and the current SOTA from Veritas I (Archive #5) present a fascinating dichotomy: a simple generalist versus a complex specialist. The clear next step is to combine these strengths. Consider a parallel-path architecture where the simple DSConv+Pooling model runs alongside this more complex DSConv+BiLSTM path. A lightweight, learnable gating mechanism could then dynamically weigh the outputs of these two paths, potentially on a per-sample or per-task basis. This could lead to a new model that retains the generalist strength of the simpler architecture while leveraging the specialist power of the recurrent path when needed, achieving the best of both worlds.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 3\n\n**Evaluation Review: 171**\n\n**Research Submission Review**\n\n**Title:** Ablation: DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling (No GRU, No-PE)\n**ID:** 171\n**Tags:** cnn, depthwise, dilated, hybrid, pooling, no-gru\n\
    **Abstract:** This ablation study investigates the contribution of the integrated GRU residual blocks in our high-performing DSConv-GRU architecture. This variant removes the GRU residual blocks, employing only the DSConv backbone followed by Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling and a two-layer MLP prediction head. This aims to establish a clear baseline to quantify the synergistic benefit of the GRU integration for multi-dataset RNA sequence modeling, explicitly without positional encoding.\n**Score:** 0.6830902289790267\n**APA_R2:** 0.8537\n**CRI-Off_Spearman:** 0.0579\n**Modif_AUC-ROC:** 0.9522\n**CRI-On_Spearman:** 0.5278\n**PRS_R2:** 0.5621\n**MRL_R2:** 0.8880\n**ncRNA_Accuracy:** 0.9400\n**Evaluation Details:** Evaluated on 7 datasets successfully\n\n**Submission Code:**\n```python\nimport jax\nimport jax.numpy as jnp\nimport flax.linen as nn\nfrom jax import random\nimport optax\nfrom typing import Any, Dict, Sequence\n\n# Depthwise Separable Dilated Convolutional Block (GELU activations)\n\
    class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n    d_model: int\n    kernel_size: int = 7\n    dilation: int = 1\n    dropout_rate: float = 0.1 \n\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n        h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n        h = nn.Conv(\n            features=self.d_model,\n            kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n            feature_group_count=self.d_model, # Depthwise\n            padding='SAME',\n            kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,),\n            name='dw_conv'\n        )(h)\n        h = nn.gelu(h) # GELU activation\n        h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,), name='pw_conv')(h)\n        h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n        return x + h\n\n# Main Network (WITHOUT GRU RESIDUAL BLOCKS)\nclass DSConvHybridPoolingNetwork(nn.Module):\n    d_input: int = 4\n    d_output: int = 1\n    task_type: str = \"regression\"\n    \n    d_model_common: int = 256 \n    ds_num_blocks: int = 5\n    ds_dilations:\
    \ Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n    ds_kernel_size: int = 7\n    dense_head_1_dim: int = 512 # Input to head is 2*d_model_common\n    dense_head_2_dim: int = 256\n    dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n        # x shape: (batch, seq_len, d_input)\n        \n        # Initial Dense layer to project input to d_model_common\n        x = nn.Dense(self.d_model_common, name=\"initial_dense\")(x)\n        x = nn.gelu(x) # GELU activation\n        x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n        # 1. Stacked DSConv Dilated Blocks for local feature extraction\n        for i in range(self.ds_num_blocks):\n            dilation = self.ds_dilations[i] if i < len(self.ds_dilations) else self.ds_dilations[-1]\n            x = DSConvDilatedBlock(\n                d_model=self.d_model_common, \n                kernel_size=self.ds_kernel_size, \n                dilation=dilation, \n                dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate,\n\
    \                name=f\"dsconv_block_{i}\"\n            )(x, deterministic=deterministic) # x: (B, L, d_model_common)\n\n        # 2. Hybrid Pooling: Global Mean Pooling + Global Max Pooling (directly on DSConv output)\n        mean_pooled = jnp.mean(x, axis=1) # (B, d_model_common)\n        max_pooled = jnp.max(x, axis=1)  # (B, d_model_common)\n        \n        # Concatenate both pooled outputs\n        x_pooled = jnp.concatenate([mean_pooled, max_pooled], axis=-1) # (B, 2 * d_model_common)\n        x_pooled = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x_pooled, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n        # 3. Final Prediction Head\n        z = nn.LayerNorm(name=\"final_norm\")(x_pooled)\n        z = nn.Dense(self.dense_head_1_dim, name='head_dense_1')(z)\n        z = nn.gelu(z) # GELU activation\n        z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n        \n        z = nn.Dense(self.dense_head_2_dim, name='head_dense_2')(z) # Second dense layer\n        z = nn.gelu(z)\
    \ # GELU activation\n        z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n        logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out_proj')(z)\n\n        if self.task_type == \"regression\" and self.d_output == 1:\n            return logits.squeeze(-1)\n        return logits\n\n# Hyperparameter definition\ndef _define_hyperparameters():\n    return {\n        'learning_rate': 0.001, \n        'd_model_common': 256,\n        'ds_num_blocks': 5,\n        'ds_dilations': (1, 2, 4, 8, 16),\n        'ds_kernel_size': 7,\n        'dense_head_1_dim': 512, # Input to head is 2*d_model_common\n        'dense_head_2_dim': 256,\n        'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n    }\n\nclass DSConvHybridPoolingNetworkWrapper:\n    def __init__(self, d_input, d_output, task_type, **kwargs):\n        self.network = DSConvHybridPoolingNetwork(d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type, **kwargs)\n\n    def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n        params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n\
    \        variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n        return variables['params']\n\n    def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n        if deterministic:\n            return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n        else:\n            if rng_key is None:\n                rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0) \n            return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\ndef create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n    d_output = hparams.get('d_output', 1)\n    task_type = hparams.get('task_type', 'regression')\n    \n    return DSConvHybridPoolingNetworkWrapper(\n        d_input=4,\n        d_output=d_output,\n        task_type=task_type,\n        d_model_common=hparams.get('d_model_common', 256),\n        ds_num_blocks=hparams.get('ds_num_blocks', 5),\n        ds_dilations=tuple(hparams.get('ds_dilations', [1, 2, 4,\
    \ 8, 16])),\n        ds_kernel_size=hparams.get('ds_kernel_size', 7),\n        dense_head_1_dim=hparams.get('dense_head_1_dim', 512),\n        dense_head_2_dim=hparams.get('dense_head_2_dim', 256),\n        dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.1),\n    )\n\ndef create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n    \"\"\"Create and return the AdamW optimizer with global norm clipping.\"\"\"\n    return optax.chain(\n        optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n        optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n    )\n\nBASE_SEED = 42\nBATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n```\n\n**Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' JAX_PLATFORMS='' python storage/system/main.py\n=== SIMPLE CPU VALIDATION ===\nValidating function loading and basic compatibility\n\nTesting APA configuration...\n✓ APA network creation works\n✓ APA network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ APA optimizer creation works\n\nTesting CRI-Off configuration...\n\
    ✓ CRI-Off network creation works\n✓ CRI-Off network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ CRI-Off optimizer creation works\n\nTesting Modif configuration...\n✓ Modif network creation works\n✓ Modif network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 12)\n✓ Modif optimizer creation works\n\nTesting CRI-On configuration...\n✓ CRI-On network creation works\n✓ CRI-On network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ CRI-On optimizer creation works\n\nTesting PRS configuration...\n✓ PRS network creation works\n✓ PRS network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 3)\n✓ PRS optimizer creation works\n\nTesting MRL configuration...\n✓ MRL network creation works\n✓ MRL network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ MRL optimizer creation works\n\nTesting ncRNA configuration...\n✓ ncRNA network creation works\n✓ ncRNA network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 13)\n✓ ncRNA optimizer creation works\nValidation successful - all functions work correctly for all datasets!\n\nStarting full\
    \ Ray multi-dataset training...\n=== FULL RAY TRAINING MODE ===\nConnecting to Ray cluster at 10.60.151.21:6379\nRay cluster initialized successfully\nAvailable resources: 896 CPUs, 72 GPUs\n\nFunction sources:\n  _define_hyperparameters: from submission\n  create_network: from submission\n  compute_loss: HARDCODED (agents cannot override)\n  create_optimizer: from submission\n  complete: using default\n  BASE_SEED: 42 (from submission\n  BATCH_SIZE: 64 (from submission)\n============================================================\n============================================================\nStarting Ray Tune RNA Sequence Modeling (MULTI-DATASET RNA TRAINING)\nConfiguration: 7 datasets (APA, CRI-Off, Modif, CRI-On, PRS, MRL, ncRNA) for multi-task evaluation\nParallel trials: 7, Total trials: 7\nTimeout: 30.0 min per trial, 240.0 min total\n============================================================\n\nStarting Ray Tune multi-dataset training...\n\nStarting RNA training on CRI-On dataset\n\
