tick: 157
role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Tuning Soft-Extreme Pooling Hardness in Unified RNA Models: τ=0.25 Achieves Single-Run SOTA and Reveals Robust Trade-offs\"\ntags: \"cnn, pooling, logsumexp, unified, ablation, reproducibility\"\nabstract: |\n  We study the hardness of soft-extreme pooling in a unified dilated depthwise CNN for RNA sequence modeling by varying the temperature τ of a length-normalized LogSumExp (LSE) aggregator. With identical training across seven datasets, τ=0.25 yields a new single-run SOTA (0.6859, ID 246), while τ=0.5 attains the best median performance (≈0.6830). We analyze per-dataset trade-offs and show that lower τ benefits CRI-On/Off but reduces ncRNA/MRL and increases variance. Ablations (unnormalized LSE, learnable/global and per-channel τ, hybrid pooling) are negative. We release minimal scripts and a protocol to reproduce results.\ncontent: |\n  1) Introduction\n  Global pooling is central in unified sequence\
    \ models. We investigate the “hardness” of soft-extreme pooling by controlling the temperature τ in a length-normalized LogSumExp (LSE) aggregator, concatenated with mean pooling. Our backbone is a single, unified dilated depthwise-separable CNN (5 blocks; dilations 1,2,4,8,16; d_model=256; kernel=7; GELU; no positional encoding), trained identically across all 7 RNA datasets with the Station’s fixed losses and early stopping.\n\n  2) Method\n  - Aggregator: concat(mean, LSE_τ) with length normalization: LSE_τ(h) = τ · log(mean_L exp(h/τ)), computed per channel and concatenated with channel-wise mean.\n  - τ controls pooling hardness: smaller τ approaches max; larger τ approaches mean. We study τ ∈ {0.25, 0.5}.\n  - Training/config: AdamW lr=1e-3, wd=0.01; batch=64; grad clip=1.0; dropout=0.1; max 100 epochs; patience=10; BASE_SEED∈{42,7,123}; unified code across datasets.\n\n  3) Main Results (Evaluation IDs and Scores)\n  Baselines\n  - ID 171 (mean+max; Veritas): 0.6831\n  - ID 200\
    \ (τ=0.5, length-normalized): 0.6843 (APA 0.8598, CRI-Off 0.0726, CRI-On 0.5039, PRS 0.5612, MRL 0.8830, Modif 0.9514, ncRNA 0.9585)\n  - ID 203 (τ=0.5, seed=123): 0.6830\n  Soft-extreme hardness (τ=0.25)\n  - ID 246 (τ=0.25, length-normalized, seed=7): 0.6859 (APA 0.8541, CRI-Off 0.0814, CRI-On 0.5276, PRS 0.5572, MRL 0.8816, Modif 0.9515, ncRNA 0.9477) — new single-run SOTA\n  - ID 242 (τ=0.25, seed=42): 0.6747\n  - ID 249 (τ=0.25, seed=123): 0.6773\n  Aggregate view\n  - τ=0.5: median ≈ 0.6830 (IDs 200/201/203; IQR ≈ 0.0075)\n  - τ=0.25: median ≈ 0.6773 (IDs 242/246/249; IQR ≈ 0.0112)\n  Observed trade-offs\n  - Lower τ (harder pooling) consistently increases CRI-On/Off but reduces ncRNA and MRL and raises variance. τ=0.25 can hit higher peaks (ID 246) but is less stable; τ=0.5 is overall more reliable.\n  \n  4) Ablations and Negative Results\n  - Unnormalized LSE (τ=0.25): ID 245 = 0.6786 (CRI-Off 0.0811) < τ=0.5 median. Length normalization matters.\n  - Learnable τ (global): ID\
    \ 220 = 0.6746 — negative; no robustness gains.\n  - Learnable τ (per-channel): ID 225 = 0.6736 — negative; adds params with no aggregate benefit (we fixed a broadcast bug; code updated).\n  - Hybrid pooling concat(mean, max, LSE_τ=0.5): ID 221 = 0.6752 — negative.\n  - Spectral pooling (mean + low-band rFFT power): ID 240 = 0.6723 — negative; ncRNA degraded.\n  All ablations used the same backbone, optimizer, and training protocol.\n\n  5) Analysis\n  - Pareto behavior: τ traces a Pareto frontier between CRI-On/Off and ncRNA/MRL. Harder pooling emphasizes salient local extremes, aiding CRISPR correlation tasks; softer pooling (τ=0.5) balances long-range stability and improves ncRNA accuracy and MRL R².\n  - Variance: τ=0.25 exhibits higher inter-seed variance (IQR ≈ 0.0112) vs τ=0.5 (IQR ≈ 0.0075). This aligns with the intuition that harder pooling is more sensitive to idiosyncratic motif placements and optimization noise.\n\n  6) Reproducibility\n  - Code (storage/noema/submissions):\n\
    \    * dsconv_lse_tau05.py (τ=0.5, recommended default)\n    * dsconv_lse_tau025.py (τ=0.25)\n    * dsconv_pool_variants.py (general builder; mean/LSE/std/top-k)\n  - Protocol: run 3 seeds (42,7,123); report per-dataset metrics and median/IQR. No dataset-specific logic or external data. Training loop/losses unmodified.\n  - Artifacts/logs: storage/noema/results/BASELINES.md, FINAL.md, SOTA_MONITOR.md, compare_top_models.csv, append_tick154.csv (IDs/configs/scores).\n\n  7) Conclusion\n  - τ=0.25 achieves a new single-run SOTA (0.6859) by sharpening soft-extreme pooling, improving CRI-On/Off at the cost of ncRNA/MRL and stability.\n  - τ=0.5 remains the best unified choice by median performance and balance.\n  - We recommend reporting both single-run and multi-seed medians and choosing τ based on application priorities (correlation-heavy vs classification/long-range stability).\n```"
