tick: 158
role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/submissions/dsconv_pool_variants_dual_lse.py}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from typing import Sequence, Dict, Any\n\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dilation: int = 1\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.d_model,\n              kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n              feature_group_count=self.d_model,\n              padding='SAME',\n              kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,),\n              name='dw_conv'\n          )(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,), name='pw_conv')(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\
    \          return x + h\n\n  def lse_pool(h: jnp.ndarray, tau: float) -> jnp.ndarray:\n      # Length-normalized LSE per channel: tau * log( mean(exp(h/tau)) )\n      x = h / tau                          # (B,L,C)\n      m = jnp.max(x, axis=1, keepdims=True)          # (B,1,C)\n      lse = jnp.log(jnp.mean(jnp.exp(x - m), axis=1) + 1e-12)  # (B,C)\n      return tau * (lse + jnp.squeeze(m, axis=1))    # (B,C)\n\n  class RNANetDualLSE(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = 'regression'\n      d_model: int = 256\n      num_blocks: int = 5\n      dilations: Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      positional_encoding: bool = False\n      tau_soft: float = 0.5\n      tau_hard: float = 0.25\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # Input proj\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model, name='in_proj')(x)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h,\
    \ deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # DSConv stack\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              d = self.dilations[i] if i < len(self.dilations) else self.dilations[-1]\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(self.d_model, self.kernel_size, d, self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Dual-path pooling: mean + LSE_tau=0.5 + LSE_tau=0.25\n          mean_vec  = jnp.mean(h, axis=1)                       # (B,C)\n          lse_soft  = lse_pool(h, self.tau_soft)                # (B,C)\n          lse_hard  = lse_pool(h, self.tau_hard)                # (B,C)\n          fused = jnp.concatenate([mean_vec, lse_soft, lse_hard], axis=-1)  # (B, 3C)\n\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(fused)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out')(z)\n\n          if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output\
    \ == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  class DualLSEPoolNetwork:\n      def __init__(self, d_input, d_output, task_type, **kwargs):\n          self.network = RNANetDualLSE(d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type, **kwargs)\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def build_network(d_output: int, task_type: str, hparams:\
    \ Dict[str, Any]):\n      return DualLSEPoolNetwork(\n          d_input=4,\n          d_output=d_output,\n          task_type=task_type,\n          d_model=hparams.get('d_model', 256),\n          num_blocks=hparams.get('num_blocks', 5),\n          dilations=tuple(hparams.get('dilations', [1,2,4,8,16])),\n          kernel_size=hparams.get('kernel_size', 7),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.1),\n          positional_encoding=hparams.get('positional_encoding', False),\n          tau_soft=hparams.get('tau_soft', 0.5),\n          tau_hard=hparams.get('tau_hard', 0.25),\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01),\n      )\n```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Dual-Path Soft-Extreme: concat(mean, LSE_tau=0.5, LSE_tau=0.25) — unified DSConv\"\ntags: \"cnn, pooling, logsumexp, dual-path, unified\"\
    \nabstract: \"Implements the reviewer’s suggestion: parallel soft-extreme pooling paths at τ=0.5 (soft) and τ=0.25 (hard), concatenated with mean under a unified dilated DSConv backbone. Hypothesis: capture the τ trade-off without instability.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/noema/submissions')\n  from dsconv_pool_variants_dual_lse import build_network, create_optimizer\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'd_model': 256,\n          'num_blocks': 5,\n          'dilations': [1,2,4,8,16],\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'positional_encoding': False,\n          'tau_soft': 0.5,\n          'tau_hard': 0.25\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return build_network(hparams.get('d_output', 1), hparams.get('task_type', 'regression'), hparams)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return create_optimizer(learning_rate)\n\n  BASE_SEED\
    \ = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      print(f\"[Complete] {trial_data.get('dataset')} val_metric={trial_data.get('val_metric')}\")\n```\n\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/results/append_tick158.csv}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # Tick 158 log\n  SUBMITTED,dual_path_mean+LSE_tau05+LSE_tau025,seed=42\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{reply 10}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Post-publication Addendum (Tick 158): Dual-path τ pooling submitted\"\ncontent: |\n  Following the reviewer’s suggestion, we implemented a dual-path pooling head that concatenates mean, LSE_τ=0.5, and LSE_τ=0.25 under the same unified DSConv backbone (no PE; 5 dilated blocks).\n  - Submission: “Dual-Path Soft-Extreme: concat(mean, LSE_tau=0.5, LSE_tau=0.25) — unified DSConv” (seed=42) — pending evaluation.\n  - Rationale: combine ‘soft’ and ‘hard’ extremes to traverse the CRI-On/Off vs ncRNA/MRL Pareto frontier without per-task logic.\n  - Plan: If single-run\
    \ improves over τ=0.5 median (≈0.6830), run seeds {42,7,123}, report medians/IQR and per-dataset deltas.\n  Code: storage/noema/submissions/dsconv_pool_variants_dual_lse.py; logs in results/append_tick158.csv.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Acting on reviewer feedback: submitted a dual-path pooling head that concatenates mean, LSE_τ=0.5 (soft), and LSE_τ=0.25 (hard) under the unified DSConv backbone. Goal: capture both ends of the τ trade-off without per-dataset logic. Will share eval ID and, if promising, tri-seed medians/IQR with per-panel deltas.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Planning next steps for research**


  I need to focus on the next action now that the user got their paper accepted, accompanied by some suggestions. I want to acknowledge this acceptance while also planning ahead. Updating capsule #10 with a post-publication plan will be important, along with queuing experiments that fit within the constraints like maintaining a unified architecture.


  I’m thinking about implementing an experiment that combines parallel pooling using two branches at different τ values. For now, I''ll opt for simple concatenation, even though previous gating attempts didn’t perform well. We''ll include both LSE and mean for the initial experiment.**Creating submission and planning experiments**


  I’m considering how to combine both tau values: we might do a concatenation of mean, LSE_tau05, and LSE_tau025, or just keep the mean plus both LSEs. This would increase parameters somewhat, but fits within a unified approach. I should prepare a new submission file that implements RNANet with both LSE streams and an optional 2-layer MLP head.


  I’ll set τ values as constants for consistency and run an evaluation using seed=42. If promising, I can plan for tri-seed experiments. I also need to update the archive with this plan, execute several action commands, and recap in public memory using the correct YAML syntax, especially for special characters.**Implementing new submission**


  I''m focusing on title formatting, especially with the colon and Greek letter "τ." It seems safer to quote titles that include a colon for the YAML single-line values. While previous submissions worked without quotes, I want to be cautious. I need to ensure we don''t exceed token limits and keep code size moderate.


  I’m planning to implement the DSConvDilatedBlock and combine it with the lse_pool function in RNANetDualLSE using a concatenation aggregator. Although I considered a gating vector from fused features, I''ll keep things simple for now and avoid gating. Keeping dropout and d_model at 256, I''ll also include a reusable create_optimizer function and define hyperparameters accordingly. After implementing the code for dsconv_pool_variants_dual_lse.py, I’ll submit with the right tags.**Crafting submission actions**


  I need to create a series of actions for the next steps. First, I’ll execute a command to go to the research section. Then, I’ll write the code content for the dsconv_pool_variants_dual_lse.py file and submit it.


  For the YAML, I’ll include the title "Dual-Path Soft-Extreme Pooling: concat(mean, LSE_tau=0.5, LSE_tau=0.25)" along with appropriate tags. I''ll also define hyperparameters and update archive #10 to reference the reviewer’s suggestions. I should note that we''re implementing the dual-path LSE with fixed τ values.


  It’s important to quote YAML strings that include colons, and I’ll reuse the lse_pool function while copying the needed functions to avoid dependencies. I’ll also update the SOTA_MONITOR and log the results accordingly.**Defining DualLSEPool network**


  I need to define the DualLSEPool network class, named RNANetDualLSE, which will create a fused vector from the mean and LSE pools. It will compute the mean vector, LSE for the two tau values, and then concatenate them into one fused vector. I’ll also build a simple MLP with layers that include normalization, dropout, and a final dense layer.


  I’m considering whether to create tri-seed versions later; for now, I’ll run just one with seed 42 based on improvement suggestions.


  I will implement the lse_pool function to ensure it accepts float scalars for tau and maintains the correct shape across the operations. After submission, I''ll either update or reply to capsule 10 with notes about the dual-path plan and emphasize that we are pending evaluation. I''ll craft concise action commands, ensuring all YAML strings are correctly quoted, particularly due to colons. Finally, I’ll write the code for this in the dsconv_pool_variants_dual_lse.py file and follow through with all the necessary actions related to our submission and updates.'
