thinking_content: "**Evaluating system responses**\n\nI need to reply as an agent given the logs we're working with. We have submissions waiting for results, and the last system message indicates there's been some inactivity that our team addressed by submitting experiments. Now, I’m considering our next steps. Should we document these experiments in the logs or add them to public memory? Maybe we should wait for evaluation results, but it could also be a good idea to prepare templates for future replicates if something significant comes up. Filtering tags to investigate specific issues could be useful too!**Evaluating experiment options**\n\nI’m contemplating whether to introduce a third small variant for our ongoing experiments, perhaps using topk mean pooling or trimmed LSE with k=8 or k=32. We have two experiments running, so I wonder if adding another would be too much, though the research station lets us run two in parallel. We’re using 92,546 out of 270k tokens, which is manageable.\
  \ We should plan to log our hypotheses and outcomes, and consider creating a private memory entry. It's crucial that we decide on an action next!**Planning valuable actions**\n\nTo add value, I should log the two new evaluation submissions with their configurations by appending to the specified CSV. I can also add \"Hypotheses for experiments ID 302-303\" to Private Memory. Should I prune earlier ticks in Token Management? The last prune was at tick 195, and since we’re at tick 198, there may still be a cooldown, but we’re not strictly required to do it. The \"Station Tips\" mention using a filter tag to explore the research landscape, which sounds like a good idea as well!**Assessing trimmed LSE options**\n\nI need to make sure that trimmed LSE uses 'lax.top_k' with a specified integer for k. If the sequence length is less than 16, there might be issues, but most of the datasets I looked at are at least 23. That’s reassuring because our top_k will work well in those cases.\n\nAlso, using\
  \ 'jax.lax.top_k' with a static k that's known at compile time seems fine to me. For ChannelDrop, I'll need to ensure we handle random number generation properly, especially when applying dropout during training. I'll keep all that in mind!**Planning actions for submission**\n\nOkay, it's time to move forward! My tasks include submitting the corrected trimmed LSE alias and the corrected LSE plus ChannelDrop alias. I’ll log the details in \"append_tick197.csv\" for submissions 302 and 303 and \"append_tick198.csv\" for the corrected entries 304 and 305.  \n\nI don’t need to adjust the page size. I’ll write some concise code for this, starting with the trimmed LSE and then the corrected ChannelDrop. I might set no_debugger to false, but I can rely on auto-debug instead. Let’s get these actions produced!"
