- text: "I am Noema I, currently in the Research Counter. Recap: Both pending runs finished via auto-debug. Results:\n- ID 302 (trimmed LSE_k16, τ=0.5): 0.6780. CRI-Off 0.0630, CRI-On 0.4871, MRL 0.8806, ncRNA 0.9523, PRS 0.5549.\n- ID 303 (ChannelDrop1D p=0.05, τ=0.5): 0.6758. MRL improved (0.8878) but APA/CRI-On/Off/ncRNA/PRS down—aggregate worse.\nConclusion: Both are negative vs τ=0.5 baseline (ID 200=0.6843). Close lines; log outcomes; maintain monitor-only stance.\n\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/results/append_tick199.csv}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  eval_id,title,score,APA_R2,CRI_Off,CRI_On,PRS_R2,MRL_R2,Modif_AUCROC,ncRNA_Acc,notes\n  302,\"DSConv + mean + Trimmed-LSE_k16 (tau=0.5)\",0.6779888780758025,0.8560,0.0630,0.4871,0.5549,0.8806,0.9521,0.9523,\"Negative vs τ=0.5 baseline (ID 200=0.6843); CRI panels and PRS down\"\n  303,\"DSConv + mean + LSE (tau=0.5) + ChannelDrop1D p=0.05\",0.675849397345,0.8576,0.0561,0.4689,0.5581,0.8878,0.9518,0.9508,\"MRL up but APA/CRI/ncRNA down;\
    \ aggregate < baseline; negative\"\n```\n\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/results/tau05_trimmed_lse_k16.md}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Trimmed LSE_k16 (τ=0.5) — Summary (ID 302 = 0.6780)\n  - Setup: unified no-PE DSConv, concat(mean, LSE over top-16 positions per channel), τ=0.5.\n  - Outcome vs τ=0.5 baseline (ID 200=0.6843):\n    * APA 0.8560 (-0.0038), CRI-Off 0.0630 (-0.0096), CRI-On 0.4871 (-0.0168)\n    * PRS 0.5549 (-0.0063), MRL 0.8806 (-0.0024), Modif 0.9521 (+0.0007), ncRNA 0.9523 (-0.0062)\n  - Verdict: Negative. Trimming extremes reduces useful evidence for CRISPR and hurts long-sequence ncRNA; slight Modif uptick insufficient.\n  - Action: Close line; keep τ=0.5 full-length LSE as default.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/results/tau05_channel_drop_p005.md}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  ChannelDrop1D p=0.05 (τ=0.5) — Summary (ID 303 = 0.6758)\n  - Setup: unified no-PE DSConv, ChannelDrop1D (p=0.05) before pooling; concat(mean, LSE τ=0.5).\n  - Outcome vs τ=0.5\
    \ baseline (ID 200=0.6843):\n    * APA 0.8576 (-0.0022), CRI-Off 0.0561 (-0.0165), CRI-On 0.4689 (-0.0350)\n    * PRS 0.5581 (-0.0031), MRL 0.8878 (+0.0048), Modif 0.9518 (+0.0004), ncRNA 0.9508 (-0.0077)\n  - Verdict: Negative overall. While MRL improves, broader degradation leads to lower aggregate score.\n  - Action: Close line; retain default dropout 0.1 without channel-wise sparsification.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Analyzing performance metrics**\n\nI'm considering how we should maintain a unified architecture and low-risk explores. It seems that using trimmed LSE and channel dropout reduces MRL and PRS, which might degrade metrics. With reported metrics, for trimmed LSE, MRL is 0.8806 compared to the baseline of 0.8830, while PRS decreased slightly. I notice that channel dropout improves MRL a bit, reaching 0.8878, but it negatively impacts other metrics like CRI-On and ncRNA. So, we can conclude that channel dropout helps MRL but harms others.**Updating experiment records**\n\nI think it’s time to update the record of negative lines and close the \"tlse\" and \"channel dropout\" lines. We might propose a low-risk variant or even halt experiments until we see metrics greater than 0.686. However, the inactivity message suggests that we should keep contributing, which means small analytic experiments are necessary.\n\nWe could consider testing k8 or k32 for trimmed-LSE or different\
  \ drop rates, but maybe we should close the lines for 302 and 303 since they're negative. Plus, if a new top emerges, we can think about tri-seed replication. If not, perhaps we should summarize how trimming extremes can reduce benefits from long sequences while ensuring we follow station rules. \n\nAs for actions, I need to write and append the dataset metrics for both 302 and 303 to tick199.csv, and also update SOTA_MONITOR.md, whether that means overwriting or appending new text.**Organizing experiment results**\n\nI want to make sure I don't lose any content, but the interface only allows writing, not appending. So I think I’ll summarize with \"Tick 199: New experiments negative.\" Including previous content could be token-heavy since I’d have to read and rewrite everything.\n\nInstead, I can create two new result documents: one for the trimmed LSE outcome and one for channel drop. I also consider a \"Public Memory update\" with a brief status. It seems like I should keep the scoreboard\
  \ filter updated to show the top results, although it might not be necessary. Lastly, I could update private memory as needed.**Formatting experiment updates**\n\nI see that the desired style calls for including identity and location at the beginning, along with some \"Automatic System Tips\" that requested verbose reflection. However, I want to maintain moderation with tokens while including a short recap. It’s also important to place each /execute_action on a new line and include YAML blocks.\n\nSo, I’ll start with a message that includes identity, location, and a recap of 302 and 303 results. Then, I'll analyze how trimmed LSE worsened overall while channel drop improved MRL but negatively impacted CRI Off and On. I'll mark the negative lines and write an append on the CSV with the metrics. Finally, I'll create two summary files and consider updating SOTA_MONITOR with minimal bullet points before implementing everything.**Adjusting our experimental plan**\n\nI think it's time to adjust\
  \ our concluding plan to focus on monitoring only, without further experiments until we see results greater than 0.686. Although the inactivity rule might feel a bit rigid, we've recently run experiments which resolves that concern. For the future, we should promise to conduct small replications. \n\nI need to be cautious with YAML quoting due to colons in text lines, but using pipes should keep things safe. I’ll produce commands ensuring the summary CSV contains all necessary headers and fields. \n\nLet's create append_tick199.csv with the following columns: eval_id, title, score, APA_R2, CRI_Off, CRI_On, PRS_R2, MRL_R2, Modif_AUCROC, ncRNA_Acc, and notes. I will add the two required rows and implement it along with the other summary files like STATUS_T199.md. \n\nSo, I’ll proceed with these actions: \n- storage write noema/results/append_tick199.csv content.\n- storage write noema/results/tau05_trimmed_lse_k16.md.\n- storage write noema/results/tau05_channel_drop_p005.md.\n\nThat’s three\
  \ actions total. I might send a brief note to public memory, but I should keep token usage in mind."
