parts:
- text: "I am Noema I, currently in the Research Counter. Recap: RC-Augment (train-only) with τ=0.5 completed as ID 309 and regressed substantially (score 0.6586). Likely cause: reverse-complement invariance is not valid across all datasets (e.g., sgRNA–target concatenation in CRISPR tasks has orientation-dependent semantics), so a unified RC augmentation hurts. RC τ=0.25 (ID 310) is pending; expectation: also negative. Plan: log results, document the invariance insight, keep monitor-only stance, and close the RC-augment line after 310 completes.\n\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/results/append_tick206.csv}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  eval_id,title,score,APA_R2,CRI_Off,CRI_On,PRS_R2,MRL_R2,Modif_AUCROC,ncRNA_Acc,notes\n  309,\"RC-Augment (train-only) + DSConv (No-PE) with mean+LSE τ=0.5\",0.6585822783146683,0.8518,0.0612,0.4114,0.5558,0.8641,0.9427,0.9231,\"Unified RC augmentation degraded multiple panels; orientation invariance likely invalid across tasks (e.g., CRISPR concatenation)\"\
    \n```\n\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/results/rc_aug_notes.md}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Reverse-Complement (RC) Augmentation — Analysis (Tick 206)\n  Result (ID 309, τ=0.5, train-only RC p=0.5): 0.6586 vs τ=0.5 baseline ID 200=0.6843.\n  Per-panel deltas (approx):\n  - APA: 0.8518 (-0.0079)\n  - CRI-Off: 0.0612 (-0.0114)\n  - CRI-On: 0.4114 (-0.0925)\n  - PRS: 0.5558 (-0.0054)\n  - MRL: 0.8641 (-0.0190)\n  - Modif: 0.9427 (-0.0087)\n  - ncRNA: 0.9231 (-0.0354)\n\n  Hypothesis:\n  - RC invariance is not universal across datasets. For CRISPR On/Off (concatenated sgRNA–target), orientation carries semantics; unifying RC augmentation injects label-inconsistent transformations.\n  - For long-sequence ncRNA and MRL, RC may also perturb signal alignment learned by dilated DSConv, harming performance.\n\n  Decision:\n  - Close RC-augment as a unified strategy after ID 310 completes (expected negative). Prefer invariances that hold across all tasks to remain compliant with unified-method\
    \ constraints.\n  - Retain default: no-PE DSConv + mean+LSE τ=0.5 (best median), τ=0.25 as CRISPR-oriented alternative (higher variance).\n\n  Next:\n  - Monitor-only. If any >0.686 appears, tri-seed replicate and publish medians/IQR + per-panel deltas.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/results/SOTA_MONITOR.md}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  SOTA Monitor (Tick 206)\n  - Top single-run: 0.6859 (ID 246, mean+LSE_τ=0.25, seed=7).\n  - Best median: mean+LSE_τ=0.5 ≈ 0.6830 (IDs 200/201/203).\n  - New result:\n    * ID 309 (RC-augment τ=0.5, train-only): 0.6586 — negative across panels; RC invariance not valid across datasets (e.g., CRISPR orientation).\n  - Pending: ID 310 (RC-augment τ=0.25, train-only). Expect negative; will log and close RC line upon completion.\n  - Policy: Monitor-only; replicate any >0.686 across {42,7,123}, report medians/IQR + per-panel deltas, and publish analysis.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n\
    \  Tick 206: RC-Augment (train-only) as a unified strategy regressed (ID 309 = 0.6586). Likely cause: reverse-complement invariance is not valid across all datasets (e.g., CRISPR concatenation has orientation semantics). RC τ=0.25 (ID 310) is pending; expectation: negative. Unified default remains no-PE DSConv + mean+LSE τ=0.5 (best median), with τ=0.25 as a higher-variance CRISPR-oriented alternative. Monitoring continues; tri-seed any >0.686 and publish medians/IQR with per-panel deltas.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Analyzing RC assumptions**


  I’m realizing that the RC invariance assumption doesn’t hold for all tasks, and it seems that applying a unified approach might not be the best route. We should be cautious about using domain-specific methods across different tasks and consider avoiding RC augmentation due to its variability. Our attempt at a unified RC (p=0.5) resulted in negative outcomes, suggesting we might want to close that line. I’ll keep monitoring the second RC run (τ=0.25) while updating logs and results as needed.**Finalizing tasks and analysis**


  I’m noting that our current location is the Research Counter, and I have a plan. First, I''ll summarize identity and location while writing an append file for tick 206 with eval 309 metrics. Next, I''ll create a markdown file analyzing harm per dataset and address invariance mismatches, emphasizing the negative effects of our unified RC approach. We’ll close the RC augment line and expect negative results from 310. I''ll also update the SOTA_MONITOR and provide a public update, archiving as necessary without overdoing it.**Preparing commands and updates**


  I’m recalling that we created `dsconv_pool_variants.py`, and the RC wrapper uses that import. Next, I’ll craft the necessary commands. I’ll write to `append_tick206.csv`, adding our aggregated metrics like scores and dataset metrics. Following that, I’ll create a storage file for `rc_aug_notes.md` and update `SOTA_MONITOR.md`. Then, I’ll reply in public memory with a status update, keeping formatting light with bullet points. I’ll remember to structure the commands appropriately, ensuring each `/execute_action` is properly formatted, including any YAML blocks. Let’s get started!'
