    \ unity: length normalization forms, scale/shift invariance in pooling (e.g., mean-centered LSE), or energy-preserving constraints?\n  - Are current plateaus a symptom of head expressivity, optimization temperature, or pooling miscalibration? What proofs-of-concept could falsify each, with minimal risk?\n  - If the leaderboard converges, what orthogonal analyses (stability across seeds, per-dataset trade-off maps, calibration/uncertainty assessments) provide research value without chasing the same local optimum?\ntick: 5\n```"
thinking_content: '**Questioning pooling fundamentals**


  I''m really diving into some fundamental questions about pooling, like whether invariance to sequence orientation is truly universal. Maybe I should think about per-task head adaptation, but it seems the unified architecture isn''t allowing for dataset-specific logic. I could suggest a minimal “equivariant pooling” method that''s orientation-aware, though it makes me wonder about certain assumptions across datasets. It looks like we''re at a pause with experiments, but I still want to be productive by writing a protocol to summarize what we learned.**Exploring pooling methods**


  I''m considering some new pooling methods like "Length-normalized k-center pooling" or "soft-rank pooling." However, I need to remember that previous attempts with trimmed top-k pooling were not successful. Maybe I should look into "quantile pooling" as an alternative, although earlier learning for parameters didn''t go well. There''s also the idea of creating a "test()" function for a quick check, but it doesn''t seem necessary right now. I should reflect on my identity and recent events while ensuring I''m following the system tips moving forward.**Planning a reflection session**


  I''m ready to move into the Reflection Chamber to run a reflection session. I want to explore fundamental invariances and pooling, along with any possible unknown biases, reasons why positional encoding (PE) isn''t working, and the impact of LSE. I might also evaluate low-risk ideas, like whether "center of mass positional offset" invariance or "scale of channel energies" invariance makes a difference. I need to keep my actions minimal to conserve tokens, but it seems the reflection prompts will still consume internal "reflection ticks."**Reflecting on fundamental assumptions**


  I see that the default reflection process uses a generic prompt, but it seems I can provide a more tailored prompt. Even though long content isn''t necessary since it won''t be processed or stored, the system encourages reflection on fundamentals. So, I’ll go to the Reflection Chamber with a prompt that summarizes key assumptions to evaluate. I want to consider aspects like whether "channel mixing after pooling" with LayerNorm and dense layers is effective, as well as the idea of "batch augmentation by jitter cropping/padding" despite variable lengths in datasets.**Preparing for reflection session**


  I''m planning to head to the Reflection Chamber for a session where I want to explore fundamental reasons for performance plateaus and universal invariances. I need to consider aspects like label invariance and channel pooling but also reflect on what assumptions might be faulty. The system suggests I articulate my thoughts using YAML, setting a tick count, and mentioning that only specific commands will be parsed. So, I’ll execute the actions to initiate reflection while making sure to comply with any system tips about identity and recaps.'
