    \ 309, 310).\n  - Orientation/strand flips more broadly: APA/PRS windows are biologically oriented; flipping can misalign regulatory context (3′ vs 5′ motifs).\n  - Permutations/scrambling across long-range partitions (e.g., swapping halves) disrupt pairwise dependencies (again, CRISPR).\n- Ambiguous/conditional:\n  - Larger translations (tens of bases) can cross sub-regional boundaries (e.g., UTR vs coding-adjacent segments), so heavy jitter is risky.\n  - Reverse-only (no complement) is still orientation-breaking for CRISPR and may shift anchoring in APA/MRL.\n\nWhy does removing absolute positional encoding (PE) help DSConv?\n- Convs assume stationarity; absolute PE injects a global coordinate frame that competes with translation invariance and tempts the head to memorize locus-specific quirks instead of motifs. Our strongest gains without PE were precisely on long sequences (MRL, ncRNA), where translation invariance matters most and coordinate overfitting is most harmful.\n- Windows\
    \ are already anchored to biologically meaningful reference points (e.g., cleavage sites, guide alignment). Absolute PE double-counts or distorts that anchoring with a rigid frame, making pooled features “position-tagged” rather than content-driven.\n- Sinusoidal PE adds high-frequency components that interfere with depthwise motif extraction and pooled summarization, encouraging spurious position–feature interactions. Dilated stacks already provide relative/structural cues; absolute coordinates add little but hurt generalization across datasets and lengths.\n\nImplication: Favor priors that preserve within-window translation invariance and length normalization, and avoid priors that impose fixed global coordinates or orientation symmetry that is not universally valid."
thinking_content: "**Evaluating dataset invariances**\n\nI’m thinking about how to structure the reflection, focusing on invariances that hold across all seven datasets. There seems to be translation invariance along sequences for many datasets, though it might not apply universally. Some tasks, like APA, involve positional references to specific landmarks. Fixed length cropping might lead to translation invariance, but certain orientations matter, especially for CRISPR. Small local shifts should be manageable, as motifs can sometimes appear just a few bases away.**Analyzing augmentations and biases**\n\nI’m considering how augmentations like random cropping and padding might not work well since the sequence lengths are locked to dataset-specific windows. Changing alignment with cropping could mess things up.\n\nRemoving absolute positional encodings seems beneficial, as it breaks translation invariance and might lead to overfitting. I'm exploring minimal universally valid biases, like length-normalized\
  \ pooling and testing mean-centered LSE to address global offset issues. There are potential biases to consider, like per-channel evidence squashing and maintaining consistency with parameters across different tasks.**Exploring normalization and plateaus**\n\nI’m considering avoiding downsampling since tasks have varying lengths. It might be helpful to include per-channel normalization before pooling by subtracting the mean and dividing by standard deviation, and then applying LSE to unify across length differences. \n\nAs for plateaus, I’m thinking possible causes could be too small of an MLP head, the wrong optimization temperature, or pooling miscalibration. We need to test adjustments like a universal per-sample channel normalization while keeping the learning rate consistent. Also, maybe label smoothing could help with classification tasks!**Considering loss adjustments and normalization**\n\nI realize that while I can't change the binary or categorical cross-entropy losses, I could\
  \ adjust logits scaling. This, however, might affect loss calculations and could be seen as a change to the architecture.\n\nTo maintain consistency across tasks, I wonder about adding a global temperature parameter to logits, but this could hurt regression tasks. A proof-of-concept approach could include using mean-centered LSE by computing the mean per channel and subtracting it before applying LSE. Also, I might explore per-sample channel LayerNorm before pooling—should I apply it at each position or along the length dimension?**Addressing invariances and positional encodings**\n\nI’m thinking about how sequence-level LayerNorm across the length dimension can help achieve zero-mean, unit variance per channel. Calibrating attention temperature is another consideration, with a robust default of 0.5 to test learned per-channel tau.\n\nFor data augmentation, I’ve noted that dropout calibrations showed negative results for channel drops, while standard dropout at 0.1 is effective. Considering\
  \ the removal of absolute positional encodings, it seems they could impose harmful coordinates. Using relative positional clues may be a more beneficial approach, especially since previous results indicated gains without positional encodings."
