    \ preserves raw mean for global evidence.\n- Minimal scalar calibration of branches\n  - Learn a single scalar s (initialized 1.0, per-model not per-dataset): z_in = concat(m, s·e).\n  - Rationale: aligns dynamic ranges of mean and extreme branches without conditional gates; much simpler than our previously negative gated/conditional designs.\n\nWhy not normalize both branches?\n- If we length-normalize the mean branch (e.g., mean(LN_len(h))), it collapses toward 0 and removes a strong, proven signal. Branch-specific normalization preserves the successful raw-mean path.\n\nExpected per-panel effects\n- CRISPR (On/Off): Centering/LN should reduce spurious “baseline-activated” channels; could lift Spearman modestly if extremes were biased; but any benefit must not require orientation priors.\n- MRL/ncRNA (long sequences): Should be neutral to slightly positive; translation invariance preserved; extreme branch less swamped by channel scale.\n- Modif/APA/PRS: Likely neutral; small gains\
