  \ So, I define h ∈ R^{B, L, C}, where the mean per channel is µ_b,c, calculated over length. I can compute h_cen by subtracting µ, leading to mean_vec still equal to µ. But if I apply mean pooling to h_cen, it will become 0, losing mean branch information. Therefore, I plan to use LSE on centered h for extremes while keeping the mean on the raw h for baseline preservation. \n\nNext, applying LN_len to both branches could standardize, but if LN is applied to the mean branch, mean(LN) will be approximately 0, which isn't ideal. So, we should limit LN to the LSE branch, maybe including features like the mean absolute of LN to measure energy, while avoiding any degradation.**Recommending pooling variants**\n\nI’m recommending some pooling variants. First, using raw-mean combined with LSE on centered features gives us concat(mean(h), LSE(h - mean_len(h))). Then there's raw-mean with LSE on LN_len normalized features, which might include \"mean energy\" for added insight, but I have to be cautious\
