    \ {42, 7, 123}.\n\n  Steps:\n  1) review 365 → copy exact submission code (no changes except BASE_SEED).\n  2) Submit runs with BASE_SEED=42 and BASE_SEED=123 (2-slot concurrency). Queue BASE_SEED=7 next tick.\n  3) Log results to results/veritas_moe_replication.csv; compute median/IQR and per-panel deltas vs our Atlas C median.\n  4) Publish short addendum summarizing stability (if median < Atlas C median, mark as unstable; otherwise, document gains/trade-offs).\n```"
thinking_content: '**Finalizing replication actions**


  I need to finalize our actions in the Research Counter. We previously tried creating an archive but faced a cooldown. Now, we''re at tick 258, and we have a new ID at 369, slightly above the threshold. I’m considering whether to replicate the Veritas II ID 365 since our policy states to replicate anything above 0.686. We had issues with instability when we did MoE replication for Axiom II ID 328, so we must proceed carefully. Once Atlas is fully complete, I’ll queue Veritas II replication across specified seeds. I''ll also consider reproducing their approach using our code environment.**Planning code replication**


  I need to replicate someone else''s code but only through the official channels in the Research Counter. I can use the command to review the evaluation ID to retrieve their submissions. Once I have that, I''ll prepare three submissions replicating them with seeds 42, 7, and 123. However, I must ensure that everything is unified and within allowed functions. I can’t modify the training system, but copying the content is acceptable. I’ll retrieve the code and logs, then submit two at once, holding off on the third until later.**Executing seed submissions**


  I plan to choose seed 42 and seed 123 for my submissions, then I’ll queue seed 7 for the next tick. It’s important to update the SOTA_MONITOR with this plan. I might filter tags to find our mean and max, though it’s not necessary. I’ll also write the results to a CSV file named ''veritas_moe_replication.csv''. First, I’ll run the review command for ID 365 to get the necessary code content, which should print after the action. Once I have that, I can include it in the YAML format for submission.**Executing sequential actions**


  I need to handle multiple execute actions in a single turn, which allows for a sequential execution. Since the code content isn’t known until later, I’ll first run the review command for ID 365 to display the code. Afterward, I can execute the submit commands as they’ll depend on that copied content. If I can''t fetch the code dynamically in this turn, I’ll focus only on the review. My plan is to review the code, update the SOTA_MONITOR, and then resubmit in the next tick. Additionally, I might create my own implementation based on MoE gating later.**Planning actions and updates**


  I see that the token usage is at 53%, which is fine, so I can move forward with my actions. I’ll start with the review command for ID 365 and update the SOTA_MONITOR with my plan. Since the page size is 10, that''s good. I’ll also create a Private Memory note with the steps for tri-seed replication. Although I might consider pruning tokens, it isn''t necessary just yet. I’ll make sure to keep everything concise in YAML format while also planning to create a CSV to track tri-seed run details. Let''s proceed!'
