- text: "Reflection — Tick 1\n\nWhy simple position-agnostic pooling dominates\n- DSConv + no-PE encodes local motif detectors; without explicit cross-position modeling, global behavior is primarily “how many/where are motifs present,” which mean/max/LSE summarize well.\n- Attempts to model pairwise/long-range structure (bipartite, cross-half, CA-query) add inductive biases that are not uniformly valid across all datasets (e.g., CRISPR concatenation vs oriented windows) and tend to overfit or miscalibrate the sequence summary, hurting APA/MRL/ncRNA.\n- Pooling acts as a robust bottleneck that reduces variance; methods that perturb this bottleneck (gates, complex attention) often inject instability without consistent gains.\n\nAre we underusing channel interactions pre-pooling?\n- Likely yes. The backbone already mixes channels (1×1 convs), but a final, length-shared, channel-only recalibration right before pooling can reweight motifs per position so that pooling (esp. max/LSE) elevates the\
    \ right evidence and suppresses noise. Because pooling is nonlinear (max/LSE), pre-pool channel mixing is not equivalent to post-pool head mixing; it can change which positions dominate the summary.\n\nDesign principles for a minimal, unified channel mixer\n- Permutation-equivariant over length: identical operation at every position.\n- Channel-only mixing: no position/sequence attention; preserves unification and avoids invalid priors.\n- Near-identity initialization to keep training stable and attribute gains to structure, not capacity.\n- Capacity-matched ablation: shrink the post-pool head to offset added params.\n\nThree minimal variants (drop-in before pooling)\n1) PrePool-SE-Calib (length-averaged gate; identity-centered)\n   - h_ln = LayerNorm(h)  # across channels\n   - u = mean_len(h_ln)   # (B, C) squeeze\n   - g = sigmoid(W2(GELU(W1(u))))  # (B, C)\n   - h’ = h * (1 + α · (g − 0.5))  with α ∈ {0.25, 0.5}\n   - Notes: classic SE but (a) only once pre-pool, (b) identity-centered\
    \ via (g−0.5), (c) small α. Prior SE in-block was negative; this isolates the effect right at the bottleneck with gentler scaling.\n\n2) PrePool-Res-ChannelMixer (tiny 1×1 MLP; identity init)\n   - h_ln = LayerNorm(h)\n   - δ = W2(GELU(W1(h_ln)))  # (B, L, C) with W1,W2 ∈ R^{C×C}, W2 init ≈ 0\n   - h’ = h + δ\n   - Notes: strictly channel-only; W2 zero-init makes it start as identity; very small residual learning capacity.\n\n3) PrePool-Hadamard-Gate (data-dependent per-channel mask from global stats)\n   - u = mean_len(h)                   # (B, C)\n   - w = tanh(W(u))                    # (B, C), centered\n   - h’ = h * (1 + α · w)\n   - Notes: like (1) but single linear path; even fewer params; emphasizes/attenuates motifs with sign control.\n\nCapacity-matched setup\n- Baseline head (post-pool): LN → Dense(C) → GELU → Dropout → Dense(d_out), where pooled_dim = 2C for concat(mean, ·).\n- Equal-params variant: reduce head hidden from C→H (e.g., 256→192) when adding the PrePool mixer\
    \ so total params are ≈ matched (compute both counts).\n- Additive-small variant: keep head at C to test if any gain scales with capacity (separate ablation).\n\nEvaluation matrix (tri-seed medians/IQR)\n- Configs A (τ=0.5), B (τ=0.25), C (mean+max).\n- For each config:\n  - Baseline (no mixer).\n  - +PrePool-SE-Calib (α=0.25), head=C (additive-small).\n  - +PrePool-SE-Calib (α=0.25), head=192 (param-matched).\n  - +PrePool-Res-ChannelMixer, head=192 (param-matched).\n  - +PrePool-Hadamard-Gate (α=0.25), head=192 (param-matched).\n- Seeds {42, 7, 123}; report medians/IQR and per-dataset deltas; ensure identical training settings (optimizer, batch, clip, dropout, patience).\n\nSuccess signals and risks\n- Desired: +0.001 to +0.003 median without increasing IQR; consistent CRI-On/Off gains while preserving ncRNA/MRL; improvements persist across A/C; B may gain on CRISPR but must not tank ncRNA.\n- Risks: over-amplifying spurious channels (variance ↑, APA/MRL/ncRNA ↓); mixer collapses to\
    \ identity (no effect). Identity-centered init and small α should mitigate both.\n\nRationale recap\n- Nonlinear pooling breaks commutativity, so channel mixing before pooling can change which positions dominate the summary in beneficial ways (reweighting motif evidence per position), while remaining unified and position-agnostic."
