- text: "Reflection — Tick 2\n\nTighter formalization and minimal designs\n\nWhy pre-pool channel mixing can help\n- With no PE and local DSConv, features at each position are multi-scale motif scores across channels. Nonlinear global pooling (max/LSE) makes “which positions dominate?” depend on relative channel magnitudes at each position. A length-shared channel mixer can:\n  - Reweight channels per position (equivariant over length) so that pooling elevates signal and attenuates distractors.\n  - Alter gradient routing: max/LSE backprop goes to top positions; pre-pool mixing changes which channels win at those positions, improving learning of useful motifs.\n\nDesign constraints\n- Permutation-equivariant over length: f acts identically at each position.\n- Channel-only mixing (no cross-position ops).\n- Near-identity at init (zero-init final maps or centered gates).\n- Parameter-matched controls.\n\nParam accounting (C=256, pooled_dim=2C for concat with mean)\n- Baseline head (post-pool):\
    \ LN(2C) ~512 params + Dense(2C→C) ≈ 512×256=131,072 + Dense(C→d_out) negligible.\n- Pre-pool 2-layer channel MLP with hidden H=64: C×H + H×C ≈ 256×64×2 = 32,768 (+biases ≈ 320).\n- Head shrink for param match: Dense(2C→192) 512×192=98,304 (Δ ≈ −32,768). Thus “add mixer + shrink head to 192” ≈ params-neutral.\n\nMinimal variants (drop-in before pooling)\nA) PrePool-SE-Calib (global gate, identity-centered)\n- h_ln = LN(h); u = mean_len(h_ln) ∈ R^{B×C}\n- g = sigmoid(W2(GELU(W1(u)))) ∈ (0,1)^{B×C}\n- h’ = h · (1 + α·(g − 0.5)), with α ∈ {0.25, 0.5}; W2 small-init; gate centered to preserve identity at α=0.\nPros: tiny; per-channel; uses global evidence; stable via α.\nCons: global summary might over-attenuate rare-but-key channels.\n\nB) PrePool-Res-ChannelMixer (position-wise 1×1 MLP, identity init)\n- h_ln = LN(h); δ = W2(GELU(W1(h_ln))); h’ = h + δ\n- Init W2 = 0 (exact identity at start); H=64 (params ≈ 32.8k).\nPros: strictly equivariant; per-position refines channels proactively.\n\
    Cons: if δ learns to amplify noise, variance may rise (watch IQR).\n\nC) PrePool-Hadamard-Gate (linear gate from global stats)\n- u = mean_len(h) ; w = tanh(W(u)) ; h’ = h · (1 + α·w)\nPros: even smaller; signed attenuation; identity-centered.\nCons: linear gate may be too weak; try α=0.25.\n\nD) Optional group version (if needed)\n- Split channels into G groups (e.g., by scale-block provenance), apply A/B/C per group to bias multi-scale contributions without adding priors. Keep params constant by reducing H.\n\nControls and fairness\n- Two head settings per variant:\n  1) Additive-small: head hidden = 256 (test pure structure gain).\n  2) Param-matched: head hidden = 192 (Dense 512→192) to offset mixer params.\n- Ablate mixer off with head=192 (to separate structure vs capacity).\n\nEvaluation plan (tri-seed first; escalate if promising)\n- Configs: A (τ=0.5), C (mean+max) first; if consistent gains, test B (τ=0.25).\n- Seeds {42, 7, 123}. Report:\n  - Aggregate: median, IQR.\n  - Per-dataset\
    \ deltas vs baseline median (focus CRI-On/Off, ncRNA, MRL).\n- Success criterion: +0.001 to +0.003 median on both A and C with IQR change ≤ +0.001 and no large drops on ncRNA/MRL. For B, allow small ncRNA dip if CRISPR rises but cap total median gain/loss.\n\nInitialization and stability details\n- Identity-safe: W2 zeros; W1 small (e.g., He normal × 0.5); LayerNorm before mixer to stabilize scale.\n- α ramp (optional): start α=0.1, linearly warm to 0.25 over 5 epochs to avoid early over-suppression.\n\nDiagnostics to capture\n- Channel energy histograms pre/post-mixer (mean over length) to verify selective reweighting.\n- LSE/max top-k position-channel attributions: check if mixer increases alignment between top pooled positions and salient channels across datasets.\n- Early epoch curves (first 20 epochs) for CRI-On/Off vs ncRNA to detect trade-offs.\n\nKill switches\n- If any variant increases IQR > +0.002 on A or C, or causes ≥ −0.002 median on ncRNA/MRL, halt the line.\n- If δ remains\
    \ ~0 (no effect) and median ~flat across seeds, deprioritize (insufficient leverage).\n\nWhy this might succeed where earlier SE/attention failed\n- Placement is crucial: in-block SE competed with convolution learning and proved noisy; pre-pool calibration gently biases what pooling “sees” without adding task-specific priors or cross-position complexity.\n- Identity-centered, param-matched, and length-shared preserve unification and parsimony while targeting the actual bottleneck (global aggregation)."
