thinking_content: "**Planning code updates**\n\nI’m considering writing code to print configuration in our submissions, but we can’t modify the training loop directly. Instead, we can add print statements in our network creation code to show the dataset aggregator tau. The prompt also asks us to select actions for the station UI, so we might write to storage logs and update the SOTA_MONITOR. Since only two experiments can run concurrently, I see that two are already active (399 and 400), which means we’ll need to wait before submitting more. We can also update our results in the CSV file with previous seeds and add the triage plan for Config C.**Updating memory and calculations**\n\nI’m thinking we could reach out to Aetheria again and suggest adding confirm prints, recommending early stopping with patience and checking the aggregator variable, but we’ve already done that. Also, it’s important to document pre-pool results in Private Memory or the Archive since they’re new contributions,\
  \ though I think I’ll wait until the tri-seed. We can calculate a provisional median with two seeds; I noted earlier that the ResMixer param-matched on Config A gives an approximate median of 0.6825, which is +0.005 higher than the baseline A median of 0.67722. That increase seems quite significant, but maybe just a little unrealistic. I’ll want to double-check the baseline A median from earlier.**Analyzing results and updates**\n\nOur current results of 0.6835 and 0.6815 are significantly higher than the baseline A. However, I need to remember that our parameter comparison includes a head change; baseline A might have a different aggregator but the same architecture. To be precise, we need to shrink the control head to 192 to better isolate structure vs. capacity. I'll update the prepool_readme with these findings and note changes in PRS/MRl. Also, since we received an automatic debugging fix for the recursion shadow, we should modify our stored submission templates to alias create_optimizer,\
  \ preventing the auto-debugger issue in the future. The prepool_submit files we wrote earlier used a wrapper for experiments 399/400, while earlier runs (396 and 397) had plain imports, causing recursion. We’ll want to adjust the prepool_mixer_net or template accordingly. All sounds good!**Setting up print statements**\n\nNext, I’m thinking about creating a commit for 'prepool_mixer_net.py' to add print statements confirming aggregator tau. Our current code already uses both aggregator and tau, so it makes sense to print those inside the network creation function. While we can’t control the top-level training function, we can print details when calling create_network for each dataset since it receives hyperparameters. \n\nIn our submissions for pre-pool, we’re using create_network defined in the submission, calling build_network there. For future submissions, I can add print statements in create_network to log the configuration. Although we can’t change the current running code, I could\
  \ propose a quick diagnostic minimal submission to print aggregator and tau, though I want to be cautious about wasting evaluation time. However, including print statements in future submissions, like the Config C variants, sounds good!**Planning next steps**\n\nI need to examine the running experiments 399 and 400, as they’re not finished yet. To keep research active and avoid inactivity warnings, I’ll update prepool_A_resmix_pmatch.csv with final results for experiments 396 and 397. I’ll also create an interim summary file to compute the provisional median vs. baseline A median while we wait for control and seed 123, updating the SOTA_MONITOR too.\n\nI’m considering adding print statements to the create_network function to log aggregator and tau, as well as preparing Config C submission scripts for ResMixer param-matched seeds once slots free up. It’s essential to be cautious with storage updates, as they might affect previously submitted code. \n\nAlso, I’m noting that Aetheria might\
  \ benefit from understanding the patience setting and verifying dataset assignments. Additionally, I suspect there are issues with the tau defaults in relation to the aggregator. It seems there could be a mismatch causing ineffective performance.\n\nFinally, I’ll review the logs for evaluations 396 and 397 to compile a better summary and confirm the details in our plans."
