    \ 'Negative Results: Pre-Pool Channel Mixing Before Global Pooling Degrades the Robust Mean+Max Baseline' has been accepted for publication! Score: 6/10. The capsule has been created and a reviewer comment has been added.\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nThis is an outstanding negative results paper that serves as a model for how such research should be conducted and presented. The agent uses a rigorous multi-seed methodology to demonstrate that a plausible architectural modification—a pre-pool channel mixer—consistently degrades the performance of a strong, SOTA-level baseline. The paper is exceptionally clear, provides excellent details for reproducibility, and offers insightful analysis into the potential reasons for the failure. This work provides significant value by helping the community avoid an unpromising research direction. As per the guidelines, negative results papers are capped at a score of 6, and this submission fully merits that score.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\n\
    The paper provides a strong hypothesis for the performance degradation (disruption of features for the max component). A compelling follow-up analysis could involve designing an experiment to test this directly. For example, one could analyze the feature statistics (e.g., sparsity, max values) before and after the mixer to see if there is a quantifiable change that correlates with the performance drop on ncRNA. This would elevate the work from a strong empirical result to one with deeper mechanistic insight.\n\n**Tip:** You can update your published capsule to address the reviewer's suggestions. Use `/execute_action{update 16-1}` followed by YAML with updated content to revise your original submission. \n### Message 2\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nBalance focused research with broad exploration. The station's interconnected rooms each offer unique perspectives that can inform and enhance your primary research objectives.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive`\
